[08:25:10] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-8ffae23668dfe1b0","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\.coda\\skills\\researching-prd\\SKILL.md\" lines=\"994\" start=\"400\" end=\"799\" returned=\"400\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n   400|      ## Match by id reference in the directive text OR by semantic equivalence\n   401|      ## between the directive's instruction and oq.question.\n   402|      IF directive references oq.id OR\n   403|         directive.instruction semantically answers oq.question:\n   404|        decision = {\n   405|          id: derive_decision_id(oq.id),               # e.g. OQ-03 → DEC-03\n   406|          topic: oq.topic,\n   407|          decision: extract_answer(directive.instruction),\n   408|          source: \"reviewer-feedback@\" + directive.timestamp,\n   409|          confidence: \"confirmed\"\n   410|        }\n   411|        REFERENCE_MAP.decisions_confirmed.append(decision)\n   412|        PREVIOUS_SPEC.open_questions.remove(oq)\n   413|        LOG to SOURCE_LOG:\n   414|          \"OQ resolved by reviewer: \" + oq.id + \" → \" + decision.id\n   415|        break\n   416|\n   417|  ## After the pass, only genuinely unresolved questions remain in open_questions.\n   418|  ## The regenerated spec MUST NOT re-emit a question with the same topic as any\n   419|  ## entry in decisions_confirmed (see Zero Re-Ask Rule in Step 3).\n   420|```\n   421|**Execution:** automated\n   422|\n   423|**Modernization-specific rules (apply only when ENGAGEMENT_MODE == \"modernization\"):**\n   424|\n   425|The PRD must read as a product definition for a modern application that happens to encode the same business domain as the legacy system — not as a modernization project plan or a legacy inventory report. `legacy-insights` has already produced the knowledge extraction (business rules, workflows, domain terminology, data models, constraints); this skill's job is to synthesize that extracted knowledge into forward-looking requirements for the NEW system, not to re-describe the old one.\n   426|\n   427|1. **Zero Invention still wins.** Legacy docs are a source like any other — a business rule must appear in `documentation-final.md`, the project brief, or a transcript to make it into an FR with `status: complete`. Inferred rules remain `status: assumption` with ASM-XX IDs.\n   428|2. **Preserve vs. deprecate.** Every PRESERVE-XX behavior extracted from the legacy docs MUST be represented as an FR (or explicit NFR) in the PRD. Every DEPRECATE-XX must appear in the \"Out of scope\" / \"Not in scope\" section with the legacy-behavior ID so reviewers can audit what was intentionally dropped.\n   429|3. **Terminology fidelity.** When the legacy docs name a concept (e.g., \"loan term\", \"amortization schedule\"), reuse that exact term in FR titles and acceptance criteria. Renames belong in a glossary entry tagged `rename: {legacy_term} → {new_term}` so the traceability is explicit.\n   430|4. **Audit footprint.** The PRD-AUDIT must record `engagement_mode: {greenfield|brownfield|modernization}` plus, for non-greenfield modes, the trigger phrases and/or legacy source files consumed, so downstream capabilities (software-architecture, code-development) can verify the engagement context was actually used.\n   431|5. **No legacy-inventory FRs.** Never emit an FR whose purpose is to describe, catalog, or inventory the legacy system itself — e.g. \"document mainframe integrations\", \"inventory existing screens\", \"catalog legacy interfaces\", \"map current batch jobs\". That work is `legacy-insights`' job and is already done. An FR in this PRD describes behavior of the NEW system; if a candidate FR only names or describes legacy artifacts with no forward-looking system behavior, drop it — it is not a requirement.\n   432|6. **No migration-mechanics FRs/NFRs — but migration-driven product guarantees are in scope.** Never emit an FR/NFR whose subject is migration *execution*: data migration scripts, cutover plans, parallel-run/coexistence procedures. Those belong to a downstream execution/delivery plan, not the product definition. EXCEPTION: a migration-driven *product guarantee* is still a legitimate, testable requirement — e.g. \"users retain access to N years of historical transaction data\", \"the new system supports read access to legacy records during a defined coexistence window\". The line is mechanics (the \"how\" of the cutover) are out; guarantees the business/users need from the new system (the \"what\", even if migration-triggered) are in — sourced and tagged like any other FR/NFR.\n   433|7. **NFRs informed, not capped, by legacy.** Derive NFR candidates (performance, scalability, security, availability, observability, accessibility) for the new application from legacy constraints where they provide evidence of a floor (e.g., \"legacy processes N txns/sec\" → new-system NFR target ≥ N), but do not treat legacy limitations as the ceiling. A legacy constraint is a data point informing the target, not the target itself.\n   434|8. **Legacy docs exhaust before open_questions.** Before registering an item in `open_questions`, check whether `documentation-final.md` / `documentation-draft.md` or `analysis-report.md` already answers it — domain terminology, business rule, workflow logic, data relationship, validation constraint, edge case. If the legacy docs answer it, use that as the source and mark `status: complete` (or `assumption` with an ASM-XX ID if inferred rather than stated outright). Do not leave a requirement underspecified or flag it as an open question when the legacy documentation already provides the answer.\n   435|\n   436|### Step 2.5: Source Extraction Pass\n   437|\n   438|**Apply execution-protocol.md Section 10.2** (trigger thresholds, mechanism, REPAIR reuse, audit). This step provides the PRD-specific extraction schema — everything else is in Section 10.2.\n   439|\n   440|**Schema for each `_extractions/{name}.md` file** (omit empty sections):\n   441|\n   442|```\n   443|functional_requirements:\n   444|  - statement: {verbatim or near-verbatim} | line: {N} | priority_hint: {if present}\n   445|business_rules:\n   446|  - rule: {verbatim} | line: {N}\n   447|non_functional_hints:\n   448|  - hint: {what + measurable target if stated} | line: {N}\n   449|jtbd_signals:\n   450|  - who: {role} | what: {goal} | why: {motivation} | line: {N}\n   451|kpis:\n   452|  - metric: {name} | target: {value if stated, else [PENDING]} | line: {N}\n   453|risks:\n   454|  - risk: {statement} | likelihood_hint: {if present} | line: {N}\n   455|terminology:\n   456|  - term: {name} | definition: {from source} | line: {N}\n   457|personas:\n   458|  - role: {name} | attributes: {bullet list} | line: {N}\n   459|constraints:\n   460|  - constraint: {statement} | type: {regulatory|organizational|technical} | line: {N}\n   461|preserve_behaviors (modernization only):\n   462|  - behavior: {statement} | line: {N} | tag: PRESERVE-XX\n   463|deprecate_behaviors (modernization only):\n   464|  - behavior: {statement} | line: {N} | tag: DEPRECATE-XX\n   465|plan_report_personas (design-plan-report input only):\n   466|  - id: {PE-NN} | matched_to: {P-XX | none} | tier: {evidenced|provisional|assumed} | not_known: {verbatim from source} | line: {N}\n   467|plan_report_jobs (design-plan-report input only):\n   468|  - id: {JTBD-DNN} | matched_to: {JTBD-XX | none} | persona_refs: [{PE-NN, ...}] | line: {N}\n   469|```\n   470|\n   471|`plan_report_personas` and `plan_report_jobs` are populated ONLY when `design_plan_report_path` resolves and the Step 2 Persona/JTBD Reconciliation Pass has run. `matched_to` records the outcome of that pass — the PRD id it enriched, or `none` if it was flagged as a new-persona/new-job candidate (see the scope-authorization gate in Step 2 and Step 3). Kept as their own extraction categories, separate from `personas`/`jtbd_signals`, so the reconciliation decision is auditable independently of the brief-derived extraction.\n   472|\n   473|The PRD-AUDIT must list extraction files alongside raw sources so the provenance chain is complete: `raw_source -> extraction -> spec_item`.\n   474|\n   475|**Execution:** automated\n   476|\n   477|### Step 3: Generate Agent-Native Spec\n   478|\n   479|**Pattern Reuse Search (MANDATORY before proposing new components / integrations / capabilities / FRs that imply system behavior):**\n   480|\n   481|Before adding any candidate that names or implies a system component (e.g., a scheduler, a sync job, a worker, a runtime, a delivery surface), the agent MUST search the context-pack for an existing pattern that solves the same problem. Inventing a new pattern when one is documented in the context-pack is a protocol violation.\n   482|\n   483|```\n   484|CP_FILES = <context-pack files surfaced in the system prompt at\n   485|            session start (loaded by the harness)>\n   486|\n   487|FOR EACH proposed in candidate_components:\n   488|  search_terms = derive_search_terms(proposed)   # nouns + stems from name/purpose\n   489|  matched = false\n   490|  FOR EACH file in CP_FILES:\n   491|    IF exists(file):\n   492|      hits = grep_case_insensitive(file, search_terms)\n   493|      IF hits:\n   494|        proposed.pattern_source = file + \"#\" + hits[0].line\n   495|        proposed.classification = \"reuse\"\n   496|        record_in_audit:\n   497|          \"Pattern reuse: '\" + proposed.name + \"' grounded in \" + file\n   498|        matched = true; break\n   499|  IF NOT matched:\n   500|    proposed.classification = \"new\"\n   501|    record_in_audit:\n   502|      \"Pattern reuse search: no existing pattern for '\" + proposed.name +\n   503|      \"'. Searched \" + len(CP_FILES) + \" context-pack files.\"\n   504|\n   505|## Reuse-classified items MUST reference pattern_source verbatim downstream\n   506|## (do NOT rename or paraphrase the existing pattern in FR titles / glossary).\n   507|```\n   508|\n   509|**Apply execution-protocol.md Section 10** — Phase A (skeleton-first) then Phase B (one section per `Edit` call). Tool discipline (Section 10.5) is **mandatory**: skeleton via `Write`, sections via `Edit`, NEVER `Bash + sed/python3/awk` to mutate SPEC_FILE. NEVER fall back to bulk rewriting when one `Edit` fails — fix the `Edit` call instead.\n   510|\n   511|**Non-ASCII output fallback:** Apply execution-protocol.md §10.5.1. If DETECTED_LANGUAGE is not English, OR the project brief / transcripts / legacy docs / design-plan-report contain a character with codepoint > 127 (e.g. accented names, CJK terminology, non-Latin domain terms carried verbatim per Zero Invention), use full-file `Write` for every section write in this run (instead of `Edit`). Per-section discipline is unchanged — one section populated per call.\n   512|\n   513|**Before the Phase A `Write`, read `references/spec-template.md`.** The skeleton is a\n   514|transcription of that file's structural spine — see \"Skeleton carries the SHAPE\" below for\n   515|exactly what to copy. This is the one moment the template must be open; after it, the shape\n   516|lives on disk in SPEC_FILE and Phase B works from there.\n   517|\n   518|**Tracker updates — MANDATORY inline callouts, on the FIRST pass as well as on re-entry.**\n   519|§10.3 step 2 and §10.4 step 4 own these mechanics, but a protocol-only reference is not\n   520|reliably followed for bookkeeping writes that do not affect the deliverable — the same\n   521|reason §2 requires inline FIRST/LAST ACTION callouts. The Resume Discipline below already\n   522|names the update for the re-entry path; the first pass needs it just as much, because a\n   523|run that never updates the tracker leaves nothing for that resume path to read.\n   524|\n   525|- **After the Phase A skeleton `Write` is verified** (§10.3 step 2):\n   526|  `UPDATE _progress.json: skeleton_written = true`. All sections stay `pending`.\n   527|- **After EACH Phase B section `Edit` lands, in the same step** (§10.4 step 4):\n   528|  `UPDATE _progress.json: sections[{section_name}] = \"complete\"`.\n   529|\n   530|`started_at` / `completed_at` are real clock reads at the moment of writing. Never emit a\n   531|round placeholder such as `T00:00:00Z` and never back-fill either value at the end to\n   532|describe what the run would have done.\n   533|\n   534|**Single-writer discipline — MANDATORY.**\n   535|Phase B sections are written by YOU, into SPEC_FILE, via `Edit`. Do NOT create a\n   536|per-section sidecar folder, do NOT write section bodies to separate files for later\n   537|assembly, and do NOT let any delegated worker write `_progress.json`. The only\n   538|skill-defined working folder is `_extractions/` (§10.2, read-only inputs distilled from\n   539|sources); there is exactly ONE `_progress.json` for this skill, at the top of the output\n   540|folder. Observed 2026-08-11 (`api-launch__pli-20260811-ul9zpf__pd-001`): a run improvised\n   541|a `_sections/` folder containing per-section `.md` files and a second `_progress.json`\n   542|with its own envelope and fabricated one-minute timestamps — a shadow tracker no reader\n   543|of this skill's contract knows to look for. §12 delegation is read-only: workers return\n   544|conclusions and source pointers, never file writes.\n   545|\n   546|**Resume Discipline (CONTINUE/REPAIR re-entry) — MANDATORY:**\n   547|\n   548|If `_progress.json` exists on disk with `skeleton_written: true` and any `sections.{name}: complete`, this is a re-entry. Apply execution-protocol.md §10.6 verbatim:\n   549|\n   550|1. Read `_progress.json` first — nothing else.\n   551|2. Pick the first `pending` section in template order — call it `N`.\n   552|3. Read SPEC_FILE once.\n   553|4. For section `N` ONLY, load the `_extractions/*.md` files whose schema contains entries this section consumes (use the extraction-to-section map below). Then `Edit` SPEC_FILE for section `N`. Then update `_progress.json`. Then drop and advance.\n   554|\n   555|**Do NOT** open every `_extractions/*.md` file on entry. **Do NOT** compose a `think` block planning all remaining sections. **Do NOT** re-run Step 2 / Step 2.5 — extractions and SOURCE_LOG are already on disk. The prior run already paid for those reads; reading them again is the failure mode §10.4.1 and §10.6 exist to prevent.\n   556|\n   557|**Extraction → section map** (load only these files for each section iteration):\n   558|\n   559|| Section | Extraction schemas to load |\n   560||---------|-----------------------------|\n   561|| `project_info` | `{project_name}` parameter (use as-is) + `client_name` and `project_description` from project-brief extraction |\n   562|| `scope` | `functional_requirements`, `constraints` from project-brief extraction |\n   563|| `personas` | `personas`, `jtbd_signals`, `plan_report_personas` |\n   564|| `functional_requirements` | `functional_requirements`, `business_rules`, `preserve_behaviors` |\n   565|| `non_functional_requirements` | `non_functional_hints`, `constraints` (`type: technical|regulatory`) |\n   566|| `kpis` | `kpis` |\n   567|| `jtbd` | `jtbd_signals`, `plan_report_jobs` |\n   568|| `traceability` | SPEC_FILE cross-section (FR + JTBD) — no extraction read |\n   569|| `dependencies` | `non_functional_hints`, `constraints` (`type: organizational|technical`) |\n   570|| `risks` | `risks` |\n   571|| `assumptions` | SPEC_FILE cross-section (consolidate ASM-XX) — no extraction read |\n   572|| `constraints` | `constraints` |\n   573|| `mvp_phasing` | SPEC_FILE cross-section (FR priority) — no extraction read |\n   574|| `literal_registry` | `terminology`, `functional_requirements`, `business_rules`, `constraints` + SPEC_FILE cross-section |\n   575|| `glossary` | `terminology` + SPEC_FILE cross-section |\n   576|| `open_questions` | SPEC_FILE cross-section (consolidate pending/orphan/ASM-XX) — no extraction read |\n   577|\n   578|If an extraction file does not contain entries for the section's schema, do NOT open it for that section.\n   579|\n   580|**Skeleton carries the SHAPE, not just the headings — MANDATORY.**\n   581|\n   582|The Phase A skeleton is the template's structural spine, copied from\n   583|`references/spec-template.md` while that file is open. Read it once, immediately before the\n   584|skeleton `Write`, and transcribe:\n   585|\n   586|1. The **H1 title line**, byte-for-byte from the template — including its em dash (U+2014)\n   587|   delimiter before `PRD Spec`. Substitute only `{project_name}`.\n   588|2. **Every header field line**, all eight, in template order, none omitted:\n   589|   `version`, `session`, `mode`, `date`, `language`, `status`, `contract_format`,\n   590|   `content_hash`. Fill the values you already know; the rest carry their `{placeholder}`\n   591|   until Step 4/5 resolves them. A field you cannot value yet is still written now — a\n   592|   header line that is absent from the skeleton is a header line that ships missing.\n   593|3. **Every `##` section heading AND every `###` subsection heading** the template declares —\n   594|   including the three under `open_questions` (`pending_inputs`, `traceability_gaps`,\n   595|   `assumptions_to_validate`) and the `summary` block.\n   596|4. **Every table's header row and separator row**, verbatim, with the template's exact column\n   597|   names in the template's exact order — `traceability`, `literal_registry`, `glossary`, and\n   598|   all three `open_questions` tables. Delete only the template's illustrative example rows.\n   599|\n   600|The `status: pending - will be generated` stub then goes **underneath** the shape, never\n   601|instead of it. Phase B fills rows and bullets under structure that is already correct on\n   602|disk; it never authors structure from memory.\n   603|\n   604|**Why this is not optional.** §10.4 step 5 and §10.4.1 rule 3 require you to drop the\n   605|template between sections, and §10.4.1 rule 1's load allowlist does not include it.\n   606|`open_questions` is section 16 of 16 with no extraction read — so at the moment you write\n   607|its three tables you are entitled to hold nothing but SPEC_FILE. If the column names are not\n   608|already on disk, they are being recalled from ~15 `Edit` cycles ago. Observed 2026-09-05\n   609|(`product-discovery__pli-20260905-k4i93s__pd-001`): a run shipped a spec missing the `date:`\n   610|header line, with an ASCII hyphen in the H1, with `traceability_gaps` and\n   611|`assumptions_to_validate` rendered as bullet lists, and with wrong columns in the\n   612|`open_questions` tables — four blocking gate findings, all of them shape the skeleton could\n   613|have pinned for free.\n   614|\n   615|**Empty registries are still tables.** A registry with zero rows is the header row plus the\n   616|separator row and nothing else. Never a bare heading, never `- none`, never prose saying the\n   617|section is empty. `count: 0` belongs in `summary`, not in place of the table.\n   618|\n   619|**Stub text — must be ASCII:**\n   620|\n   621|> `status: pending - will be generated`\n   622|\n   623|ASCII hyphen (U+002D), not em dash. Non-ASCII characters in the stub create encoding traps\n   624|if anything later reads or matches the stub via shell tools. **This rule governs the stub\n   625|string only.** It does NOT apply to the H1 title, headings, or any other content copied from\n   626|the template — those keep the template's characters exactly, em dash included. Normalizing\n   627|the H1 delimiter to `-` because of this rule is a title-conformance defect, not compliance\n   628|with it.\n   629|\n   630|**Section list (template order):**\n   631|\n   632|```\n   633|project_info -> scope -> personas -> functional_requirements -> non_functional_requirements ->\n   634|kpis -> jtbd -> traceability -> dependencies -> risks -> assumptions ->\n   635|constraints -> mvp_phasing -> literal_registry -> glossary -> open_questions\n   636|```\n   637|\n   638|**`project_info` population rules (Phase B, first `Edit`):**\n   639|- `client_name`: extract from `project_brief` (name of the client or organization commissioning the project). Apply the `client_name` sanitization rules above. If not found or empty after sanitization, write `not stated in inputs` — do NOT open a `pending_inputs` row; client name is a descriptive field, not a decision-bearing one.\n   640|- `project_name`: use the sanitized `project_name` value from Parameter Sanitization — do NOT re-infer from the brief.\n   641|- `project_description`: synthesize a 1–2 sentence description of the project from the `project_brief`. Apply the `project_description` sanitization rules above. If the brief lacks sufficient context or the result is empty after sanitization, write `not stated in inputs`.\n   642|\n   643|**Cross-section dependency graph** (which sections read SPEC_FILE for prior state, per Section 10.4 step 1):\n   644|\n   645|| Section | Reads from SPEC_FILE |\n   646||---------|----------------------|\n   647|| `traceability` | `functional_requirements` + `jtbd` |\n   648|| `assumptions` | ALL prior populated sections (consolidates ASM-XX entries) |\n   649|| `mvp_phasing` | `functional_requirements` |\n   650|| `literal_registry` | ALL prior populated sections (extracts exact source-backed literals, enum members, ranges, IDs, route strings, field names, and positive/negative constraints) |\n   651|| `glossary` | ALL prior populated sections (consolidates terms; also reads `terminology` from extractions) |\n   652|| `open_questions` | ALL prior populated sections (consolidates PI-XX / TG-XX / ASM-XX) |\n   653|| all other sections | none — extraction inputs are sufficient |\n   654|\n   655|**Zero Re-Ask Rule (REPAIR mode):**\n   656|Before emitting an entry into `open_questions`, check `REFERENCE_MAP.decisions_confirmed[]`\n   657|(populated by the Step 2 Open-Question Resolution Pass). If any confirmed decision shares\n   658|the same topic, FR-ID, NFR-ID, or line anchor as the candidate question, DO NOT emit the\n   659|question — use the decision instead. Re-emitting a question the reviewer already answered\n   660|is a protocol violation.\n   661|\n   662|**Sweep Entry Guard (delegated-exploration items):** This `open_questions` sweep\n   663|consolidates whatever `status: pending` items already exist on the sections it reads —\n   664|it does NOT re-litigate whether each one deserved that status (see the Downstream Impact\n   665|Test above). That is normally fine, because the test already ran at the point of\n   666|assignment. The one case it is NOT fine: an item produced by a delegated-exploration call\n   667|(execution-protocol.md §12) that ran its own Status Protocol pass without this skill's\n   668|Domain Exclusion Rule or Downstream Impact Test in view. If, while consolidating, you find\n   669|a `pending` item whose `why_it_matters` cannot name the FR/NFR/AC it would change, treat\n   670|it as a Step 2 miss now: collapse it per the Question Consolidation Rule below, or convert\n   671|it to `assumption` with an ASM-XX — do not carry it into `open_questions` unexamined just\n   672|because it already carries `status: pending`.\n   673|\n   674|**Question Consolidation Rule (apply at the same point, before emitting a row):**\n   675|Do not emit fragmented, one-per-field questions for multiple missing attributes on\n   676|the SAME feature or epic. If a single FR/NFR is missing its target value, its\n   677|acceptance criteria, AND its boundary/scope, that is ONE open question summarizing\n   678|the missing scope for that specific capability — not three. This is the same\n   679|\"one row per decision\" principle the Status Protocol already applies across\n   680|different items sharing one field (§ above); here it applies across different\n   681|fields on the SAME item.\n   682|\n   683|**Question Framing Constraint (audience-aware, applies when writing the `question`\n   684|text and, for the sidecar, `answer_shape`):** When the intended `audience` is\n   685|`client` or `product_owner`, do not phrase a non-functional requirement or a\n   686|complex metric as `answer_shape: free_text`. Translate the technical gap into a\n   687|business trade-off and prefer `answer_shape: enum` with concrete, consequence-bearing\n   688|options (execution-protocol.md §11.6 — \"Options carry the trade-off, not just the\n   689|choice\"). Example: instead of \"What is the latency target?\", ask \"What is the\n   690|expected latency?\" with options such as \"Standard (< 2s, lower cost)\" and\n   691|\"High-Performance (< 500ms, requires aggressive scaling budget)\".\n   692|\n   693|Section structure comes from `references/spec-template.md`, transcribed into the skeleton at\n   694|Phase A. Fill content under the shape that is already on disk — do not re-derive headings,\n   695|header fields, or table columns here.\n   696|\n   697|Generate the complete spec following the template. Apply these rules to ALL sections:\n   698|\n   699|**Structured Contract Rule:**\n   700|Native PRDs emitted by this skill declare `contract_format: structured`, use\n   701|globally stable AC IDs (`FR-XX-AC-YY`), include a typed `literal_registry`, and\n   702|emit enriched `open_questions` with `affected_ids`, `blocking`, and\n   703|`fallback_behavior`. Source references must use stable section/ID anchors instead\n   704|of line numbers.\n   705|\n   706|**Status Protocol:**\n   707|- Every item has a `status` field: `complete`, `pending`, or `assumption`\n   708|- `complete`: every evidence-bearing field populated from source evidence. A DESCRIPTIVE\n   709|  field the inputs never mention (`usage_frequency`, `measurement_method`, `owner`,\n   710|  `likelihood`, `impact`, and the like) is written `not stated in inputs` and does NOT\n   711|  change the status or open a question — a template field is not a question\n   712|  (execution-protocol.md §11.6).\n   713|- `pending`: a DECISION-BEARING field is missing — acceptance criteria of an MVP FR, an\n   714|  NFR target the product commits to, a threshold / amount / formula / provider the\n   715|  downstream artifacts would be built differently on → item also registered in\n   716|  `open_questions`, ONE PI row per decision (never one per field, never one per item\n   717|  sharing the same gap).\n   718|- `assumption`: inferred from industry standard → ASM-XX ID assigned\n   719|\n   720|**Domain Exclusion Rule (apply before assigning `status: pending`):** The\n   721|`open_questions` registry is strictly for business logic, product constraints,\n   722|and user-facing behavior. If a gap relates to CI/CD tooling, backend toolchains\n   723|(e.g., SonarQube, Sentry), infrastructure runners (e.g., macOS runners), or\n   724|internal architectural refactoring (e.g., BFF topology): do NOT set\n   725|`status: pending` and do NOT emit an open question. Assign an `ASM-XX` id with\n   726|a standard technical default and set `status: assumption` instead — these\n   727|belong in downstream architecture artifacts (e.g., a DTR), not the PRD.\n   728|\n   729|**Downstream Impact Test (apply at THIS point, before assigning `status:\n   730|pending` — not only at the Step 3 consolidation sweep):** A gap earns\n   731|`status: pending` only when you can already name which FR/NFR/AC would be\n   732|worded or valued differently depending on the answer. If you cannot name that\n   733|artifact right now, the field is either `not stated in inputs` (no decision\n   734|riding on it) or `status: assumption` with an ASM-XX (a safe default exists) —\n   735|never `status: pending` \"to be safe\". Assigning `pending` speculatively, on\n   736|the expectation that the later consolidation pass will filter it out, is the\n   737|exact failure mode KFM-001 records: the sweep at Step 3 consolidates whatever\n   738|`status: pending` items already exist, it does not re-litigate whether each\n   739|one deserved the status. Get it right here, at the point of assignment.\n   740|\n   741|**Source Tagging:**\n   742|- Every item has a `source` field pointing to REFERENCE_MAP entry\n   743|- No source → status MUST be `pending` or `assumption`\n   744|\n   745|**Consolidation Rules (FRs):**\n   746|- Group trivially similar FRs (same JTBD, same source, differ by variant) as sub-items\n   747|- Otherwise unique IDs: FR-01, FR-02, etc.\n   748|\n   749|**Atomicity Rule (MANDATORY):**\n   750|Each FR MUST describe exactly one testable behavior. Apply this test: if a single FR contains more than one \"shall\" statement, or its acceptance criteria cover more than one independently verifiable outcome, split it into separate FRs. Bundling configuration sub-options (e.g., working hours AND breaks AND holidays) into one FR is a violation — each independently testable behavior gets its own FR ID.\n   751|\n   752|**Implementation-Detail Prohibition (MANDATORY):**\n   753|FRs and NFRs MUST describe WHAT the system does, not HOW. Prohibited in FR/NFR body and acceptance criteria: specific technology names, storage engine identifiers, environment variable names, network protocol specifics, file permission values, container runtimes, or infrastructure product names — unless the brief explicitly mandates them as a named constraint. When such detail appears in a source, extract the behavioral intent and move the mechanism to a `design_note` sub-field tagged `status: assumption`.\n   754|\n   755|**FR Priority (MANDATORY):**\n   756|- Every FR MUST carry an explicit priority label using MoSCoW (Must Have / Should Have / Could Have / Won't Have) or P0–P3 tier. Derive from source evidence where present; infer from JTBD criticality and mark `status: assumption` with an ASM-XX ID when not stated.\n   757|\n   758|**Inter-Feature Dependencies (MANDATORY):**\n   759|- After all FRs are written, add a `depends_on: [FR-XX, ...]` field to any FR whose acceptance criteria reference another FR as a precondition. If no inter-feature dependencies exist, emit an explicit `inter_feature_dependencies: none identified` note in the `dependencies` section rather than leaving it absent.\n   760|\n   761|**NFR Targets:**\n   762|- MUST be numeric/measurable. \"Fast\" or \"Secure\" → status: pending\n   763|- Inferred targets → append ASM-XX ID\n   764|- **Mandatory NFR sub-categories:** The spec MUST include at least one NFR entry (or an explicit `status: pending` placeholder registered in `open_questions`) for EACH of the following sub-categories, regardless of whether the brief mentions them: (a) performance/response-time, (b) scalability (user capacity, data volume, concurrency), (c) availability/uptime SLA, (d) usability/accessibility (e.g., WCAG level or equivalent standard), (e) security/compliance. If a sub-category has no source evidence, set `status: pending` and register an open question — do NOT silently omit the sub-category.\n   765|\n   766|**Acceptance Criteria:**\n   767|- Extract ONLY from source evidence\n   768|- Format: `given: / when: / then:` (structured, no prose)\n   769|- Missing on an MVP-scoped FR → status: pending, registered in open_questions (decision-\n   770|  bearing). Missing on a could_have / later-phase FR → `acceptance_criteria: not stated in\n   771|  inputs`, no PI row; the phasing already says it is not being built yet.\n   772|\n   773|**Traceability Table:**\n   774|- Bidirectional: FR→JTBD (forward) and JTBD→FR (reverse)\n   775|- status: `mapped` or `orphan` or `unsatisfied`\n   776|- Every non-mapped entry → registered in open_questions\n   777|\n   778|**Bias Detection (apply during generation):**\n   779|- Technology names not in sources → reframe as capability need\n   780|- Architecture decisions without source evidence → flag as assumption\n   781|- Implementation details in FRs → extract to notes, keep FR behavioral\n   782|\n   783|**Constraints sub-categories (MANDATORY):**\n   784|The `constraints` section MUST include an explicit entry for EACH of: (a) budget/cost, (b) timeline/schedule, (c) resource/staffing, (d) regulatory/compliance. Rules:\n   785|- If source evidence exists: populate and set `status: complete`.\n   786|- If no source evidence exists: emit the entry with `status: pending` and register an open question in `open_questions` whose `fallback_behavior` names an interim baseline per the Deferral Protocol below (e.g. `\"Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: no budget ceiling assumed, scope treated as uncapped until stated\"`) — never a bare placeholder value with no baseline. Do NOT silently omit the sub-category.\n   787|- The Agentic-First Output Contract (no calendar dates) applies to timeline entries: express as phase gates or relative sequencing, not calendar dates. If no timeline constraint is stated in sources, the entry is still required — set `status: pending`.\n   788|- A constraint entry in `risks` or `mvp_phasing` does NOT satisfy this requirement; the entry must appear in the `constraints` section itself.\n   789|\n   790|**Research Methodology and Persona Prioritization (MANDATORY):**\n   791|- The `scope` or `personas` section MUST include a brief `research_methodology` note describing how inputs were gathered and synthesized (e.g., \"derived from project brief analysis; no primary user interviews provided\"). If no user research beyond the brief exists, state that explicitly rather than omitting the field.\n   792|- The `personas` section MUST designate one persona as primary and provide a one-line rationale for the prioritization. If the brief provides no basis for prioritization, set `status: assumption` with an ASM-XX ID and state the assumed rationale.\n   793|- Each persona entry MUST include a `demographics` sub-field covering at minimum: (a) role/seniority, (b) technical proficiency level, (c) organizational context (team size or business size where relevant). If the brief provides no demographic evidence, populate from JTBD signals and mark `status: assumption` with an ASM-XX ID — do NOT omit the sub-field.\n   794|\n   795|**Design-Plan-Report Fold-In Rule (MANDATORY, no-duplication) — applies when `plan_report_personas` / `plan_report_jobs` extractions are non-empty, in ANY engagement mode (greenfield, brownfield, or modernization):**\n   796|- When rendering `### P-XX: {role_name}` or `### JTBD-XX: {...}`, first check whether a `plan_report_personas` / `plan_report_jobs` entry has `matched_to` pointing at this same id (set during the Step 2 Reconciliation Pass). If so, FOLD its fields in — do NOT render a second `### P-XX:` / `### JTBD-XX:` heading for the same segment or job:\n   797|  - Persona: append the design-plan-report id to `source` (e.g. `source: brief §2; PE-02`), let its `tier` inform `status` (evidenced → complete; provisional/assumed → assumption[ASM-XX]), fold `context`/`friction`/`category_expectations` into `description`/`pain_points`, and carry `not_known` into the persona's ASM-XX rationale.\n   798|  - JTBD: append the design-plan-report id to `source`, and merge its `persona_refs`/`grounded_in`/`answers` into the existing entry's own citations.\n   799|- Only a `plan_report_personas` / `plan_report_jobs` entry whose `matched_to` is `none` may become a NEW `### P-XX:` / `### JTBD-XX:` heading — and only if it is covered by an `OBJ-NN` or an accepted item already in this run's `scope` section (the scope-authorization gate from Step 2). An uncovered `matched_to: none` entry is NOT rendered as a persona/JTBD; register it in `open_questions` instead, citing the design-plan-report id.\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":7}}
