[08:25:12] {"type":"tool_start","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-aae17225e9a67deb","toolName":"read","args":{"path":"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\.coda\\skills\\researching-prd\\SKILL.md","offset":799,"limit":200}}}
[08:25:12] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-aae17225e9a67deb","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\.coda\\skills\\researching-prd\\SKILL.md\" lines=\"994\" start=\"799\" end=\"994\" returned=\"196\" has_more=\"false\" truncated=\"false\">\n   799|- Only a `plan_report_personas` / `plan_report_jobs` entry whose `matched_to` is `none` may become a NEW `### P-XX:` / `### JTBD-XX:` heading — and only if it is covered by an `OBJ-NN` or an accepted item already in this run's `scope` section (the scope-authorization gate from Step 2). An uncovered `matched_to: none` entry is NOT rendered as a persona/JTBD; register it in `open_questions` instead, citing the design-plan-report id.\n   800|- This rule exists specifically to prevent duplication: `design-plan-report.md` and this skill's own brief/transcript extraction may independently describe the same segment or the same job, and the reconciliation pass + this fold-in rule are what keep that as ONE PRD entry, not two.\n   801|\n   802|**Execution:** automated\n   803|\n   804|### Step 4: Quality Validation Gate\n   805|\n   806|**Reads SPEC_FILE from disk, section by section.** Phase B flushed per-section content\n   807|from working memory by design — do not assume in-memory state. Each check below is a\n   808|targeted disk read of the section(s) it covers. If a correction is needed, edit SPEC_FILE\n   809|in place (one section per Edit call, same discipline as Phase B).\n   810|\n   811|**Command:**\n   812|```\n   813|1. Source Fidelity: Every FR/NFR has a source or is marked pending/assumption\n   814|2. Zero Invention: No item with status:complete lacks source evidence\n   815|3. Traceability: Every FR maps to a JTBD or is flagged orphan in open_questions\n   816|4. NFR Targets: All numeric or pending. No qualitative targets allowed\n   817|5. Acceptance Criteria: Every FR has ≥1 AC or is registered in open_questions\n   818|6. Bias Check: No technology names, vendor names, or implementation details\n   819|   not explicitly in sources\n   820|7. open_questions: Every pending/orphan/assumption item appears exactly once\n   821|8. ID Consistency: No duplicate IDs, no gaps in numbering\n   822|9. Structured Contract: `contract_format: structured` present, AC IDs use\n   823|   `FR-XX-AC-YY`, open_questions use enriched columns, and every source-backed\n   824|   literal in requirements/ACs/constraints appears in `literal_registry`.\n   825|10. Hash Contract: `content_hash` is sha256 over canonical PRD-SPEC content with\n   826|    the `content_hash:` line omitted — COMPUTED by running the recipe in\n   827|    `execution-protocol.md` §10.5.4, never written from your own estimate. A\n   828|    verifier recomputes it, and a hand-written 64-hex string matches only about\n   829|    one time in four. Do not use mtime.\n   830|\n   831|IF corrections needed → apply in place, log to CHANGE_LOG\n   832|```\n   833|**Execution:** automated\n   834|\n   835|### Step 5: Write Outputs\n   836|\n   837|**Command:**\n   838|```\n   839|1. Write SPEC_FILE (PRD-SPEC-{SESSION_ID}.md) inside SPEC_FOLDER.\n   840|   - Set the SPEC front-matter `version:` to NEW_VERSION. On REPAIR this MUST be the\n   841|     incremented patch (Step 1: PREVIOUS_VERSION → NEW_VERSION) — editing the content\n   842|     in place without bumping `version` is a §7 violation. See execution-protocol §7.2 step 8.\n   843|2. Write AUDIT_FILE (PRD-AUDIT-{SESSION_ID}.md) inside SPEC_FOLDER:\n   844|   - Session metadata (version=NEW_VERSION, mode, session_id, timestamp, language)\n   845|   - Sources referenced (numbered list from SOURCE_LOG)\n   846|   - Decisions made (from CHANGE_LOG)\n   847|   - Summary counts: FRs, NFRs, JTBDs, KPIs, open_questions\n   848|   - Structured contract validation result and final `content_hash`\n   849|   - REPAIR-only: set `mode: REPAIR` and APPEND (do not overwrite) a `## Repair History`\n   850|     entry — version, timestamp, directives_applied, sections_changed, sections_preserved,\n   851|     repair_delta (per execution-protocol §7.2 step 8).\n   852|3. REPAIR self-check (execution-protocol §7.2 step 9): before final_response, confirm the\n   853|   SPEC `version` is strictly greater than PREVIOUS_VERSION AND the AUDIT has the new\n   854|   `## Repair History` entry. If not, fix the bookkeeping now — do not finish.\n   855|4. Verify EXACTLY 2 files exist inside SPEC_FOLDER and are non-empty\n   856|5. Verify files are INSIDE SPEC_FOLDER (the session-scoped subfolder),\n   857|   NOT at the parent output_folder level. If files were written to the\n   858|   wrong location, move them into SPEC_FOLDER before completing.\n   859|\n   860|VIOLATION: Writing PRD-SPEC or PRD-AUDIT outside of SPEC_FOLDER\n   861|(e.g., directly in the outputs root) breaks the folder naming contract.\n   862|Downstream capabilities expect files inside {prd_output_path}/.\n   863|\n   864|Ready for downstream consumption (Epics, Architecture, Implementation).\n   865|```\n   866|**Execution:** automated\n   867|\n   868|### Step 6: Emit Harness Outputs Sidecar\n   869|\n   870|**Apply execution-protocol.md Section 11.** Mandatory when the prompt contains a `## Run metadata` block (Stepwise invocation); skip otherwise (standalone invocation).\n   871|\n   872|**Output parameters this skill produces** (one key per row of the prompt's `## Output parameters` table):\n   873|\n   874|- `prd_output_path`: the resolved `prd_output_path` parameter — the SPEC_FOLDER you wrote into in Steps 1–5.\n   875|\n   876|**`_open_questions` — ALWAYS emitted (execution-protocol §11.6).** Reserved key, not a\n   877|declared output parameter, so it is absent from the list above and from the `## Output\n   878|parameters` table — a sidecar carrying only `prd_output_path` is a violation, not a minimal\n   879|sidecar. This skill's `open_questions` section holds `pending_inputs`, `traceability_gaps`\n   880|and `assumptions_to_validate` as tables. §11.6 owns the field mapping, including\n   881|`fallback_behavior` → `assumption_if_unanswered` and the `BLOCKED` rule. Mirror every row of\n   882|every one of those tables; if the artifact you actually wrote carries a registry not listed\n   883|here, mirror it too — the headings on disk are the authority. If `summary.pending_inputs` is\n   884|34, the array has 34 PI entries plus the TG and ASM rows.\n   885|\n   886|**Deferral and Fallback Protocol (`fallback_behavior` in the artifact tables,\n   887|`assumption_if_unanswered` in the sidecar):** Registering and deferring a question\n   888|to a future phase (\"Ideation\", \"Discovery\", \"Foundation\") is ALLOWED and ENCOURAGED\n   889|when the information is legitimately missing — but the deferral alone is not a valid\n   890|value for this field (execution-protocol.md §11.6, \"Deferring to a later phase still\n   891|names an interim baseline\"). Every deferred row MUST follow the pattern\n   892|`Deferred to <phase>; interim baseline: <actionable default>`, where the interim\n   893|baseline is a concrete state a downstream agent can build against immediately —\n   894|e.g. `\"Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: scope excluded from Phase 0\"` or\n   895|`\"Deferred to Foundation; interim baseline: proceed with the industry-standard\n   896|99.9% SLA target\"`. Never leave this field as a bare `TBD`, `To be defined`, or\n   897|`Blocked` with no baseline attached — that is the same refusal-token violation\n   898|§11.6 already prohibits, only spelled as a phase name instead of the literal word.\n   899|\n   900|**Example sidecar contents** (values illustrative, list truncated):\n   901|\n   902|```json\n   903|{\n   904|  \"prd_output_path\": \"/abs/path/to/artifacts/outputs/<capability>/prd\",\n   905|  \"_open_questions\": [\n   906|    {\n   907|      \"id\": \"PI-10\",\n   908|      \"question\": \"Which batch window is authoritative: 19:00-06:00 or 23:00-23:59:59?\",\n   909|      \"kind\": \"question\",\n   910|      \"subject\": \"functional_requirements\",\n   911|      \"why_it_matters\": \"HIGH — FR-06 cannot be enabled in production until the window is fixed\",\n   912|      \"blocking\": false,\n   913|      \"assumption_if_unanswered\": \"tests are parameterized for both values and production enablement is withheld\",\n   914|      \"audience\": \"client\",\n   915|      \"traces_to\": [\"FR-06\"],\n   916|      \"artifact_ref\": \"artifacts/outputs/<capability>/prd/PRD-SPEC-{SESSION_ID}.md#open-questions\"\n   917|    }\n   918|  ]\n   919|}\n   920|```\n   921|\n   922|**Execution:** automated\n   923|\n   924|## Agentic-First Output Contract — No Calendar Time\n   925|\n   926|PRD artifacts are agentic-first: they are consumed by downstream AI agents, not human project managers. Do NOT include calendar-time references in any generated content.\n   927|\n   928|**PROHIBITED in all PRD output:**\n   929|- Week numbers or ranges (e.g., \"Weeks 1-4\", \"Week 3\")\n   930|- Month names or numbers (e.g., \"Month 2\", \"Q1\")\n   931|- Calendar dates (e.g., \"by March\", \"2026-06-01\")\n   932|- Sprint numbers tied to calendar (e.g., \"Sprint 3\" = \"3 weeks\")\n   933|- Duration estimates in calendar units (e.g., \"4-6 weeks\", \"2 months\")\n   934|\n   935|**USE INSTEAD:**\n   936|- Phase identifiers: Phase 0, Phase 1, Phase 2\n   937|- Priority tiers: Must Have, Should Have, Could Have, Won't Have\n   938|- Epic dependency ordering: EPIC-01 before EPIC-02\n   939|- Relative sequencing: \"before production\", \"after Phase 0 gate passes\"\n   940|\n   941|VIOLATION: Any week/month/date reference in PRD output is a format violation. Apply auto-correct: replace with phase name or priority tier.\n   942|\n   943|## Common Rationalizations\n   944|\n   945|| Excuse | Why It Fails | Counter |\n   946||--------|-------------|---------|\n   947|| \"The brief is vague, so I'll fill in the gaps with reasonable assumptions\" | Inventing requirements violates the Zero Invention Policy. Assumptions become facts downstream. | Mark unknowns as `status: assumption` with ASM-XX IDs. Never present assumptions as confirmed requirements. |\n   948|| \"The stakeholder will clarify later, I'll put a placeholder\" | Placeholders propagate as literal text into epics and stories. Downstream agents treat them as requirements. | Use `status: pending` with explicit open_questions — for DECISION-BEARING gaps. A descriptive template field with no evidence is `not stated in inputs`, not a question (§11.6 \"A template field is not a question\"). |\n   949|| \"Every empty template column is a pending input — completeness demands it\" | It produced 59 open questions from one PRD (51 shaped \"what is the value of `field`? (missing)\", 17 sharing five assumptions, 33 addressed to the client), buried the eight real unknowns, and the prompt budget dropped 41 of them before the next step ran. Operators stop reading an inbox like that. | Apply the decision test: raise a question only when a downstream artifact would be built differently on the answer. Collapse same-field gaps into one question or none. Phrase it for the owner, never as the column name. More than a third of items with a PI row means you are registering the template, not the project. |\n   950|| \"This NFR is obvious, everyone knows it should be secure/performant\" | Obvious NFRs without measurable targets are unverifiable. \"Secure\" means nothing without criteria. | Every NFR needs: metric, target value, measurement method. \"Secure\" → \"OWASP Top 10 compliance verified by SAST scan.\" |\n   951|| \"I'll add more detail in the epics phase\" | PRD is the single source of truth for requirements. Epics decompose; they don't invent. | If it's a requirement, it belongs in the PRD. If it's a decomposition detail, it belongs in epics. Don't defer requirements. |\n   952|| \"The brief already covers this, I'm just reformatting\" | Reformatting without validation propagates errors. The PRD must verify, not just transcribe. | Cross-reference every requirement against the brief. Flag contradictions. Verify completeness against JTBD framework. |\n   953|| \"The spec is small enough to generate in one inference call\" | This was the original assumption. It produced a 1.3M-token prompt and a gateway 504 on a real engagement. Single-call generation does not scale with input size. | Always emit one section per Write/Edit call (Step 3 Phase B). The cost on small briefs is negligible; the cost on large ones is the difference between completing and timing out. |\n   954|| \"I'll re-read the brief whenever I need to remember a fact\" | Re-reading raw sources on every section is what causes the input-token explosion. 15 sections × full source re-read = the failure mode. | Read raw sources ONCE in Step 2. Distill to `_extractions/` in Step 2.5. Phase B reads extractions only. |\n   955|| \"I remember the template's shape, I don't need to re-open it for the skeleton\" | The context budget makes that memory unreliable by design: §10.4.1 forbids carrying the template between sections, so `open_questions` — section 16 of 16 — writes its three tables ~15 `Edit` cycles after the template was last read. Observed 2026-09-05 (`product-discovery__pli-20260905-k4i93s__pd-001`): missing `date:` header line, ASCII hyphen in the H1, two registries rendered as bullet lists, wrong `open_questions` columns. Four blocking gate findings, one repair lap, ~25 min of gate time, zero design defects. | Pin the shape in Phase A. Read `references/spec-template.md` immediately before the skeleton `Write` and transcribe the H1, all eight header lines, every heading and subheading, and every table's header + separator row. Phase B then fills rows, never authors structure. |\n   956|| \"This registry is empty, so I'll write `- none` instead of a table\" | Empty is a row count, not a shape change. A structural verifier reads the header row; a bullet list where a table belongs fails it, and the reader downstream loses the column contract. | Zero rows = header row + separator row, example rows deleted. Put the zero in `summary`. |\n   957|| \"The sidecar just carries my declared output parameters\" | `_open_questions` is reserved and universal (§11.6), not a declared parameter — it never appears in the `## Output parameters` table, so a sidecar built only from that table is always missing it. Observed 2026-09-05 (`product-discovery__pli-20260905-nrz19z__pd-001`): the sidecar carried `prd_output_path` alone, so the orchestrator scraped 34 rows out of the artifact and stamped every one \"came from a capability that has not adopted the structured sidecar contract\" — with no `assumption_if_unanswered`, every answer then counts as invalidating, including answers that agree with what the PRD already assumed. | Emit `_open_questions` on every Stepwise run, mirroring all three registries. §11.6 carries the field mapping for this registry shape; Step 6 only names it. |\n   958|| \"`fallback_behavior: BLOCKED` copies straight into `assumption_if_unanswered`\" | §11.6 forbids refusal tokens in that field — it renders as \"proceed on: BLOCKED\", which is worse than an open question with no assumption. The template sets `BLOCKED` on every `blocking: yes` row, so a naive copy poisons exactly the rows that matter most. | Write what the run actually did instead (\"the field is left pending and no value was assumed\"), set sidecar `blocking: true`, and put the reason in `why_it_matters`. §11.6's standard-registry mapping states this rule. |\n   959|| \"`scope` and `mvp_phasing` are just lists, the status/source rule is for requirements\" | The Template Rules bind every bulleted line in every section. A phasing decision without a source is an untraced claim exactly like an FR without one, and it is scored as such. | Every line in `scope` and `mvp_phasing` carries `\\| source: {ref} \\| status: {…}`. If the phase assignment is your inference rather than sourced, it is `status: assumption` with an ASM-XX and an `open_questions` row. |\n   960|\n   961|## Red Flags\n   962|\n   963|Signs that this PRD spec is being generated superficially or incorrectly:\n   964|\n   965|- FRs or NFRs have no acceptance criteria (unverifiable requirements)\n   966|- All items marked `status: complete` but no source tags present (untraced claims)\n   967|- Zero open_questions on a complex brief (indicates insufficient scrutiny)\n   968|- NFRs without measurable targets (\"fast\", \"secure\", \"scalable\" without numbers)\n   969|- Calendar dates or week numbers in output (violates Agentic-First Output Contract)\n   970|- Assumptions not tagged with ASM-XX IDs (invisible assumptions propagate as facts)\n   971|- FR count doesn't match brief scope (too few = missed requirements, too many = invented requirements)\n   972|- JTBDs missing or generic (\"user wants to do things efficiently\")\n   973|\n   974|## Verification Checklist\n   975|\n   976|Every item requires **evidence**, not assertion. \"Seems right\" is never sufficient.\n   977|\n   978|- [ ] Every FR has acceptance criteria — Evidence: each FR entry contains testable conditions\n   979|- [ ] Every NFR has measurable target — Evidence: metric + target value + measurement method per NFR\n   980|- [ ] Zero Invention Policy respected — Evidence: every item traces to brief, meeting transcript, or research input via source tag\n   981|- [ ] All assumptions tagged — Evidence: ASM-XX IDs on every `status: assumption` item\n   982|- [ ] Open questions captured — Evidence: open_questions section addresses gaps found during synthesis\n   983|- [ ] Literal registry complete — Evidence: every source-backed literal/enum/range/route/constraint in FRs/NFRs/ACs appears in `literal_registry`\n   984|- [ ] Structured contract emitted — Evidence: `contract_format: structured`, global AC IDs, enriched open_questions, and sha256 `content_hash`\n   985|- [ ] No calendar references — Evidence: grep for week/month/date patterns returns zero matches\n   986|- [ ] JTBD framework applied — Evidence: at least one Job-To-Be-Done per major user segment\n   987|- [ ] Spec validates against template — Evidence: all required sections from references/spec-template.md present\n   988|\n   989|## Reference Files\n   990|- `references/spec-template.md` — Agent-native spec structure\n   991|\n   992|## Required Tools\n   993|- **meeting-transcription** (`^1.0.0`): operation `transcribe`\n   994|\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":3}}
