[08:25:20] {"type":"tool_start","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-8cd95d575d6a00e1","toolName":"read","args":{"path":"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md","offset":190,"limit":250}}}
[08:25:20] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-8cd95d575d6a00e1","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"1541\" start=\"190\" end=\"439\" returned=\"250\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n   190|## 2. _progress.json — Early Signal Write\n   191|\n   192|**Purpose:** Prevents the orchestrator coordinator from sending SIGINT to a running skill.\n   193|\n   194|Write this as the **FIRST file action** in any skill execution (before creating any other outputs):\n   195|\n   196|```json\n   197|{\n   198|  \"skill\": \"{skill-name}\",\n   199|  \"session_id\": \"{SESSION_ID}\",\n   200|  \"status\": \"RUNNING\",\n   201|  \"started_at\": \"{ISO-8601 UTC, e.g. 2026-08-15T13:21:37Z}\",\n   202|  \"completed_at\": null,\n   203|  \"total\": 0,\n   204|  \"completed\": 0,\n   205|  \"items\": []\n   206|}\n   207|```\n   208|\n   209|Write to: `{output_folder}/_progress.json`\n   210|\n   211|**Before that write, check the path budget — §2.c.** A successful `_progress.json`\n   212|write is not evidence the output folder can hold your actual artifact.\n   213|\n   214|**Update cadence:**\n   215|- On FIC trigger (Section 5): `\"status\": \"COMPACTION_NEEDED\"`\n   216|- On completion: `\"status\": \"COMPLETED\"`, `\"completed_at\": \"{ISO-8601 UTC}\"`\n   217|- On failure: `\"status\": \"FAILED\"`, `\"completed_at\": \"{ISO-8601 UTC}\"`\n   218|\n   219|**UTC, not local time with a `Z` on it.** Read the real clock and convert. A local timestamp\n   220|labelled `Z` reads as a lie about when the work ran — Stepwise stamps its own state in true UTC,\n   221|so the two disagree by your offset and every reviewer sees fabricated evidence where there was\n   222|only a wrong clock. If you cannot get UTC, omit the field rather than guess.\n   223|\n   224|**Status vocabulary — closed set.** Every value carries distinct semantics; the set is\n   225|closed so a monitor can classify any tracker without knowing which skill wrote it.\n   226|\n   227|| status | meaning | live? |\n   228||---|---|---|\n   229|| `RUNNING` | step is executing | yes |\n   230|| `PARTIAL` | chunked step finished its chunk with work remaining; a later invocation resumes | yes |\n   231|| `COMPACTION_NEEDED` | FIC trigger (§5); awaiting compaction | yes |\n   232|| `COMPLETED` | all declared work done | no — terminal |\n   233|| `FAILED` | step could not complete | no — terminal |\n   234|| `SCOPE_REFUSED` | input exceeded the skill's scope envelope; step declined and recommended decomposition rather than producing a degraded artifact | no — terminal |\n   235|\n   236|**Never invent a synonym for a value already in the table** — `IN_PROGRESS`, `STARTED`,\n   237|`OK`, `DONE` and similar are prohibited precisely because they mean `RUNNING` or\n   238|`COMPLETED` while reading as unrecognized to a monitor keying on the set above.\n   239|\n   240|Adding a genuinely new state (distinct semantics, not a synonym) means adding a row to\n   241|this table in the same change. Do not introduce one ad hoc in a single skill: an\n   242|undocumented status is indistinguishable from a typo at read time.\n   243|\n   244|Item-level statuses inside `items[]` / `sections{}` are a **separate** vocabulary and are\n   245|not governed by this table — `\"status\": \"complete\"` on an entry is fine.\n   246|\n   247|### 2.a Seed the denominator (list shape — MANDATORY)\n   248|\n   249|The skeleton in the template above ships `\"total\": 0` because the count is not yet known\n   250|at FIRST ACTION. It is a placeholder, **not** the value you leave behind.\n   251|\n   252|**As soon as the item count is known — and before the first item is produced — write\n   253|`total` with the real number.** For most skills this is the end of the planning/discovery\n   254|step, once the catalog of services / contexts / ADRs / units / diagrams exists:\n   255|\n   256|```\n   257|total = len(ITEMS_TO_PRODUCE)      # + any fixed tail artifacts (manifest, audit)\n   258|UPDATE _progress.json → { \"total\": total }\n   259|```\n   260|\n   261|A tracker still reporting `total: 0` after production has begun is a defect, not a\n   262|cosmetic gap: `0/0` is indistinguishable from \"hung before doing anything\", which is\n   263|exactly the ambiguity this file exists to remove. Observed 2026-08-09\n   264|(`establishing-architecture-foundation`): a 27-minute run showed `0/0` end to end while\n   265|12 service files and a 36 KB manifest were being written.\n   266|\n   267|### 2.b Co-locate the tracker update with the checkpoint write (MANDATORY)\n   268|\n   269|Skills with a Write-Flush-Forget production loop (§5) typically maintain **two** state\n   270|files: `_checkpoint.json` (rich resume state, internal) and `_progress.json` (thin\n   271|liveness signal, read by the orchestrator and by humans). The observed failure is that\n   272|per-item bookkeeping lands in the checkpoint and never in the tracker — the loop is\n   273|invisible from outside for its entire duration.\n   274|\n   275|**Rule: the `_progress.json` update is part of the same loop step as the checkpoint\n   276|write. Never one without the other.** Both go in the same numbered sub-step so a skipped\n   277|write is visible when reading the skill:\n   278|\n   279|```\n   280|## WRITE CHECKPOINT + UPDATE PROGRESS (single step — never split)\n   281|   1. Serialize INDEX to CHECKPOINT_FILE (_checkpoint.json)\n   282|   2. UPDATE _progress.json → completed += 1, items += [{id, status: \"complete\", file}]\n   283|```\n   284|\n   285|This applies to every discrete unit the loop emits: per-service, per-context, per-ADR,\n   286|per-unit, per-diagram, per-tab.\n   287|\n   288|**Timestamps come from a real clock read.** `started_at` and `completed_at` MUST be\n   289|actual timestamps at the moment of writing — never round numbers back-filled at the end\n   290|to describe what the run \"would have\" done. A `completed_at` that postdates the file's\n   291|own mtime is a fabricated tracker and fails §2.1 check 5.\n   292|\n   293|**Schema variants.** A skill picks ONE of the three shapes below based on its work model\n   294|and uses it consistently for the whole run. The common envelope (`skill`, `session_id`,\n   295|`status`, `started_at`, `completed_at`) is identical across variants.\n   296|\n   297|- **List shape (default — shown above)** — uses `total`, `completed`, `items[]`. Suits\n   298|  skills that produce many discrete artifacts (e.g. `implementing-code` writing source\n   299|  files; `planning-code-tasks` tracking task entries).\n   300|- **Section shape** — uses `skeleton_written` (bool) + `sections{}` map. Suits skills\n   301|  that produce a single multi-section spec file from large unstructured inputs. See\n   302|  **Section 10** for the full protocol (skeleton-first + one-section-per-write +\n   303|  source extraction + CONTINUE re-entry).\n   304|- **Hybrid shape** — carries BOTH: `skeleton_written` + `sections{}` for the manifest,\n   305|  AND `total` / `completed` / `items[]` for the per-item files produced alongside it.\n   306|  Suits skills that emit a sectioned manifest *and* a fan-out of per-entry artifacts\n   307|  (e.g. `secreview-static-scanning`: `sections{}` tracks the SCAN-MANIFEST while\n   308|  `items[]` grows one entry per finding). See §10.1.\n   309|\n   310|**A hybrid tracker MUST say so.** State the variant in the FIRST ACTION callout —\n   311|\"write `_progress.json` using the **hybrid** variant (per §10.1)\" — so a reader can tell\n   312|a deliberate hybrid from a skill that drifted between two shapes. Carrying both field\n   313|groups *without* that declaration is the drift failure §2.1 check 5 catches:\n   314|`establishing-architecture-foundation` (fixed 2026-08-09) incremented list-shape counters\n   315|in one phase and wrote `sections{}` in the next, leaving no coherent denominator.\n   316|\n   317|Both halves of a hybrid follow their own rules: §2.a seeds `total` for the `items[]` half,\n   318|§10 governs the `sections{}` half. `secreview-memory-synthesis` and the `pentest-*` family\n   319|are the reference implementations.\n   320|\n   321|### Skill-Level Enforcement (FIRST ACTION / LAST ACTION)\n   322|\n   323|Every skill MUST include these two inline callouts — agents do not reliably follow\n   324|protocol-only references for session bookend writes:\n   325|\n   326|**At session start** (after folder creation, before any other file write):\n   327|\n   328|> **FIRST ACTION — MANDATORY:** Write `_progress.json` to the output folder before any other file write.\n   329|> This prevents the orchestrator from sending SIGINT.\n   330|\n   331|Include the minimal JSON schema inline in the skill so the agent can write immediately\n   332|without reading this file first. Use `\"session_id\": \"initializing\"` as placeholder.\n   333|\n   334|**At session end** (after all spec files written, after Memory Bank writes):\n   335|\n   336|> **LAST ACTION — MANDATORY:** Update `_progress.json` status to `COMPLETED` with `completed_at` timestamp.\n   337|> If the session failed, set status to `FAILED` instead.\n   338|\n   339|These callouts are NOT infrastructure duplication — they are the minimum viable bridge\n   340|between protocol and agent behavior. The full lifecycle lives here; the callouts trigger it.\n   341|\n   342|**For `implementing-code` only** — also include `output_contract` (survives compaction):\n   343|\n   344|```json\n   345|{\n   346|  \"skill\": \"implementing-code\",\n   347|  \"session_id\": \"{SESSION_ID}\",\n   348|  \"status\": \"RUNNING\",\n   349|  \"output_contract\": {\n   350|    \"progress_folder_path\": \"{progress_folder_path}\",\n   351|    \"impl_state_file\": \"IMPL-STATE-{session_id}.md\",\n   352|    \"source_path\": \"{source_path}\",\n   353|    \"expected_outputs\": [\"source_path\", \"impl_state_path\"]\n   354|  }\n   355|}\n   356|```\n   357|\n   358|Pin the same values in the IMPL-STATE header under `output_contract`. After context\n   359|compaction, read IMPL-STATE header (not filesystem) to recover the output path and format.\n   360|\n   361|### 2.c Path budget — measure the LONGEST path, not the first one (MANDATORY)\n   362|\n   363|`_progress.json` is the shortest filename a run ever writes. Writing it successfully\n   364|proves nothing about the artifact that follows it, and on Windows the gap between the\n   365|two is the entire failure.\n   366|\n   367|**At FIRST ACTION, alongside the `_progress.json` write, compute the budget from the\n   368|longest path THIS run will produce:**\n   369|\n   370|```\n   371|BUDGET = len(abspath(output_folder)) + 1 + len(longest filename this run emits)\n   372|         # your own artifact name — IMPL-STATE-{SESSION_ID}.md,\n   373|         # PLAN-REVIEW-{SESSION_ID}.json, <section>-{SESSION_ID}.md —\n   374|         # NEVER _progress.json, which is 14 characters\n   375|BUDGET > 240  ->  write one probe file at exactly that length, then delete it\n   376|```\n   377|\n   378|240, not 260: `.runlogs/<name>-<attempt>.txt`, sidecars and per-item files live under\n   379|the same root and outrun the artifact that set the budget.\n   380|\n   381|**A probe failure is a PATH BUDGET failure, not a missing directory.** The signature is\n   382|counter-intuitive, and misreading it is the reason this rule exists:\n   383|\n   384|| symptom | what it actually means |\n   385||---|---|\n   386|| `ENAMETOOLONG` on write | over the limit — unambiguous |\n   387|| `ENOENT` on a path whose parent directory you just wrote into | over the limit. Windows returns `ERROR_PATH_NOT_FOUND` for an over-long path on some APIs and `ERROR_FILENAME_EXCED_RANGE` on others; the first surfaces as `ENOENT` and reads as \"the folder is missing\" |\n   388|\n   389|**On a probe failure, do NOT investigate with shell calls.** The folder is fine, `mkdir`\n   390|reports success, and a compound diagnostic command is exactly what a headless\n   391|authorization policy fails closed on — converting a legible path error into an\n   392|illegible approval denial. Instead, in this order:\n   393|\n   394|1. `_progress.json` → `status: FAILED` with a real `completed_at`. It is writable; it is\n   395|   short. It is the only artifact that survives this.\n   396|2. `stepwise session exec-fail --message \"path budget exceeded: <N> chars at <path>;\n   397|   shorten the project root or the output slug\"`.\n   398|3. Never end the turn in prose. A prose explanation exits 0 and the orchestrator records\n   399|   the step as successful (§2.1).\n   400|\n   401|You cannot repair this from inside the run: the path is composed upstream, from the\n   402|project root and the parameters handed to you. Reporting it precisely IS the fix.\n   403|\n   404|Two contributors routinely blow the budget and neither is capped by §1. The directory\n   405|segment comes from `feature_id` and §1 governs only the SESSION_ID *inside* a filename,\n   406|not the folder path around it. And §1's own `≤ 32 chars` cap applies to a MINTED\n   407|SESSION_ID — the fallback branch, \"the runtime's `session = '...'` name verbatim\", has\n   408|no cap at all. Name both in the `exec-fail` message so the operator fixes the right one.\n   409|\n   410|Observed 2026-09-23 (`code-development-lean`, `pli-20260923-9as14x` iter-2): an\n   411|80-character sub-goal slug plus a 57-character SESSION_ID in the filename put\n   412|`IMPL-STATE-*.md` at 267 characters. `_progress.json` wrote fine at 210. The step read\n   413|the resulting `ENOENT` as a missing output folder, issued a compound PowerShell\n   414|diagnostic to inspect it, had that denied by headless authorization, and ended in prose\n   415|with no IMPL-STATE, no sidecar, a tracker still reading `RUNNING`, and a harness that\n   416|recorded the step as a success. The plan-review step had hit the identical 267-character\n   417|limit nine minutes earlier.\n   418|\n   419|---\n   420|\n   421|### 2.1 Pre-Completion Self-Validation Gate (universal — generative skills)\n   422|\n   423|**Purpose:** Prevent predictable, repeatable gaps from reaching the human quality gate.\n   424|A generative step MUST self-validate against the checklist below **immediately before**\n   425|the LAST ACTION completion write (`_progress.json.status: COMPLETED`). This runs on the\n   426|agent's own output, before any human sees it.\n   427|\n   428|Run all five checks. Each is tech-agnostic — no framework, language, or project name.\n   429|\n   430|1. **Input-contract completeness.** Every required input, section, and output-contract\n   431|   item declared by the skill/step is addressed in the artifact. No declared section is\n   432|   empty, stubbed, or silently omitted.\n   433|2. **No unresolved placeholders.** The artifact contains no lingering `[TBD]`,\n   434|   `[to be identified]`, `[PLACEHOLDER]`, `TODO`, or unanswered `OQ-###` / open-question\n   435|   left as if resolved. Each is either resolved from available evidence OR surfaced\n   436|   explicitly as a pending item / assumption / blocker — never left silent. An\n   437|   `OQ-########` id that appears in the prompt's `## DECISIONS` / `## PROJECT DECISIONS`\n   438|   block is settled — the artifact must apply the decision and cite the id, never\n   439|   list it as open.\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":4}}
