[08:25:23] {"type":"tool_start","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-8b1a277b2f7d47ba","toolName":"read","args":{"path":"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md","offset":440,"limit":300}}}
[08:25:23] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-8b1a277b2f7d47ba","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"1541\" start=\"440\" end=\"739\" returned=\"300\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n   440|3. **Context-pack / source grounding.** Every non-trivial claim, path, identifier,\n   441|   technology, decision, estimate, and status traces to context-pack, upstream artifacts,\n   442|   or the source under analysis. Nothing is invented. If evidence is missing, mark the\n   443|   uncertainty visible rather than fabricating a resolution.\n   444|4. **Format-contract conformance.** Output matches the skill's declared structure:\n   445|   headings, stable IDs, tables, schemas, traceability links, filename, and status\n   446|   conventions.\n   447|5. **Tracker integrity.** `_progress.json` is a truthful record of the run, not a summary\n   448|   written at the end. Check the half/halves your declared §2 variant actually uses:\n   449|   - **list half** (list or hybrid): `total > 0` — a `COMPLETED` step still showing\n   450|     `total: 0` never seeded its denominator per §2.a — and `completed == total`.\n   451|   - **section half** (section or hybrid): `skeleton_written == true` and every declared\n   452|     section has a terminal status.\n   453|   - **all variants**: the tracker matches the variant the skill declared at FIRST ACTION\n   454|     and did not drift into another mid-run; `status` is from the §2 closed set; and\n   455|     `started_at` / `completed_at` are real clock reads, with `completed_at` not\n   456|     postdating the write itself.\n   457|\n   458|   Carrying both field groups is NOT a failure when the skill declared the hybrid variant\n   459|   — that is the sanctioned third shape. It IS a failure when the skill declared list or\n   460|   section and grew the other half mid-run.\n   461|\n   462|**On failure of any check:**\n   463|- If self-repairable from available evidence → repair, then re-run the checklist.\n   464|- If it requires information the agent does not have → do **not** mark `COMPLETED`.\n   465|  Record the gap as a visible pending item / open question / assumption in the artifact\n   466|  and (when a blocker) escalate per §8 Deviation taxonomy. Marking a step COMPLETED with\n   467|  a known, silent gap is a protocol violation.\n   468|\n   469|**If you cannot finish the work at all — the run out of room / out of budget path.**\n   470|This is distinct from a gap in the inputs: the plan is sound, some items are done, and you\n   471|have concluded you cannot produce the rest in this execution. Ending your turn with a prose\n   472|sentence saying so is NOT a failure signal. The process exits 0, the orchestrator reads a\n   473|clean exit as success, the step is marked `completed`, and the next step runs against\n   474|whatever half-built output exists. Three things are mandatory instead:\n   475|\n   476|1. **Leave the tracker truthful.** Do not set `COMPLETED`. `completed` and `total` must\n   477|   describe what is actually on disk, and every finished item must be listed. A later\n   478|   REPAIR/CONTINUE run resumes from exactly this, so an accurate tracker is what makes the\n   479|   work recoverable rather than repeated.\n   480|2. **Persist the durable checkpoint** — whatever your skill uses to resume (`_checkpoint.json`,\n   481|   the carry-forward index). Write it before you stop, not after you decide to stop.\n   482|3. **Signal failure explicitly.** Call `stepwise session exec-fail --message \"<what is done,\n   483|   what remains, where to resume>\"`. That message is what a human reads and what a retry\n   484|   acts on. Never let an unfinished run return normally.\n   485|\n   486|Observed 2026-09-06 (`generating-adrs`, `pli-20260906-1vz9ubr`): the run produced 2 of 6\n   487|ADRs and none of the manifest, left `_progress.json` at `status: RUNNING, completed: 2,\n   488|total: 7`, said \"I'm unable to complete the remaining ADR generation within this execution\n   489|window\" and returned. `EXECUTION_SUCCESS, exitCode=0`. The step was marked completed, the\n   490|downstream review step reviewed the two ADRs that existed and passed them, and the\n   491|verification step then failed on a manifest that was never written — three steps and a\n   492|wasted lap to surface a failure the producing step already knew about.\n   493|\n   494|**Budget awareness.** Check before starting each item, not after: if finishing the next\n   495|item would exhaust your room, stop at the item boundary and take the three steps above.\n   496|Stopping cleanly between items with an accurate tracker is a good outcome. Stopping\n   497|mid-item, or stopping silently, is not.\n   498|\n   499|**Skill-Level Enforcement.** Generative skills SHOULD include a one-line inline callout\n   500|at their finalization step so the agent triggers this without reading the protocol first:\n   501|\n   502|> **PRE-COMPLETION CHECK — MANDATORY:** Before the LAST ACTION completion write, self-validate\n   503|> the artifact against §2.1 (input-contract completeness · no unresolved placeholders ·\n   504|> context-pack grounding · format conformance · tracker integrity). Repair or surface gaps;\n   505|> never complete silently. A `COMPLETED` tracker still showing `total: 0` fails check 5.\n   506|\n   507|This gate is the counterpart to the FIRST/LAST ACTION callouts in §2: FIRST ACTION opens\n   508|the step, §2.1 qualifies the output, LAST ACTION closes it.\n   509|\n   510|---\n   511|\n   512|\n   513|---\n   514|\n   515|### 10.5 Tool Discipline (Hard Rules)\n   516|\n   517|These rules exist because each one has been observed as a failure mode. Violations defeat the protocol even when the prose discipline appears to be followed.\n   518|\n   519|- **Skeleton write: use `Write`.** Single tool call, single file creation. Do NOT compose the skeleton via `Bash` heredoc.\n   520|- **Section population: use `Edit`.** Targeted find-and-replace, one section per call. The `Edit` tool handles unicode natively and validates the prior content matches what you expect.\n   521|- **NEVER use `Bash` + `sed` / `awk` / `python3 <<EOF` / `cat >` to mutate SPEC_FILE.** These are bulk-rewrite paths disguised as targeted operations. They read the whole file into memory, transform it, and write it back — the exact pattern Phase B is designed to prevent. They also bypass `Edit`'s prior-content validation, so a bug in the script silently corrupts the spec.\n   522|- **If `Edit` fails on a section, fix the `Edit` call.** Provide more surrounding context to make `old_string` unique. Do NOT switch to `Bash` / `python3` as a workaround — that path has been observed to cascade: agent hits one snag, then rewrites all remaining sections in a single script, violating one-section-per-write.\n   523|- **NEVER compose multiple sections in memory before writing.**\n   524|- **NEVER emit more than one section per Write/Edit call.**\n   525|- **NEVER re-read raw sources during Phase B.** Read `_extractions/*.md` files, or SPEC_FILE for declared cross-section state.\n   526|- **RETIRED (2026-09-09):** this bullet previously told agents to swap decorative non-ASCII glyphs (em dashes, smart quotes, `…`, `•`, `×`, NBSP) for ASCII equivalents, purely to stay on the targeted-`Edit` path and avoid the §10.5.1 fallback. That fallback is retired (see §10.5.1) — targeted `Edit` now handles non-ASCII content of any kind, decorative or required, natively. No transliteration is needed for tool-safety reasons. Accented and localized characters in non-English output (`á é í ó ú ñ ü ç ã …`) remain content to preserve exactly, as always.\n   527|\n   528|If a section's content genuinely will not fit a single `Edit` call (extreme size), split that one section into sub-edits — but every sub-edit is still an `Edit` call against SPEC_FILE, never a Bash mutation.\n   529|\n   530|---\n   531|\n   532|#### 10.5.1 Non-ASCII Content Fallback — RESOLVED, retired 2026-09-09\n   533|\n   534|**Status:** RESOLVED. The runtime bug this subsection worked around is fixed. Targeted `Edit` is the path for every section, in every language — there is no fallback to apply. This subsection is kept (not deleted) only so the many existing `§10.5.1` citations across skill files keep resolving to something; do not add new detection logic that routes around targeted `Edit`.\n   535|\n   536|**History (for context, no longer actionable).** Some runtimes' targeted-edit operations were observed (first noted 2026-05-19) to fail with `'ascii' codec can't encode character ...` errors when `new_string` contained non-ASCII codepoints (accented vowels in Spanish / French / Portuguese / German, em dashes, smart quotes, etc.), and the failure was destructive — the tool truncated the file before catching the encode exception, zeroing prior content. The workaround was to detect non-English/non-ASCII content and force full-file replace (`Write`) instead of targeted `Edit` for the whole run, at ~5× the token cost.\n   537|\n   538|**Verification (2026-09-09).** Re-tested directly against the current runtime: targeted `Edit` calls with `new_string` containing Spanish accents/punctuation (á é í ó ú ñ ü ¿ ¡), em dash, smart quotes, CJK, and emoji — single and back-to-back — all succeeded with correct UTF-8 output, no truncation, sibling content untouched. The underlying tool fix described below appears to be in place.\n   539|\n   540|**If this ever regresses:** the two-line runtime fix this subsection used to wait on was (1) UTF-8 encoding in the edit tool's write path, (2) atomic-write semantics so a failed edit leaves the prior file intact. §10.5.2 (Safe-Write Protocol) stays in force regardless as a general defensive backup — it is not specific to this bug and was never retired.\n   541|\n   542|---\n   543|\n   544|#### 10.5.2 Safe-Write Protocol (mandatory defensive backup for SPEC files)\n   545|\n   546|**Why this exists.** Even with the discipline in §10.5, a single bad targeted-edit call has been observed to truncate a SPEC file to 0 bytes mid-run (authored work wiped) — the non-ASCII-specific case is resolved (§10.5.1), but any other cause of a bad `Edit` call is still possible. The Safe-Write Protocol adds a `.bak` snapshot and a destructive-edit refusal gate so a single bad call cannot destroy authored work.\n   547|\n   548|**Applies to:** any skill writing to `RESEARCH-SPEC-*.md`, `PLAN-SPEC-*.md`, `IMPL-STATE-*.md`, `REVIEW-SPEC-*.md`, or any other SPEC-shaped artifact the skill declares.\n   549|\n   550|**Rule 1 — Defensive backup before any large edit:**\n   551|\n   552|Whenever you must edit a SPEC file larger than 20 KB AND the edit changes more than 30% of the file's lines:\n   553|\n   554|1. Compute: `lines_to_change` (new content vs current).\n   555|2. If `lines_to_change > 0.30 * total_lines AND file_size_kb > 20`:\n   556|   - `cp {target_file} {target_file}.bak.{ISO_TIMESTAMP}` via a single Bash call.\n   557|   - Proceed with the edit.\n   558|   - On success (post-edit file is non-empty AND > 50% of pre-edit size): delete the `.bak` file.\n   559|   - On failure or suspected truncation (post-edit file is < 50% of pre-edit size): restore from `.bak`, then emit a structured event:\n   560|     ```json\n   561|     {\"event\":\"safe_write_restore\",\"file\":\"<path>\",\"reason\":\"post_edit_too_small\",\"pre_size\":<N>,\"post_size\":<M>}\n   562|     ```\n   563|\n   564|**Rule 2 — Never `replace_all=true` on SPEC files.** Reinforces §10.5 \"Section population: use `Edit`.\" Use targeted, anchored Edits with non-empty `old_string`. If a full rewrite is genuinely needed, write to a new file first, then `mv` atomically.\n   565|\n   566|**Rule 3 — Refuse destructive `replace` calls (empty / too-short `old_string`):**\n   567|\n   568|Before any `replace` / `Edit` whose `old_string` is empty (`\"\"`) or shorter than 10 characters: REFUSE the operation. Emit:\n   569|\n   570|```json\n   571|{\"event\":\"safe_write_refuse\",\"reason\":\"old_string_too_short_or_empty\",\"file\":\"<path>\",\"old_string_length\":<N>}\n   572|```\n   573|\n   574|This is the direct guard for whole-file overwrites caused by under-specified anchors.\n   575|\n   576|**Rule 4 — Stray `.bak` cleanup at FIRST ACTION:**\n   577|\n   578|In each section-shape skill's FIRST ACTION step (the `_progress.json` write described in §2), also glob `{output_path}/*.bak.*` and delete any files older than 1 hour. This bounds disk overhead from crashes that leave a `.bak` on disk.\n   579|\n   580|**Relationship to §10.5 and §10.5.1.**\n   581|- §10.5 prevents bulk-rewrite cascades from `Bash` / `sed` / `python3`.\n   582|- §10.5.1 documents the (now-resolved) non-ASCII-truncation failure mode; no active mitigation needed from it anymore.\n   583|- §10.5.2 (this section) is the last line of defense — even if the agent uses the correct tool with the correct intent, the `.bak` snapshot survives a runtime malfunction.\n   584|\n   585|---\n   586|\n   587|#### 10.5.3 Tool-Output Retention (context-budget hard rule)\n   588|\n   589|**Why this exists.** Running a command is a one-time cost; the expense is that its raw output then sits in the conversation and is **re-sent on every subsequent model round-trip** in the turn (and across turns until compaction). In an observed code excursion, a single tool-heavy turn (build + `npm test` + 14 edits) reached **6.9M input tokens** — not from re-reading across turns, but because verbose build/test logs and re-read source files rode along on every one of ~70 round-trips inside that one turn. The fix is not to run or log less. It is to **always execute fully, then retain only what the next round-trip needs.**\n   590|\n   591|**The rule is about retention, never about execution. Always run the full command.** What you keep resident is decided by the output's *role*:\n   592|\n   593|| Regime | Examples | Keep resident | Rationale |\n   594||--------|----------|---------------|-----------|\n   595|| **Success** | build OK, tests green, lint clean, deploy healthy, migration applied | the **verdict only** — counts, exit code, the one summary line | A passing log carries no actionable detail; the body is pure noise. Full output is never needed. |\n   596|| **Failure** | failing test, compile error, probe 5xx, healthcheck down | the **failing slice** — the error, the stack, the failing names | The signal is the failure (usually <5% of the log). The passing lines before it are noise. Trim to the failure, not the whole run. |\n   597|| **Output *is* the evidence** | stack trace under root-cause, grep map being built, scanner findings, coverage report, diff under review | full fidelity **for this turn only**, then distill → drop | Sampling here can lose correctness, so capture full once, write the conclusion to the note/artifact, then drop the raw bytes — do not let them ride into the next round-trip. |\n   598|\n   599|**The mechanism that satisfies \"I sometimes need the full log\" without paying to re-send it — redirect to a file, summarize into context, read on demand:**\n   600|\n   601|```\n   602|npm test > .runlogs/test.txt 2>&1; tail -40 .runlogs/test.txt      # context gets the tail + counts\n   603|grep -A30 \"FAIL src/work-orders\" .runlogs/test.txt                  # full detail ONLY when a failure needs it\n   604|```\n   605|\n   606|You never lose the full log — you stop re-billing it. Prefer the tool's own quiet/summary mode at the source where one exists (`jest` summary reporter, `--silent`, `pytest -q`, `gradle --quiet`, `npm --silent`), and on a run you expect to pass do **not** enable the runner's verbose / per-case-expanding mode (`--verbose`, `-vv`, `--reporter=spec`, etc.) — that prints a line per assertion and is the exact opposite of retention. Reach for verbose only after a failure, scoped to the failing target.\n   607|\n   608|**Hard rules:**\n   609|\n   610|- Never paste a large raw log (build/test/scan/probe) into the transcript when its outcome is success — emit the verdict line instead.\n   611|- On failure, emit only the failing portion; redirect the full log to a file and `grep`/`tail` it on demand.\n   612|- After any verification run, **distill the result (pass/fail counts, the exit verdict) into the working-set note or the artifact, then drop the raw output from working memory** — the same discipline §10.4.1 / §10.6 apply to re-read source.\n   613|- Do not re-read a file you just edited in the same turn to \"re-orient\" — reason from the edit result. (Observed: one service file read 4× in a single turn.)\n   614|\n   615|**Enumeration guardrail (do NOT trim these away).** When the output is an enumeration-complete deliverable — a security scan's findings, a coverage-gap list, a toolchain inventory — every item matters and must not be sampled. The rule still applies, but \"full fidelity\" lands in the **artifact/output file**, not the chat transcript: write all findings to the file, keep only the count + the file path resident. Full is retained where it is the deliverable; it is simply not re-sent on every round-trip.\n   616|\n   617|**Applies to:** any skill that runs build/test/lint/deploy/probe/scan commands inside a multi-round-trip turn. Cited by `conducting-excursion`, `implementing-code`, `fixing-bugs`, and the quick-lane `implementing-quick-change` (the quick-fix playlist runs `fixing-bugs`, so both quick lanes are covered); other tool-running skills (`bootstrapping-runtime-environment`, `launching-app`, `launching-subscription`, `tearing-down-services`, `managing-releases`, `researching-bug-fixing`, `reviewing-code`, `reviewing-code-web`, `api-smoke-probe`, `browser-smoke-probe`, and the security/enumeration skills with the guardrail above) adopt the §10.5.3 citation as they are next touched.\n   618|\n   619|---\n   620|\n   621|\n   622|---\n   623|\n   624|#### 10.5.4 Values you cannot compute — checksums and digests (mandatory)\n   625|\n   626|**You cannot compute a SHA-256 in your head. Do not write one as if you had.**\n   627|\n   628|Several artifact templates carry a `content_hash` — a sha256 over the artifact's\n   629|own content with the `content_hash:` line itself excluded — and a verifier\n   630|recomputes it. Stating that contract in prose and leaving the agent to satisfy\n   631|it does not produce a hash; it produces a plausible-looking 64-character hex\n   632|string that fails every check.\n   633|\n   634|Field evidence (2026-09-06): across 117 hashed artifacts on one machine, two\n   635|project trees and three months, **35 verified — roughly one in three**. For\n   636|PRD-SPEC specifically it is 22 of 96.\n   637|\n   638|The intermittency is the point, and it is worse than a clean failure. Some runs\n   639|get it right, presumably by shelling out; most do not, and nothing in the\n   640|artifact says which happened. A gate that fails three times in four for no\n   641|stated reason reads as flaky rather than as a contract nobody can satisfy by\n   642|hand.\n   643|\n   644|On the run that surfaced it, the mismatch failed the automated gate, burned a\n   645|full repair lap re-running the producing step, failed identically, and escalated\n   646|to a human — while that repair lap had genuinely fixed two other findings with no\n   647|regressions. The value was recomputed on the second lap and was wrong again,\n   648|differently: it was never a hash of anything. No canonicalisation matched, and no\n   649|prefix of the document matched either.\n   650|\n   651|**The rule.** Any field whose value is defined by a computation — a checksum, a\n   652|digest, a byte count, a line count — is produced by RUNNING the computation and\n   653|pasting its output. Never by estimating, and never by writing something of the\n   654|right shape.\n   655|\n   656|**For `content_hash`, do exactly this, as the LAST write before the §11 sidecar:**\n   657|\n   658|1. Write the artifact with the line `content_hash: ` followed by 64 zeros. The\n   659|   placeholder must be 64 hex characters or the verifier's own regex will not\n   660|   recognise the line, and it will report the hash as missing rather than wrong.\n   661|2. Run this, substituting your artifact's path:\n   662|\n   663|   ```bash\n   664|   python3 - \"$SPEC_FILE\" <<'EOF'\n   665|   import hashlib, re, sys\n   666|   path = sys.argv[1]\n   667|   text = open(path, encoding='utf-8').read()\n   668|   m = re.search(r'^content_hash:\\s*([0-9a-f]{64})\\s*\\n?', text, re.M)\n   669|   if not m:\n   670|       sys.exit('content_hash line missing or not 64 hex characters')\n   671|   canonical = text[:m.start()] + text[m.end():]\n   672|   digest = hashlib.sha256(canonical.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()\n   673|   open(path, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write(\n   674|       text[:m.start()] + f'content_hash: {digest}\\n' + text[m.end():]\n   675|   )\n   676|   print(digest)\n   677|   EOF\n   678|   ```\n   679|\n   680|3. The digest it prints is the value now in the file. Report that value if your\n   681|   sidecar or audit asks for it — do not retype it from memory.\n   682|\n   683|Removing the whole `content_hash:` line yields the same canonical text whatever\n   684|value sits on it, which is what makes this converge in one pass: the verifier\n   685|strips the line exactly as this does.\n   686|\n   687|**This is the one sanctioned exception to the `python3`-mutation ban above.**\n   688|That ban exists to stop bulk section rewrites that bypass `Edit`'s prior-content\n   689|validation. This script rewrites a single header line whose correct value cannot\n   690|be known before the file is final, it runs once after every section is written,\n   691|and it is the only way the field can be right.\n   692|\n   693|---\n   694|\n   695|#### 10.5.5 Tool-Denial Adaptation (mandatory) — a denial is terminal for that exact command\n   696|\n   697|**A tool/command-policy denial (`decision: deny`, an approval-required refusal, or\n   698|equivalent) is a terminal signal for the exact call that was denied — never a\n   699|transient failure to retry as-is.** The environment is telling you, deterministically,\n   700|that this literal command will not be allowed to run; issuing it again produces the\n   701|identical denial, not a different outcome.\n   702|\n   703|Field evidence (2026-09-12, FairShare `local-runtime-validation`, `smoke-probing`\n   704|step): an HTTP health probe (`curl -s http://localhost:3000/health/ready`) was denied\n   705|by the project's command policy, which returned `suggestedAlternatives:\n   706|[\"Bash(pnpm:*)\",\"Bash(bun:*)\",\"Bash(bun run test:*)\"]` in the same response. The agent\n   707|reissued the byte-identical denied command **266 times** until it hit `max_steps` —\n   708|**31.8M tokens** for zero forward progress, and the step ended `exec_failed` with no\n   709|outputs at all. The alternatives were returned and never read, let alone acted on.\n   710|\n   711|The identical shape then recurred twice more in the same project, in two different\n   712|skills, on two different commands: `bootstrapping-runtime-environment` issued\n   713|`bun run prisma:generate` (124+ identical denials) and, separately,\n   714|`launching-app` issued `bun run build` (201 identical denials, manually\n   715|interrupted) — both denied for the same reason (\"approval is required, but no\n   716|operator is present\"), both returning the same `suggestedAlternatives`, neither\n   717|ever read. This is not three unrelated incidents; it is one recurring shape —\n   718|**a package.json script invoked via `<manager> run <script>`** — that any skill\n   719|shelling out in an npm-ecosystem project can hit, not a defect local to one skill.\n   720|\n   721|**The rule:**\n   722|\n   723|1. On any denial, do not reissue the same command (same binary, same arguments) a\n   724|   second time. One denial is information; a second identical one is not — it is\n   725|   pure waste, since the policy has not changed between your two calls.\n   726|2. Read the denial payload for `suggestedAlternatives` (or an equivalent hint) and\n   727|   for the project's command policy generally. If an allowed alternative can perform\n   728|   the SAME operation — a different program, a different invocation shape, a\n   729|   language runtime already on the allowlist — rewrite the call using it and\n   730|   continue the SAME plan; only the transport/mechanism changes, not the goal.\n   731|3. If no allowed alternative can perform the operation at all, that is a hard\n   732|   blocker, not a retry target: stop after that one adaptation attempt, record the\n   733|   blocker explicitly (open_questions / audit / tracker — never a silent\n   734|   zero-finding PASS), and signal failure (`stepwise session exec-fail` or the\n   735|   skill's equivalent) naming the missing capability. Do not spend the remaining\n   736|   step budget on further attempts of the same denied operation.\n   737|\n   738|**The concrete recipe for the recurring `<manager> run <script>` shape** (this is\n   739|the single most common case rule 2 will need in practice): `<manager> run\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":6}}
