[08:25:25] {"type":"tool_start","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-8387c3048e76a933","toolName":"read","args":{"path":"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md","offset":740,"limit":280}}}
[08:25:25] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-8387c3048e76a933","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"1541\" start=\"740\" end=\"1019\" returned=\"280\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n   740|<script-name>` asks the sandbox to resolve and execute an ARBITRARY named script\n   741|from `package.json` — an approval policy that pre-authorizes a known binary prefix\n   742|(`bun`, `bunx`, `pnpm`, `npx`) cannot pre-authorize an arbitrary script name, and\n   743|denies it on sight, every time, identically. Before running any package.json\n   744|script, or immediately on its first denial:\n   745|\n   746|1. Read the script's definition from `package.json`'s `scripts` block.\n   747|2. If it is a SINGLE binary invocation (e.g. `\"build\": \"tsc -p tsconfig.json\"`,\n   748|   `\"prisma:generate\": \"prisma generate\"`) — call that binary directly through the\n   749|   allowlisted runner for the detected package manager: `bunx <binary> <args>` /\n   750|   `pnpm exec <binary> <args>` / `npx <binary> <args>` / `yarn dlx <binary> <args>`.\n   751|   This is not a guess or a workaround; it produces the identical program run,\n   752|   through a form the policy can actually authorize.\n   753|3. If it is COMPOUND (`&&`, `;`, `|`, multiple programs) there is no single-binary\n   754|   equivalent — do not invent one. This is a hard blocker per rule 3 above: record\n   755|   that the project's command policy has no path to run this specific script, and\n   756|   name the exact script. (A project can pre-approve a specific compound script,\n   757|   e.g. `Bash(bun run test:*)` in the evidence above — that is a policy decision\n   758|   for the project to make, not something a skill can route around.)\n   759|\n   760|**Applies to:** every skill that can shell out or call a tool subject to approval or\n   761|command-policy gating — this is universal, not specific to one tool or one skill.\n   762|`api-smoke-probe`, `bootstrapping-runtime-environment`, and `launching-app` each\n   763|carry the incident's own Known Failure Modes entry as concrete instances that\n   764|produced or reinforced this rule.\n   765|\n   766|---\n   767|\n   768|## 11. Harness Output Sidecar\n   769|\n   770|**Purpose.** The Stepwise harness reads each step's declared outputs from a sidecar JSON file written at a path the harness controls. If this file is missing, the orchestrator records `outputs: {}` in `state.json`, the UI's artifact panel renders empty, and downstream steps cannot pick up output values via propagation. **Artifacts on disk are not enough** — the sidecar is the framework contract between the skill and the orchestrator.\n   771|\n   772|**Applicability.** Any skill that may be invoked by Stepwise. Standalone invocations (no orchestrator) do not need the sidecar — see the trigger gate below.\n   773|\n   774|**When this section applies.** The input prompt contains a `## Run metadata` block with an `output_file = '<absolute path>'` line. If that block is absent (e.g. standalone invocation outside Stepwise), skip the sidecar write entirely.\n   775|\n   776|---\n   777|\n   778|### 11.1 Mechanism\n   779|\n   780|```\n   781|1. Re-read the prompt's `## Run metadata` block. Copy the path on the\n   782|   `output_file = '...'` line VERBATIM. Do NOT reconstruct it from runId,\n   783|   session name, project name, or any other source — the path the harness\n   784|   wrote there is the path the harness expects to read from.\n   785|\n   786|2. Re-read the prompt's `## Output parameters` table. For EVERY row in that\n   787|   table, include a key in the JSON object:\n   788|     - key   = the parameter name from the table\n   789|     - value = the resolved value the skill produced for that output\n   790|               (typically the path to the artifact folder or file)\n   791|\n   792|3. Write that JSON object to the `output_file` path. This is the FINAL file\n   793|   write of the run — strict ordering (see Section 11.4):\n   794|     - AFTER any skill-defined output verification (e.g. file-existence checks).\n   795|     - AFTER Memory Bank session-end writes (Section 4).\n   796|     - AFTER `_progress.json` flipped to `status: COMPLETED` (Section 2 LAST ACTION).\n   797|\n   798|4. Do NOT emit any tool call after the sidecar write. The next event in the\n   799|   transcript should be the `final_response` summary.\n   800|```\n   801|\n   802|---\n   803|\n   804|### 11.1.1 Path Correctness Rule (mandatory)\n   805|\n   806|**First, resolving the path you were given — before any write.** A path parameter can arrive\n   807|as a bare fragment (`prd`, `test-cases`, `domain-boundaries`) rather than a rooted path,\n   808|because the capability's `value_template` passed the operator's value through without\n   809|composing it. A fragment is **relative to this run's output directory**, never to your\n   810|working directory:\n   811|\n   812|```\n   813|GIVEN a path-typed input parameter P:\n   814|  IF P starts with \"/\" or \"./\" or \"../\"  -> use P as-is (already rooted)\n   815|  ELSE                                   -> P = <this run's output directory> + \"/\" + P\n   816|```\n   817|\n   818|Your run's output directory is normally the prompt's \"All the generated artifacts should be\n   819|placed in the output directory: ...\" line, or the `output_folder` parameter. `mkdir`-ing a\n   820|bare fragment creates a stray folder at the repository root, outside `artifacts/outputs/`,\n   821|where no downstream step or gate will look for it.\n   822|\n   823|**If the prompt gives you no rooted anchor, do not guess.** A capability whose instruction\n   824|renders that line from the same uncomposed parameter — `output directory: prd` — and which\n   825|omits `output_folder` from its parameter table has left you nothing to resolve against.\n   826|Silently falling back to the working directory is what produces the stray folder. Treat it as\n   827|a capability defect: proceed with the value you were given, and raise it as a blocking open\n   828|question naming the parameter and what you assumed, so the wiring gets fixed rather than the\n   829|symptom recurring every run.\n   830|\n   831|**Symptom.** Your outputs land next to the project's top-level folders instead of under\n   832|`artifacts/outputs/<capability>/`. Observed 2026-09-06 (`pli-20260906-qxlefw`): a playlist set\n   833|`prd_output_path: \"prd\"` — consistent with every other capability in that playlist, which\n   834|compose fragments in their `value_template` — the producing capability passed it through\n   835|unchanged, and the step created `<project>/prd/` while the prompt's stated output directory\n   836|was `<project>/artifacts/outputs/product-definition`. The verification gate then failed\n   837|against the folder nobody wrote to.\n   838|\n   839|Report the composed path per the rule below; the parameter you were handed is the input, not\n   840|the answer.\n   841|\n   842|---\n   843|\n   844|The value reported for any output **path** parameter MUST be the **actual on-disk path the skill wrote\n   845|to** — never a re-derived input parameter, never a fresh `value_template` substitution, and never a\n   846|scope-/sibling-adjusted path that was NOT the one actually used for the write.\n   847|\n   848|```\n   849|BEFORE writing the sidecar, for every path-typed output parameter:\n   850|  1. Confirm the artifact exists at the path you are about to report\n   851|     (e.g. `ls <PATH>/<EXPECTED-FILE>*` or a stat/existence check).\n   852|  2. If the path the skill actually wrote to differs from the input parameter\n   853|     for ANY reason — sibling-folder rule, scope adjustment, feature_id stripping,\n   854|     SESSION_ID derivation — report the ACTUAL written path, NOT the parameter.\n   855|  3. Reporting a parameter you did not write to is the documented cause of the\n   856|     duplicate-artifact-on-REPAIR defect: a later REPAIR run is handed the wrong\n   857|     folder, finds no prior artifact, and silently rebuilds a duplicate. (See §7.)\n   858|```\n   859|\n   860|**Worked example (sibling rule).** A planning step receives `planning_output_path =\n   861|/base/code-development/code-task-planning` but, because `feature_id` was empty and the upstream research\n   862|was scoped, Step 1 actually wrote to `/base/code-development/TICKET-001/code-task-planning/`. The sidecar\n   863|MUST report the latter (the path that holds the files), never the flat input parameter.\n   864|\n   865|> **Note for skill authors:** \"report the parameter VERBATIM\" guidance (used by some skills to avoid\n   866|> pre-resolution double-prefixing) means *do not re-prepend project/scope segments yourself* — it does\n   867|> NOT license reporting a path you did not write to. When your own logic diverges from the parameter,\n   868|> the actual written path always wins.\n   869|\n   870|---\n   871|\n   872|### 11.1.2 Verdict Fidelity Rule (mandatory)\n   873|\n   874|§11.1.1 governs path-typed outputs. This rule governs **closed-set outputs** — any output parameter\n   875|that declares an enumerated `values:` array, which in practice means every gate / verdict-gated-transition\n   876|(VGT) verdict the orchestrator routes on.\n   877|\n   878|The value reported for such a parameter MUST be **one of the declared tokens, copied character-for-character**.\n   879|\n   880|```\n   881|BEFORE writing the sidecar, for every output parameter with a declared `values:` set:\n   882|  1. Re-read the allowed tokens from the prompt's `## Output parameters` table\n   883|     (or, when the table omits them, from this skill's own verdict definition).\n   884|  2. Emit one of those tokens EXACTLY: same spelling, same case, same underscores.\n   885|     No synonym, no title-casing, no pluralisation, no added qualifier, no\n   886|     translation, no \"PASSED (with notes)\".\n   887|  3. Confirm the emitted token is a member of the declared set — a literal string\n   888|     comparison, not \"close enough\". If your computed verdict does not map onto a\n   889|     declared token, that is a bug in your mapping: pick the correct declared token,\n   890|     do not invent one.\n   891|  4. Confirm the token AGREES with the artifact you just wrote. When the report /\n   892|     spec carries the verdict in its own front-matter or summary, the two MUST be\n   893|     identical. A report that says PASSED and a sidecar that says something else is\n   894|     a self-contradiction, and the sidecar is what the orchestrator acts on.\n   895|```\n   896|\n   897|**Why this is not cosmetic.** The orchestrator matches the reported token against the step's\n   898|`gate_policy` accept/reject lists. A token in neither list is **unroutable**, so the engine\n   899|**fails closed and treats it as a reject** — the only safe default, since silently passing an\n   900|unrecognised verdict through a correctness gate would be worse. The consequence is a spurious\n   901|retry loop: the upstream producer step is re-run, every step between it and the gate is reset and\n   902|re-run with it, and the retry budget is consumed. When the budget is exhausted the run escalates\n   903|to a human gate carrying a rejection that never happened.\n   904|\n   905|**Worked example (observed).** A plan-verification gate declared\n   906|`values: [PASSED, PASSED_WITH_FINDINGS, FAILED]`. The skill wrote a report whose front-matter read\n   907|`verification_verdict: PASSED`, `weighted_total: 100`, `failed_blocker_count: 0` — a clean pass — but\n   908|emitted `\"verification_verdict\": \"APPROVED\"` in the sidecar. `APPROVED` is not in the set, so the gate\n   909|failed closed, rejected, and re-ran planning plus the two steps between. A perfect-scoring plan was\n   910|re-planned because the sidecar used a synonym.\n   911|\n   912|**Robustness note.** This write is the last action of a run, often after a long generation, and may be\n   913|performed under a degraded executor — a fallback model, a smaller tier after a quota or rate-limit\n   914|failure, or a resumed session. Those are exactly the conditions under which a token gets paraphrased.\n   915|Treat the closed-set comparison in step 3 as a real check to perform at write time, not a stylistic\n   916|preference — it is cheap, and it is the only thing standing between a correct verdict and a wasted lap.\n   917|\n   918|---\n   919|\n   920|### 11.2 Example\n   921|\n   922|For a skill that declares a single output parameter named `foo_output_path`:\n   923|\n   924|```json\n   925|{ \"foo_output_path\": \"/abs/path/to/artifacts/outputs/<capability>/<step>\" }\n   926|```\n   927|\n   928|For a skill with multiple output parameters, include every row from the `## Output parameters` table — one key per row. The skill-specific parameter names live in each SKILL.md (Section 11.5); this section does not enumerate them.\n   929|\n   930|---\n   931|\n   932|### 11.3 Violation\n   933|\n   934|Ending a Stepwise run with a `final_response` summary that lists the generated artifacts but no sidecar write breaks output propagation. The harness MAY have a `value_template` fallback for specific outputs, but that fallback is not guaranteed for every skill / every output. Treat the sidecar write as **non-optional** whenever the `## Run metadata` block is present in the prompt — do not rely on the fallback.\n   935|\n   936|Skills whose outputs lack a `value_template` fallback fail **silently** when the sidecar is missing: downstream steps see `outputs: {}` and propagate empty values; no error surfaces in the run. This is the failure mode this section prevents.\n   937|\n   938|---\n   939|\n   940|### 11.4 Composition with Section 2 LAST ACTION\n   941|\n   942|The sidecar write extends Section 2's LAST ACTION; it does NOT replace it. Strict ordering for any Stepwise-invoked skill:\n   943|\n   944|1. All skill-specific outputs written and verified.\n   945|2. Memory Bank writes (Section 4 — `active-context.md`, `progress.md`).\n   946|3. `_progress.json` flipped to `status: COMPLETED` (Section 2 LAST ACTION).\n   947|4. **Sidecar write** (this section) — final file write of the run.\n   948|5. `final_response` summary — no further tool calls.\n   949|\n   950|If the skill is invoked standalone (no `## Run metadata` block), step 4 is skipped. Steps 1–3 and 5 still apply.\n   951|\n   952|---\n   953|\n   954|### 11.5 Skill-Level Enforcement\n   955|\n   956|Section 11 cannot enumerate output parameter names — those are declared per-skill in the capability YAML's `outputs` block and surface in each prompt's `## Output parameters` table. Each SKILL.md MUST include a short inline callout naming its output parameters, so the agent knows which keys to put in the sidecar JSON without parsing the YAML.\n   957|\n   958|Recommended SKILL.md callout shape:\n   959|\n   960|> **Step N: Emit harness outputs sidecar — apply execution-protocol.md Section 11.**\n   961|>\n   962|> **Output parameters this skill produces** (one key per row of the prompt's `## Output parameters` table):\n   963|>\n   964|> - `<param_name_1>`: <one-line description of the resolved value, e.g. \"the resolved output folder path written in earlier steps\">\n   965|> - `<param_name_2>`: ...\n   966|>\n   967|> Example sidecar contents (illustrative):\n   968|>\n   969|> ```json\n   970|> { \"<param_name_1>\": \"/abs/path/...\", \"<param_name_2>\": \"...\" }\n   971|> ```\n   972|>\n   973|> **The sidecar ALSO carries the reserved `_open_questions` key — see §11.6.** It is not one\n   974|> of this skill's declared outputs — Stepwise itself renders it as a required row of the\n   975|> `## Output parameters` table — so it is not listed above; that is why the example shows\n   976|> only declared keys. Write it in every sidecar: every open question THIS run raised, `[]`\n   977|> when it raised nothing. Name the registries this skill writes (e.g. \"the standard\n   978|> `pending_inputs` / `traceability_gaps` / `assumptions_to_validate` tables\") and stop\n   979|> there — §11.6 owns the field set and the mapping.\n   980|\n   981|The callout is intentionally short. Trigger gate, mechanism, ordering, and violation rule all live in Section 11 — the SKILL.md only names the keys.\n   982|\n   983|**A worked example outranks a rule the reader has to recall.** A callout whose example shows\n   984|only declared parameters is read as the complete sidecar contract for that skill, and it will\n   985|beat §11.6 at the moment of writing — observed 2026-09-05, where a skill with a\n   986|fourteen-row open-questions registry emitted a one-key sidecar and the orchestrator had to\n   987|scrape the artifact instead. That is why the paragraph above belongs in the callout rather\n   988|than being left implicit.\n   989|\n   990|---\n   991|\n   992|\n   993|### 11.6 Open Questions in the sidecar (universal)\n   994|\n   995|**Trigger.** The prompt has a `## Run metadata` block (§11 trigger gate). That is the only condition: the key is written on EVERY sidecar, whether or not this run raised a question.\n   996|\n   997|**Rule.** Write the reserved `_open_questions` key in the same sidecar JSON as your declared outputs — ALWAYS. If this run wrote an `## Open Questions` / `## open_questions` section (any heading level, any spelling) into an artifact, mirror that section into it; if this run raised nothing, write `[]`. This is not extra analysis: you already decided what the open questions are and wrote them down. This emits the same list in a form the orchestrator can act on, and the empty array is how you tell it, unambiguously, that there was nothing to track.\n   998|\n   999|**Only what THIS run raised — never what the prompt handed you, and never what the project has already settled.** If a question appears in your prompt's `## OPEN QUESTIONS`, `## DECISIONS` or `## PROJECT DECISIONS` block, it is already tracked. The same holds for a decision you meet anywhere else you read — a value tagged `[OQ-xxxxxxxx]` in an upstream artifact, a row of `context-pack/decisions.md`, or the count in the `## PROJECT DECISIONS` pointer sentence (\"N further settled decisions are not repeated here\"): the block carries only what nothing else in your inputs holds yet, so most settled decisions reach you through the documents, not the block. A settled decision is not a question. Do not put it in your artifact's open-questions section, and do not declare it in `_open_questions` — not under your own numbering, not re-worded in your own voice, not \"for completeness\", and not as an assumption that reads \"…, per settled decision OQ-xxxxxxxx\" (observed 2026-09-09, `backlog-story-generation`: four such rows, each re-asking a decision it had already applied, each answered with the very value it cited). Where your work depends on one, cite its id in the sentence that proceeds on it:\n  1000|\n  1001|```\n  1002|- EPIC-01 asume el claim `smart.legajo` como fuente de identidad del analista (OQ-bd39279d).\n  1003|```\n  1004|\n  1005|`OQ-xxxxxxxx` (eight hex characters) is Stepwise's ledger id, and citing it is how an\n  1006|artifact shows which decision or open question a sentence rests on. It is a cross-reference,\n  1007|not one of the artifact's own ids: a verifier that checks \"every id uses an allowed prefix\"\n  1008|must treat `OQ-[0-9a-f]{8}` as always allowed, or it fails the exact behaviour this\n  1009|section requires (observed 2026-09-08: CHK-008 on a PRD flagged `OQ-3551076d` as a\n  1010|disallowed id and rejected a correctly cited document).\n  1011|\n  1012|**Why this is a hard rule and not a preference.** Identity is a hash of the question text, so a re-worded copy does not match the original — it opens a SECOND row that no automatic merge can close, and the operator has to recognise and fold it by hand. Measured on a live run: a step was handed 33 inherited questions and 17 decisions in its prompt, re-listed them in its own artifact under its own numbering, mirrored all of it into `_open_questions`, and turned 74 questions into 124. Thirty-seven were the same question asked twice.\n  1013|\n  1014|This applies to the SIDECAR as much as to the artifact section. The earlier form of this rule addressed only the prose, on the assumption that the sidecar mirrors the prose — so a skill that writes the inherited questions into its section complies with the mirror rule and breaks the ledger anyway. Both halves are the same rule: **the open-questions registry of an artifact, and its sidecar, describe what this step discovered.**\n  1015|\n  1016|`_open_questions` is a RESERVED row. Stepwise itself renders it into the `## Output parameters` table of every step prompt, marked required; it is never one of the skill's declared outputs, and Stepwise strips it before output resolution, so writing it never breaks propagation. An absent key is a contract violation that costs one repair turn — write `[]` rather than leaving it out. Stepwise validates entries at the boundary: a `question` without `assumption_if_unanswered` is accepted but flagged \"needs a basis\" and can never be bulk-accepted; an `answer_shape: enum` without `options` is downgraded to free text.\n  1017|\n  1018|```json\n  1019|{\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":7}}
