[08:25:27] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-874c72263ea3c410","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"1541\" start=\"1019\" end=\"1218\" returned=\"200\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n  1019|{\n  1020|  \"prd_output_path\": \"/abs/path/to/artifacts/outputs/product-definition/prd\",\n  1021|  \"_open_questions\": [\n  1022|    {\n  1023|      \"id\": \"PI-10\",\n  1024|      \"question\": \"Which inventory slice does the pilot run on?\",\n  1025|      \"subject\": \"scope\",\n  1026|      \"why_it_matters\": \"Every downstream sizing estimate assumes one slice\",\n  1027|      \"blocking\": true,\n  1028|      \"assumption_if_unanswered\": \"one DTC channel with real sell-out visibility\",\n  1029|      \"kind\": \"question\",\n  1030|      \"answer_shape\": \"enum\",\n  1031|      \"options\": [\n  1032|        { \"id\": \"dtc\", \"label\": \"One DTC channel with real sell-out visibility\" },\n  1033|        { \"id\": \"wholesale\", \"label\": \"The wholesale slice\" },\n  1034|        { \"id\": \"all\", \"label\": \"Every channel\" }\n  1035|      ],\n  1036|      \"audience\": \"client\",\n  1037|      \"artifact_ref\": \"artifacts/outputs/product-definition/PRD-SPEC-....md#open-questions\",\n  1038|      \"traces_to\": [\"EPIC-02\", \"FR-14\"]\n  1039|    }\n  1040|  ]\n  1041|}\n  1042|```\n  1043|\n  1044|When this run raised nothing:\n  1045|\n  1046|```json\n  1047|{ \"prd_output_path\": \"/abs/path/to/artifacts/outputs/product-definition/prd\", \"_open_questions\": [] }\n  1048|```\n  1049|\n  1050|**Fields.** `question`, `audience`, `artifact_ref`, `subject` and — on a `question` — `assumption_if_unanswered` are required. Emit every other field you actually have; omit what you do not. Never invent one — Zero-Invention applies here exactly as it does to the artifact.\n  1051|\n  1052|`artifact_ref` is required because the trigger already told you the answer: you are mirroring a section you just wrote INTO an artifact, so you know which artifact it is. There is nothing to look up and nothing to invent.\n  1053|\n  1054|- `id` — your own local id (`PI-10`, `OQ-03`) if the section has one.\n  1055|- `kind` — `question` (default) or `assumption`. See the note below; getting this right decides which verbs the operator is offered. **Every row of an `assumptions_to_validate` table is an `assumption` entry**, whether or not it carries a fallback and whether or not it is blocking: an assumption with a fallback is still a choice the work made on the operator's behalf, and one that stays only in the artifact is a decision nobody was offered. Observed 2026-09-08 (`researching-code-design`): the backend runtime was chosen in an `ASM-01` row, the sidecar said `[]`, and nothing reached the ledger — implementation would have proceeded on Node.js because one table was read as \"not questions\".\n  1056|- `subject` — **REQUIRED.** What the question is ABOUT (`scope`, `compliance`, `personas`, `observability`). It turns a 60-row inbox into a dozen themes — and it is also how a settled decision finds its way into the context pack: Stepwise routes a decision to the topic file whose `when_to_read` its subject overlaps. A decision with no subject can never be routed, so it is re-offered on every reconcile forever and never folded into the pack. Measured on one project: 30 of 126 decisions carried no subject and were permanently unharvestable, from four steps that emitted everything else correctly. Use the noun the registry row is already about; do not invent a taxonomy.\n  1057|- `why_it_matters` — the consequence of leaving it open.\n  1058|- `blocking` — `true` ONLY if downstream work genuinely cannot proceed. See the warning below.\n  1059|- `assumption_if_unanswered` — **REQUIRED on a `question`.** What you actually proceeded on. See **Say what you proceeded on** below. (Omit it on an `assumption`: the claim IS what the work proceeded on.)\n  1060|- `audience` — **REQUIRED.** Who OWNS the answer: `client`, `architect`, `product_owner`, `internal`. See **Every question names an owner** below.\n  1061|- `answer_shape` — `free_text` (default), `enum`, `path`, `number`, `boolean`. Prefer `enum` with 2–5 `options` whenever the answer is a choice: it gives the operator one-click buttons instead of a text box.\n  1062|- `options` — required when `answer_shape` is `enum`: an array of `{ \"id\", \"label\" }` objects (or plain strings). Stepwise downgrades an `enum` without options to free text, so an enum with no options buys nothing.\n  1063|\n  1064|  **Options carry the trade-off, not just the choice.** A label naming only the option (\"Standard\" / \"High-Performance\") makes the operator go read the artifact to find out what either one costs; a label naming the concrete consequence lets them decide from the button alone. Compare \"Standard\" against \"Standard (< 2s response, lower infra cost)\" — same choice, only one is answerable without leaving the inbox. Two further rules apply to every `enum` row: options MUST be mutually exclusive (an operator who could reasonably pick two is being offered one question disguised as two) and MUST cover the reasonable range of answers a downstream artifact would actually be built differently against — not padded with an option nobody would choose to reach the 2–5 count.\n  1065|- `artifact_ref` — **REQUIRED. Project-relative** path (optionally `#anchor`) of the artifact that raised it, with `/` separators — `artifacts/outputs/product-definition/PRD-SPEC-20260812.md#open-questions`. NOT an absolute path: the ledger is committed to the project repo and opened on other people's machines, and an absolute path from yours is unusable on theirs. This field is what the operator's \"Edit the artifact\" button resolves, and on one measured project 997 references named a machine nobody else had (Stepwise issue #1047). Same rule the harness applies to every path it persists.\n  1066|\n  1067|  **Omitting it is not a smaller sidecar, it is an unrepairable question.** The orchestrator computes which documents an answer invalidated from these refs; a question with none contributes no document, so when the operator answers it there is nothing to point a repair at and no \"Edit the artifact\" to offer. Measured on one run: `prd-creation` emitted 75 refs and every one of its questions was repairable, while `backlog-epic-generation` (58) and `discovering-bounded-contexts` (13) emitted none at all — 71 questions that could be asked and answered but never applied. Emit it on every row.\n  1068|- `traces_to` — ids the question affects (`EPIC-02`, `FR-14`, `BC-09`).\n  1069|\n  1070|**Enumerate the registries from YOUR artifact, never from a remembered list.** Agent-native\n  1071|spec skills put several tables under `open_questions`, and the set differs per skill:\n  1072|`pending_inputs`, `coverage_gaps`, `alignment_gaps`, `traceability_gaps`, `missing_inputs`,\n  1073|`assumptions_to_validate`. Before\n  1074|building the sidecar, **read the `###` headings under your own `open_questions` section and\n  1075|mirror every row of every one of them** (except `summary`, which is counts, not questions).\n  1076|A registry you did not mirror is a partial list, and §11.6's completeness rule treats a\n  1077|partial list as \"the rest were resolved\".\n  1078|\n  1079|Observed 2026-09-06 (`backlog-epic-generation`, `pli-20260905-1jcnazb`): a skill was told its\n  1080|registries were `pending_inputs` / `traceability_gaps` / `assumptions_to_validate`, mirrored\n  1081|exactly those, and silently dropped `AG-01` from the `alignment_gaps` table it also had. The\n  1082|orchestrator recovered it by scraping the markdown, where it landed as a row that cannot hold\n  1083|a step. Naming a list is a hint; the artifact on disk is the authority.\n  1084|\n  1085|**Carried-forward registries are retired** (`prd_gaps_carried_forward`,\n  1086|`upstream_gaps_carried_forward` and any `*_gaps_carried_forward` table). The prompt's\n  1087|`## OPEN QUESTIONS` / `## PROJECT DECISIONS` blocks ARE the carry-forward: Stepwise delivers\n  1088|every inherited question and every settled decision there, with its id. Never rebuild one in\n  1089|the artifact and never mirror one into the sidecar; where your work relies on an inherited\n  1090|item, cite its id in the body instead.\n  1091|\n  1092|The columns below are the shape used by `pending_inputs` and its close relatives. **Do not\n  1093|assume any other registry has them** — gap tables frequently do not, and\n  1094|a missing column is never a licence to emit `null`. Map what your table actually has:\n  1095|\n  1096|| sidecar field | registry column |\n  1097||---|---|\n  1098|| `id` | `id` (`PI-XX`, `TG-XX`, `ASM-XX`) |\n  1099|| `question` | `question` on PI/TG rows; the `assumption` statement on ASM rows |\n  1100|| `kind` | `question` for `pending_inputs` and `traceability_gaps`; `assumption` for `assumptions_to_validate` |\n  1101|| `subject` | `section` on PI rows; `traceability` for TG rows; for ASM rows the section the assumption belongs to |\n  1102|| `why_it_matters` | the CONSEQUENCE, in a sentence — what breaks or degrades, named against the `field` / `item_id` it holds up; `impact_if_wrong` on ASM rows. **The bare impact level is not an answer:** `HIGH` is a rejected value, `HIGH — FR-05 cannot be enabled in production until the window is fixed` is the field. Restate the row; do not invent a new consequence |\n  1103|| `blocking` | **re-decided, never copied** — see below. The registry column and this field mean different things |\n  1104|| `assumption_if_unanswered` | `fallback_behavior`, subject to the BLOCKED rule below. Omit on `kind: assumption` |\n  1105|| `traces_to` | `affected_ids` |\n  1106|| `artifact_ref` | the artifact's project-relative path + `#open-questions` |\n  1107|| `audience` | not a column — decide it per row, see **Every question names an owner** |\n  1108|| `inherited_from` | on a repair/iterate lap, the ids from the prompt's `## OPEN QUESTIONS` / `## DECISIONS` / `## PROJECT DECISIONS` blocks this row restates |\n  1109|\n  1110|**The `BLOCKED` collision.** These templates conventionally set\n  1111|`fallback_behavior: BLOCKED` on every `blocking: yes` row, and the rule below forbids refusal\n  1112|tokens in `assumption_if_unanswered`. So where `fallback_behavior` is `BLOCKED`, do NOT copy\n  1113|it — and do NOT reach for a generic stand-in either. Name the narrowest thing THIS artifact\n  1114|actually did about THIS item, in terms of its own ids:\n  1115|\n  1116|> `FR-01 is emitted with status: pending and no acceptance criterion for legajo extraction`\n  1117|> `the existing endpoint contract is preserved unchanged; no new field was added`\n  1118|> `NFR-04 keeps the source's 19:00-06:00 value and the 23:00 variant is not modelled`\n  1119|\n  1120|Then set `blocking: true` with the reason in `why_it_matters`. A run that shipped an artifact\n  1121|proceeded on something; name it specifically.\n  1122|\n  1123|**One sentence per row — reused text is a defect.** If two rows in the same sidecar share an\n  1124|`assumption_if_unanswered` string, at least one of them is wrong: two different unknowns\n  1125|cannot have been proceeded on in the same way. Before emitting, check that the values are\n  1126|distinct and that each names its own ids. A row is finished when a reader who has not seen\n  1127|the artifact could tell, from that field alone, what the document says about that item today.\n  1128|\n  1129|Observed 2026-09-05 (`product-discovery__pli-20260905-1jcnazb__pd-001`): ten of eleven\n  1130|blocking rows carried the identical sentence \"the field is left pending and no value was\n  1131|assumed\" — an earlier revision of this section offered it as an example and it was copied\n  1132|verbatim. That is not a cosmetic problem. When the operator bulk-accepts a row, the ledger\n  1133|records `assumption_if_unanswered` AS THE ANSWER (`answered_by: operator`,\n  1134|`note: \"Bulk-accepted by the operator\"`). Ten questions were therefore settled with an\n  1135|answer of record that says nothing, and no future answer can be compared against them.\n  1136|\n  1137|**Registries without those columns — derive, do not blank.** Gap tables\n  1138|(`coverage_gaps`, `alignment_gaps` and similar) typically carry only something like `id | type | ref_id | description |\n  1139|affected_section` or `id | origin | description | impact_on_boundaries`. Every sidecar field\n  1140|is still derivable, and every one is still required:\n  1141|\n  1142|| sidecar field | where it comes from when the standard column is absent |\n  1143||---|---|\n  1144|| `id` | the table's OWN id column — `GAP-01`, `AG-01`, `ASM-01`. Never drop it: an entry with no id cannot be referenced, folded, or answered against |\n  1145|| `question` | the `description` / `assumption` text. A statement of absence (\"hosting platform is not identified\") is a valid question — keep the wording, do not invent a new one |\n  1146|| `kind` | `assumption` for any assumptions table, `question` for everything else |\n  1147|| `subject` | `affected_section`, `section`, or the registry's own name |\n  1148|| `why_it_matters` | `impact_on_boundaries`, `impact_if_wrong`, `rationale`, or the consequence implied by `type` (e.g. `deferred_by_v3.0_scope` → what is therefore unplanned) |\n  1149|| `traces_to` | `ref_id`, `affected_ids`, or the ids named in the description |\n  1150|| `blocking` | decided per the rule below; these rows are usually `false` |\n  1151|| `assumption_if_unanswered` | **SYNTHESIZE it.** These tables have no `fallback_behavior` column, and the field is still required on every `question`. State what the artifact did in its absence — \"no hosting provider is assigned to any service\", \"the dependency is recorded with owner unassigned\". Omitting it is the one thing you may not do |\n  1152|| `artifact_ref` | your own artifact's project-relative path + `#open-questions` |\n  1153|\n  1154|**An assumption already confirmed is a decision, not an open question.** If an\n  1155|`assumptions_to_validate` row carries `confidence: confirmed`, `validation_needed: none`, or\n  1156|a rationale saying the point is settled, it does not belong in `_open_questions` — it will\n  1157|sit in the operator's inbox asking them to decide something the artifact says is already\n  1158|decided. Emit only assumptions that genuinely still need validating.\n  1159|\n  1160|Observed 2026-09-05 (`discovering-bounded-contexts`, `pli-20260905-1jcnazb`): all five\n  1161|registries were correctly mirrored, and the five rows from the four non-`pending_inputs`\n  1162|tables arrived with `id`, `kind`, `subject`, `why_it_matters`, `traces_to` and `artifact_ref`\n  1163|all null — `GAP-01`, `AG-01`, `UG-01`, `UG-02` lost their ids entirely, two rows carried no\n  1164|`assumption_if_unanswered` at all, two more shared one string, and a `confidence: confirmed`\n  1165|assumption was raised as an open question. Mirroring the table is half the job; mapping its\n  1166|columns is the other half.\n  1167|\n  1168|**A mostly-blocking sidecar means you copied the column.** In the registry table\n  1169|`blocking: yes` means \"this document is not complete\"; in the sidecar it means \"downstream\n  1170|steps must not run,\" which stops a playlist and needs an operator to confirm. Those diverge\n  1171|in practice: most rows whose `fallback_behavior` names a real behavior the artifact proceeded\n  1172|on are `blocking: false` here, however the table marks them. So treat a high blocking rate as\n  1173|a self-check, not a result — if most of your rows come out `true`, re-read the column\n  1174|semantics rather than shipping it. Observed 2026-09-05: eleven of fourteen rows arrived\n  1175|`blocking: true`, `blocking_asserted_by: sidecar`, and every one was waived by the operator\n  1176|in a single bulk action.\n  1177|\n  1178|**`audience` is decided per row, not defaulted per artifact.** The test is who is ENTITLED to\n  1179|decide, and for a technical artifact that is frequently NOT the client. A tooling choice\n  1180|(\"Flyway or Liquibase for the versioned DDL?\"), a policy the delivery team owns (\"does\n  1181|architecture accept the absence of backend authorization?\"), a contract shape your own\n  1182|downstream architecture step will settle — those are `architect`. Scope, priority and\n  1183|what \"done\" means are `product_owner`. `client` is for facts about their business, their\n  1184|data, their policy or their commercial choice; it is the only value that sends a question out\n  1185|of the building, and over-using it stalls questions your own next step could answer. A useful\n  1186|check: if the question is one your own architecture or delivery capability will have to\n  1187|answer anyway, it was never `client`.\n  1188|\n  1189|A skill whose registries differ from this shape names its own mapping in its §11.5 callout.\n  1190|A skill whose registries match it needs no instruction beyond naming them.\n  1191|- `inherited_from` — the ids of questions this one restates, copied verbatim from the `## OPEN QUESTIONS` / `## DECISIONS` / `## PROJECT DECISIONS` blocks (`[OQ-1d0efb0c]` → `\"OQ-1d0efb0c\"`). This is what folds a restatement onto the row already tracking it instead of opening a second one. See **Your `## Open Questions` section lists what THIS run raised** below.\n  1192|\n  1193|**`kind` separates a gap from a claim.** A `question` names something UNKNOWN — the operator resolves it by supplying the value, or by accepting the assumption you proceeded on. An `assumption` names something you ASSERTED and could not verify (\"Theobald is compatible with the SAP S/4HANA landscape\", \"Fabric is licensable in Germany West Central\") — the operator resolves it by confirming it, which changes nothing because the artifact already says it, or by correcting it, which invalidates everything built on it. Emit `assumption` for anything you would have written into an `assumptions_to_validate` table. A question phrased as a statement of absence (\"No client-approved RPO exists\") is still a `question`: it needs a value supplied, not a claim confirmed. For an `assumption` you may omit `assumption_if_unanswered` — the claim IS what the work proceeded on.\n  1194|\n  1195|**`blocking` means the SCHEDULE, not the document.** Registry tables use `blocking: yes` to mean \"this document cannot be considered complete.\" Here it means \"downstream steps must not run until this is answered,\" and an operator has to confirm before it stops anything. If work can proceed on a stated assumption, it is `false` with an `assumption_if_unanswered` — that is the normal case.\n  1196|\n  1197|**Completeness.** The sidecar is complete by contract: list EVERY open question this run is raising, or write `[]`. Do not emit a partial list — the orchestrator reads an unlisted question as one you resolved and stops tracking it — and do not omit the key: an absent key is not \"nothing raised\", it is a missing required output.\n  1198|\n  1199|**Say what you proceeded on.** `assumption_if_unanswered` is required on every\n  1200|`question`, and it is the field that decides what answering COSTS.\n  1201|\n  1202|With it, an answer that agrees with what you assumed settles the question and\n  1203|touches nothing. Without it, there is no basis to compare an answer against, so\n  1204|EVERY answer must be treated as invalidating — and each one puts every artifact\n  1205|built on the gap into the operator's repair queue, including the answers that\n  1206|merely confirm what the document already said.\n  1207|\n  1208|Field evidence (2026-08-27): eleven open rows on one ledger, none with an\n  1209|assumption. Five of them re-asked something an earlier lap had already raised\n  1210|WITH an assumption and the operator had already settled. One — a tie-break rule\n  1211|for identical values — existed five times, had been answered twice by the\n  1212|client, and came back a fifth time with the assumption dropped, so the\n  1213|established answer could not be matched against it.\n  1214|\n  1215|If you genuinely could not proceed, that is not a reason to omit the field. Say\n  1216|what you did instead — \"the row is emitted with the field absent and flagged\" —\n  1217|because that IS what you proceeded on. Do not write a refusal token (`BLOCKED`,\n  1218|`TBD`, `N/A`, `unknown`) into it: that says you stopped, not what you assumed,\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":4}}
