[08:25:44] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-9a4f5f7bf9753f6a","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\artifacts\\transcripts\\discovery-transcript.md\" lines=\"189\" start=\"1\" end=\"189\" returned=\"189\" has_more=\"false\" truncated=\"false\">\n     1|## **Discovery Conversation Transcript — Calc-Service**\n     2|\n     3|**Time:** 15 min\n     4|**Topic:** Math Operations Microservice Requirements\n     5|\n     6|---\n     7|\n     8|### **00:00 — Context Setting**\n     9|\n    10|**A:** Thanks for the time. I want to understand why you need this math service instead of embedding calculations inside the main app.\n    11|\n    12|**PO:** We’ve got calculations spread everywhere — frontend, backend, even reports. When formulas change, it breaks things in different places. We want a single place that does math consistently.\n    13|\n    14|**A:** So consistency is the main driver?\n    15|\n    16|**PO:** Consistency and control. Also auditability for some calculations.\n    17|\n    18|---\n    19|\n    20|### **01:30 — Scope of Calculations**\n    21|\n    22|**A:** What types of calculations are we talking about?\n    23|\n    24|**PO:** Basic arithmetic first — add, subtract, multiply, divide. That covers most cases.\n    25|\n    26|**A:** And advanced?\n    27|\n    28|**PO:** Yes, powers, square roots, some trigonometry. Nothing too exotic, but it grows over time.\n    29|\n    30|**A:** Should the service be extensible?\n    31|\n    32|**PO:** Definitely. New operations will be added without breaking existing consumers.\n    33|\n    34|---\n    35|\n    36|### **03:00 — Precision & Numerical Behavior**\n    37|\n    38|**A:** Do you have precision requirements?\n    39|\n    40|**PO:** Yes. Some teams complained about rounding differences between systems.\n    41|\n    42|**A:** Financial sensitivity?\n    43|\n    44|**PO:** Not strictly accounting, but precision matters. We don’t want floating-point surprises.\n    45|\n    46|**A:** So deterministic outputs for the same input.\n    47|\n    48|**PO:** Exactly.\n    49|\n    50|---\n    51|\n    52|### **04:30 — History Requirement**\n    53|\n    54|**A:** You mentioned auditability. Do you need every calculation stored?\n    55|\n    56|**PO:** Not necessarily every one. But we want the ability to log calculations when needed — mainly debugging and traceability.\n    57|\n    58|**A:** So history is optional.\n    59|\n    60|**PO:** Yes. It shouldn’t slow down the calculation.\n    61|\n    62|**A:** If persistence fails, should the calculation still return?\n    63|\n    64|**PO:** Yes, always.\n    65|\n    66|---\n    67|\n    68|### **06:00 — Consumers**\n    69|\n    70|**A:** Who will call this service?\n    71|\n    72|**PO:** Backend services first. Later automation workflows. Possibly AI agents.\n    73|\n    74|**A:** Real-time usage?\n    75|\n    76|**PO:** Mostly synchronous, yes.\n    77|\n    78|---\n    79|\n    80|### **07:30 — API Behavior**\n    81|\n    82|**A:** How should the API look from your perspective?\n    83|\n    84|**PO:** Simple. Send operands and operation, get result. No complex payloads.\n    85|\n    86|**A:** Batch calculations?\n    87|\n    88|**PO:** Maybe later, not day one.\n    89|\n    90|**A:** Expression parsing? Like full formulas?\n    91|\n    92|**PO:** That’s interesting… not required now, but we expect it eventually.\n    93|\n    94|---\n    95|\n    96|### **09:00 — Performance Expectations**\n    97|\n    98|**A:** Any latency expectations?\n    99|\n   100|**PO:** It should feel instant. This isn’t heavy computation.\n   101|\n   102|**A:** Throughput spikes?\n   103|\n   104|**PO:** Possibly if automation kicks in, but calculations are lightweight.\n   105|\n   106|---\n   107|\n   108|### **10:00 — Error Handling**\n   109|\n   110|**A:** What should happen on invalid input?\n   111|\n   112|**PO:** Clear errors. Division by zero, unsupported operation — that kind of thing.\n   113|\n   114|**A:** Strict validation?\n   115|\n   116|**PO:** Yes. Fail fast.\n   117|\n   118|---\n   119|\n   120|### **11:00 — Operational Concerns**\n   121|\n   122|**A:** Do you need persistence durability?\n   123|\n   124|**PO:** Not initially. History is more diagnostic than regulatory.\n   125|\n   126|**A:** Multi-instance consistency?\n   127|\n   128|**PO:** Not required now.\n   129|\n   130|**A:** So persistence is auxiliary.\n   131|\n   132|**PO:** Correct.\n   133|\n   134|---\n   135|\n   136|### **12:00 — Evolution Expectations**\n   137|\n   138|**A:** How do you see this evolving?\n   139|\n   140|**PO:** It becomes the standard math engine. Then maybe reusable components for other services.\n   141|\n   142|**A:** Any orchestration role?\n   143|\n   144|**PO:** Potentially as part of automated workflows — like validating outputs inside pipelines.\n   145|\n   146|---\n   147|\n   148|### **13:30 — Risks & Constraints**\n   149|\n   150|**A:** Biggest risks?\n   151|\n   152|**PO:** Hidden logic differences between systems. Also people bypassing the service.\n   153|\n   154|**A:** Adoption challenge.\n   155|\n   156|**PO:** Yes — it must be simple or teams won’t use it.\n   157|\n   158|---\n   159|\n   160|### **14:30 — Success Criteria**\n   161|\n   162|**A:** How do you know this project succeeded?\n   163|\n   164|**PO:**\n   165|\n   166|* Teams stop duplicating math logic\n   167|* Results become consistent\n   168|* We can trace calculations when something looks wrong\n   169|\n   170|**A:** Anything else?\n   171|\n   172|**PO:** It becomes boring infrastructure — that’s success.\n   173|\n   174|---\n   175|\n   176|### **15:00 — Closing**\n   177|\n   178|**A:** Let me summarize:\n   179|\n   180|* Central math engine\n   181|* Deterministic precision\n   182|* Optional history\n   183|* Extensible operations\n   184|* Simple API\n   185|* Persistence must never block calculation\n   186|\n   187|Did I miss anything?\n   188|\n   189|**PO:** No — that’s exactly it.\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":4}}
