[08:27:08] {"type":"thinking_delta","payload":{"delta":"Template fully loaded. Now execute the workflow.\n\n**Step 1: Initialize.** MODE = SYNTHESIZE (no failure_feedback). SPEC_FOLDER = `./artifacts/outputs/product-definition/prd` (already rooted with `./`). SESSION_ID: the stale tracker says `pd-20261027-calc001`. §1 Session Identity isn't routed for this skill (only code-development family). The stale tracker's session_id is a reasonable reuse — but is it? The tracker exists from a prior run that never wrote the skeleton. Options: reuse `pd-20261027-calc001` (consistent with the existing tracker) or mint a new one. Reusing keeps continuity with the existing tracker and avoids duplicate artifacts. I'll reuse `pd-20261027-calc001` and update the tracker's started_at to a real clock read.\n\nActually — the tracker's started_at is 2026-10-27T19:20:00Z (yesterday). Since this is a fresh SYNTHESIZE run (skeleton never written), I'll update started_at to now. Today is 2026-10-28.\n\n**FIRST ACTION:** update `_progress.json` with status RUNNING, real started_at. Also §10.5.2 Rule 4: glob for stray `.bak` files older than 1 hour — none exist (folder only has _progress.json).\n\nPath budget: longest path = `C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\artifacts\\outputs\\product-definition\\prd\\PRD-SPEC-pd-20261027-calc001.md` ≈ 40 + 30 + 30 ≈ well under 240. Fine.\n\n**Step 1.5: Input Validation Gate.** Project brief has problem statement, target users (backend services, reporting pipelines, automation workflows, AI agents), 2+ features (standard ops, advanced ops, history). Minimum scope for 3+ FRs: yes. PASS.\n\n**Step 2: Input Synthesis.**\n- Sources: project-brief.md (read), discovery-transcript.md (read). prd_template_path: not provided (default `prd_template.md` — doesn't exist; the skill's own references/spec-template.md governs). legacy_docs_path: absent → greenfield. design_plan_report_path: default path doesn't exist → treated as absent.\n- Engagement mode: scan brief for brownfield triggers. Brief says \"externalizes calculation logic from client applications\", \"future automation\" — no \"current system\"/\"legacy\"/\"modernization\" phrases. Greenfield. Log it.\n- No meetings recordings (folder empty), no transcripts folder beyond the one transcript (transcript_output_path default \"transcripts\" — the discovery transcript exists at artifacts/transcripts/). Use it.\n- PLAN_PERSONAS/PLAN_JOBS empty → no reconciliation pass.\n- DETECTED_LANGUAGE: English.\n\n**Step 2.5: Source Extraction Pass.** Trigger: 2 source files, each < 200 lines (144 + 189), cumulative 333 lines > 500? No — 333 < 500. Any single file > 200? No. More than 2 files? No (2 files). So per §10.2 thresholds: SKIP extraction, read sources directly in Phase B. But the skill's extraction→section map references `_extractions/` files... §10.2 says \"If none of the above (small bounded input), SKIP — read sources directly in Phase B.\" So extraction is skipped; Phase B reads the raw sources (brief + transcript) directly. That's allowed since they're small. I'll keep both in context (they're already read) and not create `_extractions/`.\n\nHmm, but §10.5 says \"NEVER re-read raw sources during Phase B. Read `_extractions/*.md` files, or SPEC_FILE...\" — when extraction is skipped, sources are read directly in Phase B per §10.2. Both files are already fully in my context from the pre-flight reads, so no re-reads needed anyway.\n\n**Step 3: Generate spec.** Phase A skeleton (Write), then Phase B one section per Edit.\n\nLet me now plan the content carefully (Zero-Invention, from brief + transcript only).\n\n**project_info:**\n- client_name: not stated in inputs (brief doesn't name a client/org).\n- project_name: \"Math Operations Microservice (Calc-Service)\" — from brief H1. Sanitize: strip `#|`` and newlines → \"Math Operations Microservice (Calc-Service)\". Parentheses fine. Length fine.\n- project_description: 1-2 sentences from brief overview: \"A lightweight, stateless microservice providing a centralized mathematical computation capability for the platform, externalizing calculation logic from client applications to enable consistency, reuse, and controlled evolution of numerical behavior.\" Good.\n\n**scope:**\nin_scope (from brief §2 + transcript):\n- Standard operations: addition, subtraction, multiplication, division | source: project-brief §2 Core Capabilities; transcript §01:30 | complete\n- Advanced operations: exponentiation, roots, trigonometric functions | source: project-brief §2; transcript §01:30 | complete\n- High-precision deterministic computation | source: project-brief §2 Precision Strategy; transcript §03:00 | complete\n- Optional history tracking (lightweight local persistence, non-blocking) | source: project-brief §2 History Tracking; transcript §04:30 | complete\n- Calculate API endpoint POST /api/v1/math/calculate | source: project-brief §4 | complete\n- History retrieval for diagnostics/auditing | source: project-brief §4 History Retrieval | complete\n- Extensibility: new operations added without breaking existing consumers | source: transcript §01:30 | complete\n- Strict input validation with clear errors (division by zero, unsupported operation) | source: transcript §10:00 | complete\n- Stateless, independently deployable, horizontally scalable | source: project-brief §3 | complete\n\nout_of_scope:\n- Batch calculations (day one) | source: transcript §07:30 (\"Maybe later, not day one\") | complete\n- Expression evaluation (e.g., \"5 + (3 * 2)\") — planned future capability, not required now | source: project-brief §2 Future Capability; transcript §07:30 | complete\n- Workflow integration with automation agents | source: project-brief §2 Future Capability | complete\n- Event-based history instead of database persistence | source: project-brief §2 Future Capability | complete\n- Multi-instance history consistency | source: transcript §11:00 (\"Not required now\") | complete\n- Persistence durability (initially) | source: transcript §11:00 | complete\n\nresearch_methodology:\n- Derived from project brief v1.1 and one 15-minute discovery transcript with the product owner; no primary user interviews, no meeting recordings, no legacy documentation provided | source: project-brief; discovery-transcript | complete\n\n**personas:**\nFrom brief §3 consumers + transcript §06:00:\n- P-01: Backend Service Developer (primary) — backend services needing consistent math logic. Transcript: \"Backend services first.\" Goals: consistent numerical results without duplicating logic. Pain points: calculations spread everywhere; rounding differences between systems (transcript §00:00, §03:00). Technical proficiency: high (assumption ASM). Usage frequency: mostly synchronous, frequent (transcript §06:00 \"Mostly synchronous, yes\"). Demographics: role/seniority not stated; technical proficiency high [ASM]; organizational context not stated → assumption ASM-XX.\n- P-02: Automation/AI Workflow Integrator (secondary) — automation workflows and AI agents performing deterministic calculations. Goals: deterministic calculations inside pipelines/workflows. Pain points: hidden logic differences. Usage: possibly later (\"Later automation workflows. Possibly AI agents\").\n- P-03: Reporting Pipeline Developer? Brief lists \"Reporting pipelines\" as consumer. Could be folded into P-01/P-02. I'll make it a third persona: Reporting Pipeline Developer (secondary).\n\nPrimary: P-01 with rationale (transcript: \"Backend services first\") — that's source-backed, status complete.\n\n**functional_requirements:**\nFrom brief + transcript. Atomicity: one testable behavior each.\n\n- FR-01: Standard arithmetic operations (ADD, SUBTRACT, MULTIPLY, DIVIDE) via calculate endpoint. priority: must_have (transcript: \"Basic arithmetic first... covers most cases\" — that's evidence for must_have; status complete? Priority derivation: transcript says basic arithmetic first → must_have, source-backed). ACs: given operands 10.5 and 5.0 with operation ADD → result 15.5 (from brief example); given division by zero → clear error (transcript). depends_on: none.\n- FR-02: Advanced operations: exponentiation, roots, trigonometric functions. priority: should_have (transcript: \"Yes, powers, square roots, some trigonometry\" after \"basic first\" → should_have; that's inference from ordering — mark assumption? The transcript ordering \"Basic arithmetic first\" implies advanced comes after → should_have is a reasonable inference; I'll mark priority as should_have with status complete since the sequencing is stated (\"first\" vs \"yes, also\"). Hmm, Zero-Invention: the transcript says basic first, advanced also needed. Priority must_have for basic is directly evidenced; should_have for advanced is a mild inference — I'll mark it as an assumption ASM-XX. Actually simpler: FR-02 priority should_have | status: assumption [ASM-01]. Let me keep ASM numbering tidy.\n- FR-03: Deterministic high-precision computation: same input always produces same result; high-precision internal computation to reduce rounding inconsistencies. priority: must_have (brief §5 Determinism; transcript §03:00 \"Exactly\"). ACs: given same input repeated → identical result.\n- FR-04: Calculate API contract: POST /api/v1/math/calculate accepting operand1, operand2, operation; returning resultValue, operationType, timestamp. priority: must_have (brief §4). ACs from brief examples.\n- FR-05: Strict input validation with clear, fast-failing errors for invalid input (division by zero, unsupported operation). priority: must_have (transcript §10:00 \"Yes. Fail fast.\"). ACs.\n- FR-06: Optional calculation history recording: lightweight local persistence of calculation metadata for traceability/diagnostics; must not impact response delivery. priority: should_have (brief \"Optional\"; transcript \"when needed\"). ACs: given history enabled → metadata recorded; given persistence fails → calculation still returns (transcript §04:30 \"Yes, always\"; brief §5 Reliability).\n- FR-07: History retrieval endpoint providing recent calculation records for diagnostics and auditing. priority: could_have? Brief §4 \"History Retrieval (Optional)\". Transcript doesn't detail. I'd say should_have/could_have — brief marks optional → could_have [ASM]. Hmm. Brief: \"Provides recent calculation records for diagnostics and auditing.\" Optional. I'll set could_have with assumption tag.\n- FR-08: Extensibility: support adding new operations without breaking existing consumers (API contract stability). priority: must_have? Transcript: \"Definitely. New operations will be added without breaking existing consumers.\" That's strong → must_have. ACs: given a new operation added → existing consumers' contract unchanged.\n- FR-09: Stateless deployment model: no user session ownership, independent deployment, horizontal scalability. Hmm — is this FR or NFR? Stateless design is architectural positioning; horizontal scaling is NFR (scalability). \"No user session ownership\" is a design constraint. I'd put horizontal scalability as NFR (scalability category) and statelessness as a constraint/design note rather than FR. Brief §3 is \"Architectural Positioning\". I'll cover scalability in NFR and put statelessness in constraints (technical) — actually constraints sub-categories mandatory are budget/timeline/resource/regulatory; organizational/technical additive. I'll add a technical constraint entry for statelessness.\n\nWait — also persistence replaceability: \"persistence can be replaced without changing the API contract\" (brief §3). That's a design constraint → could be FR? It's about the system's behavior: persistence layer replaceable without API change. It's more architectural → design_note/constraint. I'll put it as a technical constraint with source.\n\nFR count: 7 FRs (FR-01..FR-07). Reasonable for this brief.\n\n**NFRs** (mandatory sub-categories: performance, scalability, availability, usability/accessibility, security/compliance):\n- NFR-01 performance: millisecond-level response time for standard operations (brief §5). Target: numeric? \"Millisecond-level\" — not a precise number. Target: < 100 ms? That would be invention. Brief says \"Millisecond-level response time\". Transcript: \"It should feel instant.\" I need numeric target. Options: status pending with PI row (decision-bearing: the latency target the product commits to). Per KFM-002, prefer enum with trade-off options for client. I'll set NFR-01 target: [PENDING] with PI row, fallback: \"Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: standard operations complete within 100 ms at p95 (industry-standard for lightweight computation services)\". Hmm — but is that decision-bearing? Yes: NFR target the product commits to. But wait — brief says \"millisecond-level\" which IS a stated target, just not precise. I could record target: \"millisecond-level (exact threshold not stated in inputs)\" with status pending and one PI asking for the precise p95 threshold. That's one decision-bearing gap. Good.\n- NFR-02 determinism/reliability: calculation must succeed even if history persistence fails (brief §5 Reliability). Category: availability/reliability. Target: 100% of calculations return result regardless of persistence outcome — that's measurable and source-backed. status complete.\n- NFR-03 scalability: stateless design allows horizontal scaling (brief §5). Target numeric? Not stated. Sub-category mandatory. Target: [PENDING]? Or assumption: \"scales horizontally to N instances with no shared state\" — no number in source. Per rules: NFR targets must be numeric or pending. No source number → status pending + PI row? Or assumption with ASM (inferred industry standard). The brief states the design allows horizontal scaling but no capacity target. Downstream impact: capacity planning. I'd mark pending with a PI row (one consolidated question on capacity/concurrency target). Actually KFM-001 warns against template-field questions. Is a scalability target decision-bearing? Yes — architecture would size instances differently. One PI row covering scalability target. OK.\n- NFR-04 availability/uptime SLA: no source evidence. Mandatory sub-category. status pending + PI row with fallback baseline (99.9% industry standard). Per skill: \"If a sub-category has no source evidence, set status: pending and register an open question\". So PI row.\n- NFR-05 usability/accessibility: this is an API-only service; usability = API simplicity (\"Simple. Send operands and operation, get result. No complex payloads.\" transcript §07:30). WCAG doesn't apply to a headless API. I'll frame: API usability — simple request/response contract; measurable proxy? Target: single-request operation execution with no complex payloads... numeric target hard. Could set status pending with PI? That inflates questions. Alternative: mark as assumption with ASM: \"API usability measured by contract simplicity: one endpoint, one request schema\" — not numeric. Hmm. The rule: \"MUST include at least one NFR entry (or an explicit status: pending placeholder registered in open_questions) for EACH sub-category\". For a headless API, accessibility standards (WCAG) don't apply — I can emit NFR-05 usability with target expressed as: API consumable with a single request per operation, no auth handshake complexity... I think cleanest: NFR-05 category usability, target: \"API contract requires exactly one request/response exchange per calculation; no interactive session\" — measurable-ish, source-backed (transcript \"Simple... No complex payloads\", brief \"No user session ownership\"). status complete. That satisfies the sub-category without inventing.\n- NFR-06 security/compliance: no source evidence. Mandatory. status pending + PI row (audience: product_owner/architect). Fallback: \"Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: service treated as internal-only with no public exposure; authentication deferred to architecture phase\".\n- NFR-07 observability: brief §5 \"Observability readiness: designed for future metrics and tracing integration\". Future → out of scope day one? It says \"readiness... future\". I'll add NFR-07 category observability, target pending? Or scope it as should_have design readiness. Since it's \"future\", I could put in out_of_scope and skip NFR. But it's a non-functional goal in the brief. I'll include NFR-07: observability readiness — target: [PENDING] or assumption. Hmm, keep it simple: include as NFR with status pending? That adds another PI. Alternatively mark as assumption ASM: \"structured logging sufficient at launch; metrics/tracing deferred\" — that's inventing. The brief says \"designed for future metrics and tracing integration\" — the requirement is readiness, not implementation. I'll emit NFR-07 with target: \"service instrumentation points defined for future metrics and tracing (no runtime dependency at launch)\" | status: complete | source: brief §5. That's faithful.\n\nPI rows from NFRs: latency threshold (NFR-01), scalability capacity (NFR-03), availability SLA (NFR-04), security/compliance requirements (NFR-06). That's 4 — could consolidate? They're different decisions on different NFRs — one row per decision is fine. But KFM-002: don't fragment; these are genuinely four distinct decisions. OK.\n\n**kpis:**\nFrom brief §7 Success Criteria + transcript §14:30:\n- KPI-01: Service integrated by at least one upstream system. target: 1 upstream system. measurement: integration record. timeframe: not stated in inputs. status complete.\n- KPI-02: Deterministic unit test coverage for all operations. target: 100% of operations covered. status complete.\n- KPI-03: Optional history persistence validated. target: validated (binary). Hmm numeric? \"history persistence validated\" — target: 1 validation pass. I'll phrase target: \"history persistence validated in a test pass (pass/fail)\". status complete.\n- KPI-04: API contract documented for internal reuse. target: documented (binary). status complete.\n- KPI-05 (transcript): teams stop duplicating math logic; results consistent; trace calculations. KPI-05: reduction in duplicated calculation logic across teams — target: not stated in inputs → status pending? That's descriptive... The transcript lists it as success criterion without a number. Per rules, KPI target missing → pending? KPI targets: \"target: {value if stated, else [PENDING]}\". Is it decision-bearing? A KPI without target — downstream measurement plan would differ. But per KFM-001 discipline, maybe mark target \"not stated in inputs\" and status pending with PI? I'll consolidate: one PI row for business-adoption KPI targets (KPI-05) — actually let me limit PI inflation. KPI-05 target not stated → I'll write target: \"not stated in inputs\" | status: pending and one PI row. Hmm, or assumption. I think one PI row for adoption KPI target is legitimate (product_owner audience). Let me count total PIs at the end and keep ≤ ~8.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: KPI-01..04 are source-backed with clear targets. KPI-05 (duplication reduction) has no number. I'll register one PI for it. Fine.\n\n**jtbd:**\n- JTBD-01 core_functional: When a backend service needs a calculation, perform it via the central math engine so results are consistent across systems. (transcript §00:00, §06:00)\n- JTBD-02 related: When a calculation looks wrong, trace its history to diagnose. (transcript §04:30, §14:30)\n- JTBD-03 related: When a new mathematical operation is needed, extend the service without breaking existing consumers. (transcript §01:30, §12:00)\n- JTBD-04 related: When automation/AI workflows need deterministic calculations, call the service synchronously as part of a pipeline. (transcript §06:00, §12:00)\n\n**traceability:** map FRs to JTBDs:\n- FR-01 (standard ops) → JTBD-01 forward mapped\n- FR-02 (advanced ops) → JTBD-01 forward mapped (also JTBD-03)\n- FR-03 (determinism) → JTBD-01 mapped\n- FR-04 (API contract) → JTBD-01 mapped\n- FR-05 (validation/errors) → JTBD-01 mapped\n- FR-06 (history recording) → JTBD-02 mapped\n- FR-07 (history retrieval) → JTBD-02 mapped\n- FR-08 (extensibility) → JTBD-03 mapped\n- JTBD-04 → FR-04/FR-01? JTBD-04 (automation pipelines) is served by FR-01/FR-04 (any consumer calls the API). Reverse direction: JTBD-04 satisfied by FR-01, FR-04. I'll add reverse row: JTBD-04 | FR-01, FR-04 | reverse | mapped. All mapped, no gaps. \n\n**dependencies:**\n- DEP-01: Upstream consumer systems (backend services) — external; integration by at least one upstream system required for success. owner: not stated in inputs. impact_if_unavailable: service delivered but success criterion unmet.\n- DEP-02: Embedded/local persistence abstraction for optional history — technical. owner: not stated. impact: history feature unavailable; calculations unaffected (per reliability NFR).\n- inter_feature_dependencies: FR-07 (history retrieval) depends on FR-06 (history recording) — retrieval requires records exist. That's a real dependency: FR-07 depends_on [FR-06]. Also FR-01/FR-02 depend on FR-04 (API contract)? The operations are delivered through the calculate endpoint — FR-04 defines the contract; FR-01's ACs reference the endpoint. I'd say FR-01, FR-02 depend_on FR-04. Hmm, or FR-04 is the vehicle. To keep it honest: FR-01 depends_on [FR-04] (operations exposed via the calculate contract), FR-02 depends_on [FR-04], FR-07 depends_on [FR-06]. Others none.\n\n**risks:**\n- RSK-01: Hidden logic differences between systems (transcript §13:30). likelihood: not stated → per template likelihood [PENDING]? KFM-001: likelihood is a descriptive template field → \"not stated in inputs\", no PI. Template shows likelihood: {high|medium|low|[PENDING]}. I'll write \"not stated in inputs\". Mitigation: centralize calculations in the service (from transcript context — the whole point). Mitigation is my synthesis... \"mitigation: {strategy}\" — the brief's objective (eliminate duplicated logic) serves as mitigation. I'll phrase mitigation from source: \"adopt the service as the single source of truth for mathematical behavior (brief §6)\". status complete.\n- RSK-02: Adoption challenge — people bypassing the service; must be simple or teams won't use it (transcript §13:30). Mitigation: simple API, fail-fast clarity (transcript §07:30, §10:00). status complete.\n\n**assumptions:**\n- ASM-01: FR-02 advanced operations priority should_have (inferred from \"basic arithmetic first\" sequencing in transcript).\n- ASM-02: FR-07 history retrieval priority could_have (brief marks history optional; retrieval detail not discussed in transcript).\n- ASM-03: Persona demographics (technical proficiency high for backend developers; organizational context platform engineering team) — inferred from JTBD signals.\n- ASM-04: NFR-01 interim latency baseline? No — NFR-01 is pending with PI, not assumption. Skip.\n- ASM-04: History storage volume/retention expectations not stated; lightweight local persistence assumed sufficient for diagnostics (from transcript \"more diagnostic than regulatory\"). That's source-backed-ish... transcript says diagnostic not regulatory → the assumption \"no regulatory retention requirement applies to history\" is a fair ASM.\n- ASM-05: Synchronous-only consumption at launch (transcript \"Mostly synchronous, yes\" — \"mostly\" leaves room; assumption that no async API is needed day one).\n\n**constraints:**\n- budget: not stated → [PENDING: budget_constraints] | value: \"unknown — stakeholder input required\" | status: pending. Per skill rules, this earns a PI row with fallback baseline (\"no budget ceiling assumed\"). Hmm — that's one of the 4 mandatory constraint sub-categories. The template itself ships the pending placeholder. KFM-002 says technical gaps → assumption; budget is business-facing → legitimate PI. But do I want 4 more PIs for budget/timeline/resource/regulatory? The skill mandates: \"If no source evidence exists: emit the entry with status: pending and register an open question in open_questions whose fallback_behavior names an interim baseline\". So yes, mandatory. But Question Consolidation Rule: multiple missing constraint fields... they're different sub-categories (budget, timeline, resource, regulatory) — one row per decision. Four decisions: budget ceiling, timeline, staffing, regulatory. Could consolidate into fewer? They're distinct decisions with different owners. I'll emit 4 PI rows but with good fallback baselines. Hmm, that pushes PI count: 4 NFR + 1 KPI + 4 constraints = 9. Plus maybe none else. 9 PIs for a brief with ~8 genuine unknowns — acceptable per KFM-001 (\"roughly eight genuine unknowns\" was fine; 59 was not).\n\nActually wait — regulatory: the transcript says \"Not strictly accounting, but precision matters\" and \"History is more diagnostic than regulatory\". So there IS evidence: no regulatory compliance requirement stated for history; precision matters but not accounting-grade. I can emit regulatory constraint: \"No regulatory/accounting compliance requirement identified for calculation history; history is diagnostic (transcript §11:00)\" | status: complete. That's source-backed! Good — reduces to 3 pending constraint PIs (budget, timeline, resource).\n\n- timeline: no dates in sources (and Agentic-First forbids calendar). No phase gates stated → pending placeholder + PI.\n- resource: not stated → pending + PI.\n- organizational: teams must adopt the service / no bypassing — from transcript risk. Constraint: \"adoption depends on simplicity; teams will bypass the service if it is not simple\" — that's more a risk. I'll add organizational: \"Service must be simple enough that teams adopt it over local implementations | source: transcript §13:30 | status: complete\". And technical: statelessness, persistence replaceability.\n\n**mvp_phasing:**\n- phase_1_mvp: must_have: FR-01, FR-03, FR-04, FR-05, FR-08 | source: transcript (\"basic arithmetic first\", fail fast, extensibility \"definitely\") | status: complete? The phasing assignment is my inference from priorities — priorities are source-backed for must_haves. Phase assignment follows priority → status complete (grounded in source-stated sequencing \"basic first\"). Hmm, FR-08 extensibility must_have — transcript \"Definitely\" → must_have. OK.\n- phase_2: should_have: FR-02 (advanced ops), FR-06 (history recording) | status: assumption [ASM-01 for FR-02 priority; FR-06 \"optional... when needed\" → should_have is inference → ASM]. I'll tag status: assumption [ASM-01, ASM-04]? Keep simple: status: assumption with ASM-01 (advanced ops sequencing) — and FR-06: transcript \"we want the ability to log calculations when needed\" — needed when? Not day one per \"Not initially\" (§11:00 persistence durability \"not initially\"). I'll add ASM-04: history recording deferred to phase 2.\n- phase_3: could_have: FR-07 (history retrieval) | assumption ASM-02.\n- deferred: batch calculations, expression evaluation, workflow integration, event-based history — these aren't FRs (out of scope). The deferred section lists wont_have FRs; I have no wont_have FRs. Write `- none | source: ... | status: complete`? Template: \"A phase with no members is written `- none | source: {ref} | status: complete`\". Good.\n- sequencing_notes: FR-07 depends on FR-06; FR-01/FR-02 depend on FR-04.\n\n**literal_registry:**\n- LIT-01: route `POST /api/v1/math/calculate` | route | FR-04 | brief §4\n- LIT-02: field `operand1` | field | FR-04 | brief §4\n- LIT-03: field `operand2` | field | FR-04\n- LIT-04: field `operation` | field | FR-04\n- LIT-05: enum member `ADD` | enum | FR-01, FR-04 | brief §4 example\n- LIT-06: field `resultValue` | field | FR-04\n- LIT-07: field `operationType` | field | FR-04\n- LIT-08: field `timestamp` | field | FR-04\n- LIT-09: example values operand1=10.5, operand2=5.0, ADD → resultValue 15.5 | other | FR-01-AC | brief §4. Maybe as range/other.\n- LIT-10: error conditions: division by zero, unsupported operation | error_code? They're named error conditions, not codes. type: constraint? I'll use type: other, name \"invalid_input_error_conditions\", exact_value: \"division by zero; unsupported operation\".\n- LIT-11: operations enum from brief: ADD, SUBTRACT?? Brief only shows ADD explicitly; standard ops listed as Addition/Subtraction/Multiplication/Division (prose). Transcript: \"add, subtract, multiply, divide\". Exact enum members beyond ADD are not stated as literals. Only ADD is a literal. I'll register only ADD as enum literal and note operation names in glossary. Hmm — \"Include only literals explicitly present in source material\". ADD is explicit. The others appear as words, not enum members. I'll add LIT for the operation set as prose-derived? Safer: only ADD. But FR-01 needs operation identifiers for subtract/multiply/divide... those aren't stated as literals. Keep FR-01 ACs using prose operation names, and register ADD literal. Fine.\n\n**glossary:**\n- TERM-01: Calc-Service — the math operations microservice (brief title)\n- TERM-02: Standard Operations — addition, subtraction, multiplication, division (brief §2)\n- TERM-03: Advanced Operations — exponentiation, roots, trigonometric functions (brief §2)\n- TERM-04: History Tracking — optional recording of calculation metadata for traceability and diagnostics (brief §2)\n- TERM-05: Math Engine — deterministic computation capability consumed by backend services, automation workflows, AI-driven processes (brief overview)\n\n**open_questions:**\npending_inputs rows (consolidated, decision-bearing only):\n- PI-01: Precise latency target for standard operations (NFR-01). audience: product_owner. enum: \"Standard (< 100 ms p95, commodity hosting)\" / \"High-performance (< 10 ms p95, requires low-latency deployment)\" / hmm — options carry trade-off. fallback: \"Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: standard operations complete within 100 ms at p95\". blocking: no.\n- PI-02: Scalability/capacity target (NFR-03): expected request volume/concurrency. audience: product_owner. enum options: low (<100 rps), medium (100–1000 rps), high (>1000 rps)? Trade-off labels. fallback: \"stateless horizontal scaling with no fixed instance count; capacity sized during architecture\".\n- PI-03: Availability/uptime SLA (NFR-04). audience: product_owner. enum: 99.9% / 99.95% / 99.99% with cost trade-offs. fallback: \"industry-standard 99.9% monthly uptime baseline\".\n- PI-04: Security/compliance requirements (NFR-06): authentication/authorization expectations for the API. audience: product_owner (or architect? Security requirements for the product = product_owner; the mechanism = architect). enum: internal-only unauthenticated / service-to-service auth required / full authZ per operation. fallback: \"treated as internal-only service; authentication deferred to architecture phase\".\n- PI-05: Adoption KPI target (KPI-05): measurable target for reduction in duplicated math logic. audience: product_owner. free_text? Prefer enum... it's a metric value — answer_shape: free_text is discouraged for client/product_owner per AP-002 (\"phrase a client-facing NFR/metric gap as free_text\" is an anti-pattern). Use enum: \"% of consumer teams migrated within first phase\" options? Simpler: number. answer_shape: number. fallback: \"adoption tracked as count of integrated upstream systems (KPI-01) until a duplication-reduction target is set\".\n- PI-06: Budget constraints — unknown. audience: client. fallback: \"no budget ceiling assumed; scope treated as uncapped until stated\". blocking: no.\n- PI-07: Timeline constraints — unknown. audience: client (or product_owner — schedule is PO/client). fallback: \"no delivery deadline assumed; sequencing by phase gates only\".\n- PI-08: Resource/staffing constraints — unknown. audience: client. fallback: \"no staffing constraints assumed; team composition decided at planning\".\n\ntraceability_gaps: none (all mapped) → empty table with headers.\nassumptions_to_validate: ASM-01..ASM-05 rows.\n\nSummary counts: FRs 7 (wait, I said FR-01..FR-08? Let me recount: FR-01 standard ops, FR-02 advanced ops, FR-03 determinism, FR-04 API contract, FR-05 validation, FR-06 history recording, FR-07 history retrieval, FR-08 extensibility = 8 FRs). NFRs 7. JTBDs 4. KPIs 5. PIs 8. TGs 0. ASMs 5. Risks 2. Deps 2.\n\nStatus: draft (pending_inputs > 0).\n\nPriorities recap:\n- FR-01 must_have (transcript \"basic arithmetic first... covers most cases\") — complete\n- FR-02 should_have [ASM-01] — assumption\n- FR-03 must_have (brief §5 determinism; transcript \"Exactly\") — complete\n- FR-04 must_have (brief §4 defines contract; success criteria \"API contract documented\") — complete\n- FR-05 must_have (transcript \"Yes. Fail fast.\") — complete\n- FR-06 should_have [ASM-04] — assumption (optional per brief; \"when needed\")\n- FR-07 could_have [ASM-02] — assumption\n- FR-08 must_have (transcript \"Definitely\") — complete\n\nFR statuses: FR-02, FR-06, FR-07 status: assumption? Wait — Status Protocol for FRs: status is complete|pending (template shows those two for FRs). Assumption items get ASM-XX. The FR template line: `- status: {complete | pending}`. Priority assumption is tagged on the priority field with ASM-XX. So FR-02 status: complete (all evidence-bearing fields populated; priority inferred → tagged ASM-01 on the priority line). Hmm, but \"assumption: inferred from industry standard → ASM-XX ID assigned\" — the priority inference gets ASM tag; the FR itself has source evidence → status complete. I'll do: FR-02 priority: should_have | status: assumption [ASM-01] on the priority sub-line, FR status: complete. Actually template shows priority as its own line; I'll write `- priority: should_have | status: assumption [ASM-01]`. And FR status: complete. That reads cleanly.\n\nFR-06: priority should_have | status: assumption [ASM-04]; FR status complete (ACs from transcript: persistence failure → still returns).\nFR-07: priority could_have | status: assumption [ASM-02]; FR status complete? ACs for retrieval: given history records exist → recent records returned. Source: brief §4 \"Provides recent calculation records\". OK complete.\n\nAcceptance criteria — all FRs have ≥1 AC from sources. Good, no AC-related PIs.\n\nNow — NFR statuses: NFR-01 pending (PI-01), NFR-03 pending (PI-02), NFR-04 pending (PI-03), NFR-06 pending (PI-04). NFR-02, NFR-05, NFR-07 complete.\n\nKPI-05 pending (PI-05). KPI-01..04 complete.\n\nConstraints: budget pending (PI-06), timeline pending (PI-07), resource pending (PI-08), regulatory complete, organizational complete, technical complete.\n\nTotal PIs: 8. TGs: 0. ASM rows in assumptions_to_validate: ASM-01..ASM-05 (5).\n\nPersonas: P-01 primary (backend service developer), P-02 automation/AI workflow integrator, P-03 reporting pipeline developer. Demographics: assumption ASM-03 for all three (role/seniority partially stated — \"backend services\" teams; proficiency not stated). P-01 prioritization_rationale: source-backed (transcript \"Backend services first\") → complete.\n\nWait, ASM numbering: ASM-01 (FR-02 priority), ASM-02 (FR-07 priority), ASM-03 (persona demographics), ASM-04 (FR-06 priority/phase), ASM-05 (synchronous-only at launch). Also maybe ASM-06: client_name not stated? No — that's \"not stated in inputs\", not an assumption.\n\nHmm, also NFR-05 usability target — I claimed complete with \"one request/response exchange per calculation\". Source: transcript \"Simple. Send operands and operation, get result. No complex payloads.\" + brief \"No user session ownership\". OK.\n\nNFR-07 observability: \"instrumentation points defined for future metrics and tracing integration; no runtime dependency at launch\" — brief says \"Observability readiness: Designed for future metrics and tracing integration\". Target numeric? It's a design-readiness criterion, hard to numeric. The NFR rule says targets must be numeric/measurable or pending. \"Instrumentation points defined\" is verifiable (binary). I'll phrase target: \"metrics and tracing integration points defined in the service design before launch (verifiable by design review); no runtime dependency at launch\" — measurable pass/fail. status complete. Hmm, \"before launch\" — no calendar, fine.\n\nActually, to be safe with the \"NFR Targets: MUST be numeric/measurable\" gate, binary verifiable targets are acceptable (pass/fail counts as measurable). OK.\n\n**Skeleton + Phase B writes.** Now execute:\n\n1. Update `_progress.json` (FIRST ACTION) — real UTC timestamp. Get time via bash `date -u`.\n2. Write skeleton SPEC_FILE with full template shape.\n3. Update tracker skeleton_written: true.\n4. Phase B: 16 sections via Edit, tracker update after each. That's 16 Edits + 16 tracker updates — I can batch tracker updates with bash? No — §10.5 forbids Bash mutation of SPEC_FILE but _progress.json isn't SPEC_FILE; still, use write/edit tools for tracker. To save calls, I can update the tracker after each section using the edit tool. That's 32 calls... acceptable but heavy. Alternative: the tracker update is mandatory after each section (\"in the same step\"). I'll comply — use edit on _progress.json flipping one section at a time. Actually I can flip multiple in one edit if I do sections in order and update after each — no, one edit per section update. Fine.\n\nActually, to be pragmatic: I'll do Edit(section) then Edit(_progress.json) pairs. 16 pairs = 32 tool calls plus skeleton etc. That's a lot but correct. Alternatively update tracker in batches of a few sections — violates \"in the same step\". I'll comply per-section. Hmm — the guidance says \"After EACH Phase B section Edit lands, in the same step: UPDATE _progress.json\". I'll do it.\n\nLet me get the current UTC time first, then write the tracker + skeleton.\n\nSESSION_ID: reuse `pd-20261027-calc001` from the existing tracker (continuity; avoids a second tracker). SPEC_FILE = `artifacts/outputs/product-definition/prd/PRD-SPEC-pd-20261027-calc001.md`.\n\nLet me start. First: timestamp + tracker update.","turnId":"ab5eba29-1f44-44be-8e76-cd4723fc60e0"}}
