[08:31:02] {"type":"thinking_delta","payload":{"delta":"Now the final section 16/16: open_questions. Consolidate pending items:\n\nPending items across sections:\n- FR-03 design_note pending (precision policy) — decision-bearing → PI-01\n- FR-08 design_note pending (retrieval endpoint path/shape) — decision-bearing → PI-02\n- NFR-01 target pending (latency threshold) → PI-03\n- NFR-04 target pending (scalability numeric target) → PI-04\n- NFR-05 target pending (observability) → PI-05\n- NFR-06 target pending (security) → PI-06\n- NFR-07 target pending (usability) → PI-07\n- KPI-05, KPI-06, KPI-07 targets pending → consolidate into one PI-08 (KPI numeric targets)\n- Constraints: budget, timeline, resource, regulatory pending → consolidate into one PI-09 (project constraints: budget/timeline/resource/regulatory)\n- DEP-01 owner pending → PI-10 (owner for upstream integration dependency)\n- design_notes pending on FR-01, FR-02, FR-05, FR-07, FR-09, NFR-01..07 — these are descriptive template fields, not decision-bearing → not questions (KFM-001). Hmm, but design_note [PENDING] markers... The template says all pending items appear in open_questions. But KFM-001/AP-001: a template field is not a question. design_note is a descriptive field — \"not stated in inputs\" would be better. But I already wrote [PENDING: design_note for FR-XX]. Hmm. The consolidation sweep reads all pending items. To be safe and consistent with KFM-001, design_note placeholders are descriptive fields → should be \"not stated in inputs\" rather than pending rows. But leaving [PENDING] markers in the artifact while not registering them could trip the §2.1 \"no unresolved placeholders\" check... The check says placeholders must be \"either resolved from available evidence OR surfaced explicitly as a pending item / assumption / blocker — never left silent.\"\n\nOptions: (a) register each design_note as PI rows — violates KFM-001 (fragmentation, template fields). (b) Change design_note [PENDING] to \"not stated in inputs\" — cleaner. The design_note field is optional (\"if any; [PENDING] if none surfaced\"). Actually the template says design_note: {mechanism... if any; [PENDING] if none surfaced}. So [PENDING: design_note] is the template's own convention for \"none surfaced\" — it's not a decision-bearing gap. But the open_questions consolidation says every [PENDING] marker appears in the registry. Hmm, tension. The KFM-001 rule explicitly says: \"A template field is not a question... Descriptive fields the inputs do not cover are written `not stated in inputs` with no registry row.\"\n\nBest: replace the bare [PENDING: design_note for FR-XX] with \"not stated in inputs\" for those where nothing was surfaced. That removes placeholder noise. For FR-03 and FR-08 design_notes I marked status: pending with real content — those are decision-bearing (precision policy, retrieval endpoint) → PI rows.\n\nWait, FR-03's design_note says \"the concrete precision policy... is not stated in inputs | status: pending\". Precision policy: is it decision-bearing? Yes — downstream implementation would be built differently (decimal digits, rounding mode affect results). It's business-facing (numerical behavior). → PI-01. Actually is it a question for client or architect? The precision policy (digits/rounding) is a product decision affecting financial consistency — audience: product_owner or client. The PO said \"precision matters, no floating-point surprises\" but no concrete policy. I'd say product_owner with enum options? Options like \"2 decimal places fixed\", \"High precision (e.g. 34 digits)\", \"Configurable per request\". Hmm, keep it enum with trade-offs.\n\nFR-08 retrieval endpoint: decision-bearing (API surface). Audience: product_owner. Question: what endpoint/shape for history retrieval? assumption: not modeled; FR-08 emitted with generic retrieval capability, endpoint undefined.\n\nNFR-01 latency: enum options (Standard <500ms, High-performance <100ms, etc.) — audience product_owner. Actually brief says \"millisecond-level\" — the gap is the exact numeric threshold. Enum: \"< 100 ms (strict instant)\", \"< 500 ms (standard)\", \"< 1 s (relaxed)\". Audience: product_owner.\n\nNFR-04 scalability target: audience architect? It's a capacity target — product_owner/architect. The question \"what concurrency/volume target\" — architect can derive; but the business expectation (automation spikes) is product knowledge. I'll say product_owner with enum options.\n\nNFR-05 observability: technical → architect? It's a product commitment on observability. Per Domain Exclusion Rule, observability tooling is technical... but NFR sub-category (e) rule says security/compliance etc. must have entry or pending+open question. The mandatory NFR sub-categories rule: \"If a sub-category has no source evidence, set status: pending and register an open question\". So NFR-05, NFR-06, NFR-07 pending rows with open questions are per the skill's own mandate. Audience: NFR-06 security → architect (delivery team owns) or product_owner? Security target for an internal API — architect. NFR-05 observability → architect. NFR-07 usability → product_owner.\n\nKPI targets (KPI-05/06/07): one consolidated PI row — audience product_owner.\n\nConstraints (budget/timeline/resource/regulatory): one consolidated PI row — audience client (facts about their business/commercial). Per KFM-002, one row per decision. Budget/timeline/resource are separate decisions arguably, but consolidation rule: one row per decision. Budget, timeline, resource, regulatory are four different sub-categories... but they're all \"project envelope unknowns\". KFM-002 says one row per decision, even across multiple missing fields on the same item. These are different items though. Hmm. I'll consolidate budget+timeline+resource into one \"project envelope\" question and regulatory into... actually regulatory: the transcript says history is \"more diagnostic than regulatory\" — no regulatory constraints stated. The template requires the entry with pending placeholder. I'll make one PI for budget/timeline/resource (commercial envelope, client) and one PI for regulatory/compliance constraints (client). Actually simpler: one PI per mandatory sub-category would be 4 rows — that's the template's design (\"unknown — stakeholder input required\" pending placeholder). But KFM-001 warns against fragmentation. The skill's constraints rule says: \"If no source evidence exists: emit the entry with status: pending and register an open question in open_questions whose fallback_behavior names an interim baseline\". It doesn't say one per sub-category. I'll do one consolidated PI-09 covering budget/timeline/resource (client commercial envelope) and PI-10 for regulatory. Hmm, but regulatory is genuinely different (compliance). OK.\n\nDEP-01 owner: descriptive field (owner) — per KFM-001, \"owner\" is a descriptive template field → \"not stated in inputs\", no PI row. But I wrote [PENDING: owner for DEP-01]. Change to \"not stated in inputs\". DEP-01 status: the dependency itself is sourced (success criteria) — status complete with owner not stated. Let me fix DEP-01: owner: not stated in inputs; status: complete. Similarly DEP-02 owner: not stated in inputs.\n\nAlso FR design_notes: change [PENDING: design_note for FR-01] etc. to \"not stated in inputs\". And NFR design_notes similarly. And NFR-02 design_note.\n\nWait — but §2.1 check 2 says no lingering [TBD]/[PLACEHOLDER]... \"Each is either resolved from available evidence OR surfaced explicitly as a pending item / assumption / blocker — never left silent.\" The [PENDING: ...] markers in pending_inputs rows are fine (they're the registry's own type markers). The [PENDING: budget_constraints] in constraints is the template's sanctioned placeholder shape. The [PENDING: design_note for FR-01] markers — template sanctioned (\"[PENDING] if none surfaced\"). Hmm, actually the template literally says design_note: {...; [PENDING] if none surfaced}. So [PENDING: design_note for FR-01] is the template's own convention. But KFM-001 says descriptive fields not covered → \"not stated in inputs\". design_note is optional. I'll convert bare design_note pendings to \"not stated in inputs\" to reduce noise. That's cleaner and consistent with KFM-001.\n\nLet me do these edits:\n1. FR-01, FR-02, FR-05, FR-07, FR-09 design_note → \"not stated in inputs\" (5 edits or one per FR... they're distinct strings, each unique). Also NFR-01..NFR-07 design_notes → \"not stated in inputs\" except keep pending targets.\n2. DEP-01/DEP-02 owner → not stated in inputs; DEP-01 status → complete.\n\nActually wait — is that over-editing? The design_note [PENDING] markers would then need to appear in open_questions per template rule \"All pending items appear in open_questions\". By converting to \"not stated in inputs\" they're no longer pending. Good.\n\nBut careful: FR-03 design_note has real content with status: pending → PI-01. FR-08 design_note \"the retrieval endpoint path and response shape are not stated in inputs | status: pending\" → PI-02.\n\nHmm, actually for FR-03: is the precision policy decision-bearing enough? Yes — RSK-04 references it. Keep.\n\nPI rows (final):\n- PI-01: precision policy (decimal digits/rounding mode) — FR-03 — audience product_owner, enum: (a) Fixed 2-decimal financial style, (b) High precision (dozens of digits, scientific), (c) Configurable per request. blocking: no. fallback: FR-03 emitted with determinism ACs only; precision policy left to architecture with high-precision internal computation as the stated baseline.\n- PI-02: history retrieval endpoint contract — FR-08 — audience product_owner — free_text? It's a design shape... audience architect? The endpoint path is API design → architect. But whether history retrieval is in scope day-one is product. FR-08 is could_have/phase 3. Question: what is the history retrieval API contract (endpoint, response shape)? audience: architect. assumption: FR-08 emitted as generic retrieval capability with no endpoint defined; deferred to architecture.\n- PI-03: latency numeric target — NFR-01 — enum — product_owner.\n- PI-04: scalability numeric target — NFR-04 — enum — product_owner.\n- PI-05: observability target — NFR-05 — architect — enum? (basic health metrics now vs full metrics+tracing at launch). free_text ok but prefer enum.\n- PI-06: security target — NFR-06 — architect — enum (internal-no-auth vs service-to-service auth).\n- PI-07: usability/accessibility standard — NFR-07 — product_owner — enum (API usability only vs WCAG-documented... hmm, for an API service usability = DX). Options: \"Simple contract only (no formal usability standard)\", \"Documented SLA on error clarity + docs\", \"Formal DX standard\". Keep enum.\n- PI-08: KPI numeric targets for adoption/consistency/traceability KPIs — KPI-05/06/07 — product_owner — free_text? Could be enum but really needs numbers → free_text. KFM-002 says don't phrase client-facing NFR/metric gap as free_text... \"phrase a client-facing NFR/metric gap as answer_shape: free_text\" is prohibited → use enum. Options: e.g. \"Set numeric targets now\", \"Keep qualitative for MVP, numeric targets at first review\", \"Drop from KPI set\". Hmm. I'll do enum with 3 options.\n- PI-09: budget/timeline/resource constraints — client — enum (e.g. \"No fixed ceiling — proceed uncapped\", \"Provide constraints before planning\"). Actually answer_shape enum with options: (a) No constraints to apply — proceed uncapped, (b) Constraints exist — will provide. fallback: \"no budget ceiling assumed, scope treated as uncapped until stated\" per skill example.\n- PI-10: regulatory/compliance constraints — client — enum similar. fallback: \"no regulatory constraints applied; history treated as diagnostic only\".\n\nThat's 10 PI rows. Plus TG rows: none (all mapped). Plus ASM rows: ASM-01..ASM-07 → assumptions_to_validate table (7 rows). All non-blocking.\n\nSummary counts: total_frs 10, total_nfrs 7, total_jtbds 4, total_kpis 7, pending_inputs 10, traceability_gaps 0, assumptions 7, risks 4, dependencies 2, status: draft.\n\nSidecar _open_questions: 10 PI + 7 ASM = 17 entries. Each needs question, kind, subject, why_it_matters, blocking, assumption_if_unanswered (questions), audience, answer_shape, options for enums, artifact_ref, traces_to.\n\nThat's a lot but required. Let me write the open_questions section first, then the fixes to design_notes/DEP, then hash, audit, memory bank, sidecar.\n\nActually, to reduce edit count, I'll do the design_note/DEP fixes with replace_all where the string is identical... they're all distinct (\"[PENDING: design_note for FR-01]\" etc.). 12 small edits. Alternatively use replace_all on \"[PENDING: design_note for \" — no, replace_all replaces with same string. Each needs individual edit. That's fine but many calls. Alternatively keep [PENDING: design_note ...] markers as-is — they're template-sanctioned. But then §2.1 check 2 \"no unresolved placeholders... [TBD], [to be identified], [PLACEHOLDER], TODO\" — [PENDING: design_note] is arguably a pending item surfaced explicitly. The template itself uses [PENDING: ...] markers. And the open_questions consolidation rule says every [PENDING] marker appears in open_questions. If I leave design_note pendings unregistered, a gate might flag. If I register 12 design_note PI rows, KFM-001 violation.\n\nThe cleanest compliant path: convert bare design_notes to \"not stated in inputs\" (KFM-001-sanctioned). Do it.\n\nLet me batch these edits efficiently — I can do multiple edit calls in one message since they're independent (different old_strings, same file... but edits to the same file in parallel could conflict? The tool applies sequentially; different regions, should be fine. But safer sequential? The tool validates old_string against current content; parallel edits to same file with distinct anchors should work. I'll batch a few at a time.","turnId":"70cd4f92-bf6c-4341-a61a-f791ad31445d"}}
[08:31:02] {"type":"message_delta","payload":{"delta":"**Section 16/16: `open_questions`** — plus cleanup: converting bare `design_note`/`owner` placeholders to `not stated in inputs` per KFM-001 (a template field is not a question), keeping only decision-bearing gaps as PI rows.","turnId":"70cd4f92-bf6c-4341-a61a-f791ad31445d"}}
