[08:32:33] {"type":"thinking_delta","payload":{"delta":"NFR-03's design_note is already correct (it was never a placeholder — I wrote the real one). Good. Now write the final section: open_questions. Consolidate:\n\nPending items on disk:\n- FR-03 design_note: precision policy pending → decision-bearing (affects FR-03 ACs, RSK-04) → PI-01\n- FR-08 design_note: retrieval endpoint path/response shape not stated → decision-bearing? The endpoint contract would be built differently → PI-02 (fold with FR-08)\n- NFR-01 target: exact numeric latency threshold → PI-03\n- NFR-04 target: numeric concurrency/volume target → PI-04\n- NFR-05 target: observability target → PI-05\n- NFR-06 target: security requirement → PI-06\n- NFR-07 target: usability standard → PI-07\n- KPI-05/06/07 numeric targets → consolidate into one PI-08? They're three different KPIs but same decision type (quantify qualitative success criteria). Question Consolidation Rule: one row per decision, even across multiple missing fields on the same item. These are three different items but the same decision: \"quantify the qualitative success criteria\". I'll consolidate into one PI row covering KPI-05/06/07.\n- DEP-01 status: pending (owner not stated — descriptive; but DEP-01 itself is pending because owner missing... owner is descriptive → \"not stated in inputs\" now. But DEP-01 status: pending remains because... actually the only missing field was owner, which is descriptive. Hmm — but DEP-01's status was set pending. The decision-bearing aspect: which upstream system integrates first? That's genuinely decision-bearing for KPI-01. I'll keep DEP-01 pending and fold into a PI row? Actually the Downstream Impact Test: does any FR/NFR/AC change based on the answer? KPI-01 measurement depends on it. I'll register PI for DEP-01: which upstream system is the first integration target. That's a real product decision.\n- Constraints budget/timeline/resource/regulatory pending → per skill rules these MUST be registered with fallback naming interim baseline. Consolidate into one PI row? They're four mandatory sub-categories, all unknown. One row per decision — the decision is \"project envelope (budget/timeline/resource) and regulatory constraints\". I'll make one PI row for the delivery envelope covering budget/timeline/resource, and one for regulatory. Actually regulatory: history is diagnostic not regulatory — there's evidence suggesting no regulatory constraint. But the template requires the entry; status pending with fallback. I'll consolidate budget+timeline+resource into PI-09 (delivery envelope) and regulatory into PI-10? Hmm, regulatory has evidence it's not regulatory-grade (\"more diagnostic than regulatory\"). But that's about history, not the whole system. Keep PI-10 for regulatory with fallback \"no regulatory constraints assumed; history treated as diagnostic-only per transcript\".\n\nAlso assumptions_to_validate: ASM-01..ASM-07 (7 rows). All are assumptions → mirror all in the table. But §11.6: \"An assumption already confirmed is a decision\" — none are confirmed; all active. Mirror all 7.\n\nTraceability gaps: none (all mapped) → empty table.\n\nSummary counts:\n- total_frs: 10\n- total_nfrs: 7\n- total_jtbds: 4\n- total_kpis: 7\n- pending_inputs: 10 (PI-01..PI-10)\n- traceability_gaps: 0\n- assumptions: 7\n- risks: 4\n- dependencies: 2\n- status: draft\n\nWait — also FR-03's design_note has status: pending (precision policy). That's PI-01. FR-08 design_note pending → PI-02.\n\nLet me also double check NFR-01 target has trailing space \"…threshold] \" — fine.\n\nPI rows with columns: id | affected_ids | blocking | type | section | item_id | field | impact | question | fallback_behavior | source_ref.\n\nPI-01: precision policy. affected: FR-03, FR-03-AC-*, RSK-04. blocking: no. type: missing. section: functional_requirements. item: FR-03. field: design_note/precision_policy. impact HIGH. question: \"What precision policy (decimal digits and rounding mode) must the high-precision computation apply so results are identical across systems?\" fallback: \"Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: a single high-precision decimal computation strategy is applied service-wide and treated as an implementation detail until stated\". audience: product_owner? It's a business-ish decision (financial contexts). I'd say product_owner. answer_shape: enum? Options: e.g. \"Fixed high-precision decimal (e.g. 34 digits, banker's rounding)\", \"Configurable per request\", \"Platform default chosen by architecture\". Keep enum with trade-offs.\n\nPI-02: FR-08 history retrieval endpoint contract. affected: FR-08, FR-08-AC-01. blocking no. HIGH? MEDIUM. question: \"What is the history retrieval contract (endpoint path, response shape, record retention window)?\" fallback: \"Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: FR-08 is emitted with status pending and no endpoint contract; history retrieval is excluded from the MVP phase\". audience: product_owner.\n\nPI-03: NFR-01 latency threshold. enum: \"< 100 ms (standard)\", \"< 500 ms\", \"< 1 s\". audience: product_owner. fallback: \"interim baseline: millisecond-level interpreted as sub-second (< 1000 ms) for standard operations\".\n\nPI-04: NFR-04 scalability target. fallback: \"interim baseline: horizontal scaling by instance addition with no numeric concurrency target committed\".\n\nPI-05: NFR-05 observability. fallback: \"interim baseline: structured logging sufficient for diagnostics; metrics/tracing deferred\".\n\nPI-06: NFR-06 security. audience: architect? Security requirement for internal API — architect owns. fallback: \"interim baseline: service deployed on the internal network with no additional auth assumed\".\n\nPI-07: NFR-07 usability. fallback: \"interim baseline: API simplicity treated as design principle (simple payload), no formal usability standard\".\n\nPI-08: KPI-05/06/07 numeric targets. audience: product_owner. fallback: \"interim baseline: qualitative success criteria tracked as binary adoption checks\".\n\nPI-09: DEP-01 first integration target. affected: DEP-01, KPI-01. audience: product_owner. fallback: \"interim baseline: any one upstream system qualifies; integration sequencing left to delivery planning\".\n\nPI-10: budget/timeline/resource constraints. affected: constraints. audience: client. fallback: \"Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: no budget ceiling, no calendar deadline, and no staffing cap assumed; scope treated as uncapped until stated\".\n\nPI-11: regulatory. affected: constraints. question: \"Are there regulatory or compliance obligations applying to the service or its calculation history?\" fallback: \"interim baseline: no regulatory constraints assumed; history treated as diagnostic-only per discovery-transcript.md §11:00\".\n\nHmm, that's 11 PI rows. Fine — genuine decisions, consolidated.\n\nassumptions_to_validate table: ASM-01..ASM-07 with columns id | affected_ids | blocking | assumption | impact_if_wrong | validation_method | fallback_behavior | source_ref.\n\nvalidation_method for each:\n- ASM-01: confirm consumer roles with product owner\n- ASM-02: confirm field names against final API contract review\n- ASM-03: confirm persistence technology choice in architecture phase\n- ASM-04: confirm statelessness in architecture design\n- ASM-05: confirm whether NFR-03/FR-07 duplication is intended\n- ASM-06: confirm no async/batch mode at launch\n- ASM-07: confirm MoSCoW priorities with product owner\n\nblocking: no for all.\n\nNow write the open_questions section.","turnId":"cdac3586-0ea1-448a-a553-50c8cc9631c7"}}
