[08:32:43] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-aa7022125685290e","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\artifacts\\outputs\\product-definition\\prd\\PRD-SPEC-pd-20261027-calc001.md\" lines=\"590\" start=\"120\" end=\"319\" returned=\"200\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n   120|\n   121|### FR-03: Produce deterministic, high-precision results\n   122|- priority: must_have\n   123|- source: project-brief.md §2 Precision Strategy, §5 Determinism; discovery-transcript.md §03:00 (\"deterministic outputs for the same input\", \"We don't want floating-point surprises\")\n   124|- status: complete\n   125|- design_note: high-precision internal computation is the stated mechanism to reduce rounding inconsistencies; the concrete precision policy (decimal digits, rounding mode) is not stated in inputs | status: pending\n   126|- depends_on: [FR-01]\n   127|- acceptance_criteria:\n   128|  - FR-03-AC-01: given: any supported operation | when: the same request is submitted repeatedly | then: the response returns the identical `resultValue` every time\n   129|  - FR-03-AC-02: given: two different client systems submit the same calculation | when: both responses are compared | then: both return the identical `resultValue` with no rounding differences between systems\n   130|\n   131|### FR-04: Expose a calculate operation via `POST /api/v1/math/calculate`\n   132|- priority: must_have\n   133|- source: project-brief.md §4 API Contract; discovery-transcript.md §07:30 (\"Simple. Send operands and operation, get result. No complex payloads.\")\n   134|- status: complete\n   135|- design_note: request/response field names `operand1`, `operand2`, `operation`, `resultValue`, `operationType`, `timestamp` are taken verbatim from the brief's conceptual contract | status: assumption [ASM-02]\n   136|- depends_on: [FR-01]\n   137|- acceptance_criteria:\n   138|  - FR-04-AC-01: given: the service is running | when: a request is sent to `POST /api/v1/math/calculate` with body `{\"operand1\": 10.5, \"operand2\": 5.0, \"operation\": \"ADD\"}` | then: the response contains `resultValue: 15.5`, `operationType: \"ADD\"`, and a `timestamp` value\n   139|  - FR-04-AC-02: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted | then: the response contains only the simple result payload (result, operation type, timestamp) with no complex payload structure\n   140|\n   141|### FR-05: Return clear, fail-fast errors for invalid input\n   142|- priority: must_have\n   143|- source: discovery-transcript.md §10:00 (\"Clear errors. Division by zero, unsupported operation — that kind of thing.\" / \"Strict validation? Yes. Fail fast.\")\n   144|- status: complete\n   145|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   146|- depends_on: [FR-01]\n   147|- acceptance_criteria:\n   148|  - FR-05-AC-01: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted with operation `DIVIDE` and operand2 equal to zero | then: the service returns a clear error identifying division by zero and does not return a numeric result\n   149|  - FR-05-AC-02: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted with an operation value the service does not support | then: the service returns a clear error identifying the unsupported operation\n   150|  - FR-05-AC-03: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted with missing or non-numeric operands | then: the service rejects the request with a clear validation error before any computation\n   151|\n   152|### FR-06: Record calculation history as optional, auxiliary local persistence\n   153|- priority: could_have\n   154|- source: project-brief.md §2 History Tracking; discovery-transcript.md §04:30 (\"we want the ability to log calculations when needed — mainly debugging and traceability\"), §11:00 (\"persistence is auxiliary\")\n   155|- status: complete\n   156|- design_note: implemented as lightweight local persistence via an embedded database abstraction; persistence can be replaced without changing the API contract | status: assumption [ASM-03]\n   157|- depends_on: [FR-01]\n   158|- acceptance_criteria:\n   159|  - FR-06-AC-01: given: history tracking is enabled | when: a calculation is executed | then: calculation metadata is recorded for traceability and diagnostics\n   160|  - FR-06-AC-02: given: history tracking is enabled | when: a calculation is executed | then: the history write does not delay or block delivery of the calculation response\n   161|  - FR-06-AC-03: given: history tracking is disabled or not configured | when: a calculation is executed | then: the calculation still executes and returns a result normally\n   162|\n   163|### FR-07: Calculation must succeed even if history persistence fails\n   164|- priority: must_have\n   165|- source: project-brief.md §5 Reliability; discovery-transcript.md §04:30 (\"If persistence fails, should the calculation still return?\" / \"Yes, always.\"), §15:00 (\"Persistence must never block calculation\")\n   166|- status: complete\n   167|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   168|- depends_on: [FR-06]\n   169|- acceptance_criteria:\n   170|  - FR-07-AC-01: given: history persistence is failing or unavailable | when: a valid calculate request is submitted | then: the calculation still succeeds and returns the correct result to the caller\n   171|\n   172|### FR-08: Provide history retrieval of recent calculation records\n   173|- priority: could_have\n   174|- source: project-brief.md §4 History Retrieval (\"Provides recent calculation records for diagnostics and auditing\")\n   175|- status: complete\n   176|- design_note: the retrieval endpoint path and response shape are not stated in inputs | status: pending\n   177|- depends_on: [FR-06]\n   178|- acceptance_criteria:\n   179|  - FR-08-AC-01: given: history tracking is enabled and calculations have been executed | when: a history retrieval request is submitted | then: the service returns recent calculation records for diagnostics and auditing\n   180|\n   181|### FR-09: Support extensibility so new operations are added without breaking existing consumers\n   182|- priority: must_have\n   183|- source: discovery-transcript.md §01:30 (\"Definitely. New operations will be added without breaking existing consumers.\")\n   184|- status: complete\n   185|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   186|- depends_on: [FR-01]\n   187|- acceptance_criteria:\n   188|  - FR-09-AC-01: given: existing consumers are integrated against the calculate operation | when: a new operation is added to the service | then: existing consumers continue to receive correct results for previously supported operations with no contract change\n   189|\n   190|### FR-10: Operate as a stateless, independently deployable, horizontally scalable service\n   191|- priority: must_have\n   192|- source: project-brief.md §3 Architectural Positioning (\"Independent deployment\", \"Horizontally scalable computation layer\", \"No user session ownership\")\n   193|- status: complete\n   194|- design_note: stateless design is the stated mechanism enabling horizontal scaling | status: assumption [ASM-04]\n   195|- depends_on: [none identified]\n   196|- acceptance_criteria:\n   197|  - FR-10-AC-01: given: multiple service instances are deployed | when: calculate requests are distributed across instances | then: each request is served independently with no user session ownership and identical results for identical inputs\n   198|  - FR-10-AC-02: given: the service is deployed | when: the service is deployed or scaled independently of other platform components | then: calculation behavior is unchanged\n   199|\n   200|---\n   201|\n   202|## non_functional_requirements\n   203|\n   204|### NFR-01: Standard operations respond at millisecond-level latency\n   205|- category: performance\n   206|- target: response time at millisecond level for standard operations [PENDING: exact numeric threshold] \n   207|- source: project-brief.md §5 Latency (\"Millisecond-level response time for standard operations\"); discovery-transcript.md §09:00 (\"It should feel instant. This isn't heavy computation.\")\n   208|- status: pending\n   209|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   210|\n   211|### NFR-02: Same input always produces the same result (determinism)\n   212|- category: compliance\n   213|- target: 100% of repeated identical requests return the identical resultValue\n   214|- source: project-brief.md §5 Determinism; discovery-transcript.md §03:00\n   215|- status: complete\n   216|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   217|\n   218|### NFR-03: Calculation succeeds even when history persistence fails\n   219|- category: availability\n   220|- target: 100% of valid calculate requests return a result when history persistence is unavailable\n   221|- source: project-brief.md §5 Reliability; discovery-transcript.md §04:30, §15:00\n   222|- status: complete\n   223|- design_note: behavioral guarantee mirrored as FR-07; retained here as the availability-level statement of the same guarantee | status: assumption [ASM-05]\n   224|\n   225|### NFR-04: Stateless design supports horizontal scaling\n   226|- category: scalability\n   227|- target: service capacity scales horizontally by adding instances with no change to calculation behavior [PENDING: numeric concurrency/volume target]\n   228|- source: project-brief.md §5 Scalability, §3 Architectural Positioning; discovery-transcript.md §09:00 (\"Possibly if automation kicks in, but calculations are lightweight\")\n   229|- status: pending\n   230|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   231|\n   232|### NFR-05: Observability readiness for future metrics and tracing integration\n   233|- category: usability\n   234|- target: [PENDING: nfr_target — no measurable observability target stated in inputs]\n   235|- source: project-brief.md §5 Observability readiness (\"Designed for future metrics and tracing integration\")\n   236|- status: pending\n   237|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   238|\n   239|### NFR-06: Security and access control for the calculation API\n   240|- category: security\n   241|- target: [PENDING: nfr_target — no security requirement stated in inputs]\n   242|- source: not stated in inputs\n   243|- status: pending\n   244|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   245|\n   246|### NFR-07: API usability for internal consumers\n   247|- category: usability\n   248|- target: [PENDING: nfr_target — no usability/accessibility standard stated in inputs]\n   249|- source: discovery-transcript.md §07:30 (\"Simple. Send operands and operation, get result.\"), §13:30 (\"it must be simple or teams won't use it\")\n   250|- status: pending\n   251|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   252|\n   253|---\n   254|\n   255|## kpis\n   256|\n   257|### KPI-01: Upstream system integration adoption\n   258|- target: at least 1 upstream system successfully integrated\n   259|- measurement: count of upstream systems integrated with the calculate operation\n   260|- timeframe: not stated in inputs\n   261|- source: project-brief.md §7 Success Criteria (\"Service successfully integrated by at least one upstream system\")\n   262|- status: complete\n   263|\n   264|### KPI-02: Deterministic test coverage of all operations\n   265|- target: 100% of supported operations covered by deterministic unit tests\n   266|- measurement: deterministic unit test coverage report across all operations\n   267|- timeframe: not stated in inputs\n   268|- source: project-brief.md §7 Success Criteria (\"Deterministic unit test coverage for all operations\")\n   269|- status: complete\n   270|\n   271|### KPI-03: History persistence validation\n   272|- target: optional history persistence validated\n   273|- measurement: validation run confirming history persistence works without impacting response delivery\n   274|- timeframe: not stated in inputs\n   275|- source: project-brief.md §7 Success Criteria (\"Optional history persistence validated\")\n   276|- status: complete\n   277|\n   278|### KPI-04: API contract documentation for internal reuse\n   279|- target: API contract documented for internal reuse\n   280|- measurement: published API contract documentation available to internal consumers\n   281|- timeframe: not stated in inputs\n   282|- source: project-brief.md §7 Success Criteria (\"API contract documented for internal reuse\")\n   283|- status: complete\n   284|\n   285|### KPI-05: Reduction of duplicated math logic across teams\n   286|- target: [PENDING: numeric target — success criterion stated qualitatively (\"Teams stop duplicating math logic\")]\n   287|- measurement: not stated in inputs\n   288|- timeframe: not stated in inputs\n   289|- source: discovery-transcript.md §14:30 (\"Teams stop duplicating math logic\")\n   290|- status: pending\n   291|\n   292|### KPI-06: Result consistency across systems\n   293|- target: [PENDING: numeric target — success criterion stated qualitatively (\"Results become consistent\")]\n   294|- measurement: not stated in inputs\n   295|- timeframe: not stated in inputs\n   296|- source: discovery-transcript.md §14:30 (\"Results become consistent\")\n   297|- status: pending\n   298|\n   299|### KPI-07: Calculation traceability availability\n   300|- target: [PENDING: numeric target — success criterion stated qualitatively (\"We can trace calculations when something looks wrong\")]\n   301|- measurement: not stated in inputs\n   302|- timeframe: not stated in inputs\n   303|- source: discovery-transcript.md §14:30 (\"We can trace calculations when something looks wrong\")\n   304|- status: pending\n   305|\n   306|---\n   307|\n   308|## jtbd\n   309|\n   310|### JTBD-01: core_functional\n   311|- situation: when a backend service, report, or workflow needs a calculation performed, the calculation logic is currently duplicated across frontend, backend, and reporting systems | source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00\n   312|- motivation: obtain a consistent, correct numerical result from a single authoritative math engine instead of maintaining formula logic in each consumer | source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00; project-brief.md §1 Business Objective\n   313|- outcome: the caller receives a deterministic result for the operation requested, identical to what any other system would receive for the same input | source: discovery-transcript.md §03:00; project-brief.md §5 Determinism\n   314|- source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00, §03:00; project-brief.md §1, §5\n   315|- status: complete\n   316|\n   317|### JTBD-02: related\n   318|- situation: when a formula or numerical behavior changes, each system that embedded its own calculation logic must be found and fixed individually, and changes break things in different places | source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00\n   319|- motivation: evolve numerical behavior in one controlled place so changes propagate consistently across the ecosystem | source: project-brief.md §1 (\"controlled evolution of numerical behavior\"); discovery-transcript.md §00:00\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":8}}
