[08:32:46] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-ba893f4c99915bbf","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\artifacts\\outputs\\product-definition\\prd\\PRD-SPEC-pd-20261027-calc001.md\" lines=\"590\" start=\"319\" end=\"590\" returned=\"272\" has_more=\"false\" truncated=\"false\">\n   319|- motivation: evolve numerical behavior in one controlled place so changes propagate consistently across the ecosystem | source: project-brief.md §1 (\"controlled evolution of numerical behavior\"); discovery-transcript.md §00:00\n   320|- outcome: calculation logic is updated once in the central service and all consumers immediately use the same updated behavior without breaking existing consumers | source: discovery-transcript.md §01:30; project-brief.md §1\n   321|- source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00, §01:30; project-brief.md §1\n   322|- status: complete\n   323|\n   324|### JTBD-03: related\n   325|- situation: when a calculation result looks wrong, an engineer needs to understand what the service computed and why | source: discovery-transcript.md §04:30, §14:30\n   326|- motivation: trace and debug calculations through recorded calculation metadata without coupling persistence to computation | source: discovery-transcript.md §04:30 (\"mainly debugging and traceability\"); project-brief.md §2 History Tracking\n   327|- outcome: recent calculation records are available for diagnostics and auditing, and retrieving them never degrades the calculation itself | source: project-brief.md §2 History Tracking, §4 History Retrieval; discovery-transcript.md §04:30, §11:00\n   328|- source: discovery-transcript.md §04:30, §11:00, §14:30; project-brief.md §2, §4\n   329|- status: complete\n   330|\n   331|### JTBD-04: related\n   332|- situation: when an automated workflow or AI-driven process runs, it needs lightweight deterministic calculations performed synchronously as part of the pipeline | source: discovery-transcript.md §06:00, §09:00, §12:00\n   333|- motivation: validate outputs and perform computations inside pipelines using a stable, reusable computation service rather than bespoke logic | source: discovery-transcript.md §12:00 (\"Potentially as part of automated workflows — like validating outputs inside pipelines\"); project-brief.md §1\n   334|- outcome: automation workflows and AI agents obtain instant, deterministic calculation results as a foundational building block for AI-assisted workflows | source: discovery-transcript.md §06:00, §09:00; project-brief.md §6\n   335|- source: discovery-transcript.md §06:00, §09:00, §12:00; project-brief.md §1, §6\n   336|- status: complete\n   337|\n   338|---\n   339|\n   340|## traceability\n   341|\n   342|| FR | fr_name | JTBD | jtbd_name | direction | status |\n   343||----|---------|------|-----------|-----------|--------|\n   344|| FR-01 | Execute standard arithmetic operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division) | JTBD-01 | core_functional | forward | mapped |\n   345|| FR-02 | Execute advanced operations (exponentiation, roots, trigonometric functions) | JTBD-01 | core_functional | forward | mapped |\n   346|| FR-03 | Produce deterministic, high-precision results | JTBD-01 | core_functional | forward | mapped |\n   347|| FR-04 | Expose a calculate operation via `POST /api/v1/math/calculate` | JTBD-01 | core_functional | forward | mapped |\n   348|| FR-05 | Return clear, fail-fast errors for invalid input | JTBD-01 | core_functional | forward | mapped |\n   349|| FR-06 | Record calculation history as optional, auxiliary local persistence | JTBD-03 | related | forward | mapped |\n   350|| FR-07 | Calculation must succeed even if history persistence fails | JTBD-03 | related | forward | mapped |\n   351|| FR-08 | Provide history retrieval of recent calculation records | JTBD-03 | related | forward | mapped |\n   352|| FR-09 | Support extensibility so new operations are added without breaking existing consumers | JTBD-02 | related | forward | mapped |\n   353|| FR-10 | Operate as a stateless, independently deployable, horizontally scalable service | JTBD-04 | related | forward | mapped |\n   354|| — | — | JTBD-01 | core_functional | reverse | mapped |\n   355|| — | — | JTBD-02 | related | reverse | mapped |\n   356|| — | — | JTBD-03 | related | reverse | mapped |\n   357|| — | — | JTBD-04 | related | reverse | mapped |\n   358|\n   359|---\n   360|\n   361|## dependencies\n   362|\n   363|### DEP-01: Upstream consumer systems\n   364|- type: external\n   365|- description: at least one upstream system (backend service, reporting pipeline, automation workflow, or AI agent) must integrate with the calculate operation for the service to deliver value | source: project-brief.md §7 Success Criteria\n   366|- owner: not stated in inputs\n   367|- impact_if_unavailable: no consumer integration means the service delivers no business value regardless of correctness | source: project-brief.md §7\n   368|- source: project-brief.md §7 Success Criteria\n   369|- status: pending\n   370|\n   371|### DEP-02: Embedded persistence abstraction for optional history\n   372|- type: technical\n   373|- description: optional history tracking relies on a lightweight local persistence behind an embedded database abstraction, replaceable without changing the API contract | source: project-brief.md §2 History Tracking, §3 Architectural Positioning\n   374|- owner: not stated in inputs\n   375|- impact_if_unavailable: history tracking and history retrieval (FR-06, FR-08) cannot be delivered; calculations themselves are unaffected because persistence must never block calculation (FR-07) | source: project-brief.md §2, §5\n   376|- source: project-brief.md §2, §3, §5\n   377|- status: complete\n   378|\n   379|### inter_feature_dependencies\n   380|- FR-02 (Execute advanced operations) depends_on FR-01 (Execute standard arithmetic operations): advanced operations extend the same calculate operation contract established by standard operations | source: project-brief.md §2; discovery-transcript.md §01:30 | status: complete\n   381|- FR-03 (Produce deterministic, high-precision results) depends_on FR-01 (Execute standard arithmetic operations): determinism applies to the operations FR-01 defines | source: project-brief.md §2, §5 | status: complete\n   382|- FR-04 (Expose a calculate operation via `POST /api/v1/math/calculate`) depends_on FR-01 (Execute standard arithmetic operations): the endpoint exposes the operations FR-01 defines | source: project-brief.md §4 | status: complete\n   383|- FR-05 (Return clear, fail-fast errors for invalid input) depends_on FR-01 (Execute standard arithmetic operations): error handling governs requests against the calculate operation | source: discovery-transcript.md §10:00 | status: complete\n   384|- FR-06 (Record calculation history as optional, auxiliary local persistence) depends_on FR-01 (Execute standard arithmetic operations): history records metadata of calculations performed via the calculate operation | source: project-brief.md §2 | status: complete\n   385|- FR-07 (Calculation must succeed even if history persistence fails) depends_on FR-06 (Record calculation history as optional, auxiliary local persistence): the persistence-failure guarantee is meaningful only when history recording exists | source: project-brief.md §5 Reliability | status: complete\n   386|- FR-08 (Provide history retrieval of recent calculation records) depends_on FR-06 (Record calculation history as optional, auxiliary local persistence): retrieval requires recorded history | source: project-brief.md §4 | status: complete\n   387|- FR-09 (Support extensibility so new operations are added without breaking existing consumers) depends_on FR-01 (Execute standard arithmetic operations): extensibility preserves the contract FR-01 establishes | source: discovery-transcript.md §01:30 | status: complete\n   388|\n   389|---\n   390|\n   391|## risks\n   392|\n   393|### RSK-01: Hidden logic differences remain between systems after the service launches\n   394|- likelihood: medium\n   395|- impact: high\n   396|- mitigation: centralize all calculation logic in the service and enforce deterministic, high-precision computation (FR-03) so all systems receive identical results; validate with deterministic unit test coverage for all operations (KPI-02)\n   397|- source: discovery-transcript.md §13:30 (\"Hidden logic differences between systems\")\n   398|- status: complete\n   399|\n   400|### RSK-02: Teams bypass the service and keep embedding their own calculation logic\n   401|- likelihood: medium\n   402|- impact: high\n   403|- mitigation: keep the API simple — send operands and operation, get result — because adoption depends on simplicity (\"it must be simple or teams won't use it\"); document the API contract for internal reuse (KPI-04)\n   404|- source: discovery-transcript.md §13:30 (\"people bypassing the service\", \"it must be simple or teams won't use it\")\n   405|- status: complete\n   406|\n   407|### RSK-03: History persistence degrades calculation response delivery\n   408|- likelihood: low\n   409|- impact: medium\n   410|- mitigation: treat persistence as auxiliary storage that must not impact response delivery (FR-06, FR-07); calculation must always succeed even if persistence fails\n   411|- source: project-brief.md §2 History Tracking, §5 Reliability; discovery-transcript.md §04:30, §15:00\n   412|- status: complete\n   413|\n   414|### RSK-04: Precision policy gaps reintroduce floating-point surprises between systems\n   415|- likelihood: medium\n   416|- impact: medium\n   417|- mitigation: define and apply a single high-precision internal computation strategy (FR-03); the concrete precision policy (decimal digits, rounding mode) still needs to be fixed before implementation (PI-01)\n   418|- source: discovery-transcript.md §03:00 (\"Some teams complained about rounding differences between systems\", \"We don't want floating-point surprises\")\n   419|- status: complete\n   420|\n   421|---\n   422|\n   423|## assumptions\n   424|\n   425|### ASM-01: Consumer personas are technically proficient engineering roles\n   426|- impact_if_wrong: persona goals, pain points, and usage patterns may not reflect actual consumer behavior, skewing prioritization of API usability work\n   427|- source_basis: inferred from the consumer types named in project-brief.md §3 (backend services, reporting pipelines, automation workflows, AI agents) and discovery-transcript.md §06:00\n   428|- status: active\n   429|\n   430|### ASM-02: The brief's conceptual API contract field names are the intended contract names\n   431|- impact_if_wrong: FR-04 acceptance criteria and downstream API documentation would use field names that differ from the final implemented contract\n   432|- source_basis: project-brief.md §4 API Contract (input fields `operand1`, `operand2`, `operation`; output fields `resultValue`, `operationType`, `timestamp`) — labeled \"Conceptual\" in the brief\n   433|- status: active\n   434|\n   435|### ASM-03: History persistence uses a lightweight local embedded database abstraction\n   436|- impact_if_wrong: the persistence design, deployment footprint, and replaceability guarantees in FR-06 would need rework\n   437|- source_basis: project-brief.md §2 History Tracking (\"lightweight local persistence\"), §3 (\"Optional local persistence via embedded database abstraction\"), and the brief's alignment note (\"H2-based lightweight persistence strategy\")\n   438|- status: active\n   439|\n   440|### ASM-04: Stateless design is the mechanism that enables horizontal scaling\n   441|- impact_if_wrong: scaling behavior would depend on stateful components, invalidating FR-10 acceptance criteria\n   442|- source_basis: project-brief.md §3 (\"stateless microservice model\", \"Horizontally scalable computation layer\"), §5 (\"Stateless design allows horizontal scaling\")\n   443|- status: active\n   444|\n   445|### ASM-05: The reliability guarantee is stated at both FR and NFR level intentionally\n   446|- impact_if_wrong: duplicate coverage of the same guarantee could cause inconsistent verification; if unintended, NFR-03 should be merged into FR-07\n   447|- source_basis: project-brief.md §5 Reliability states the guarantee as a non-functional goal; FR-07 carries the behavioral statement\n   448|- status: active\n   449|\n   450|### ASM-06: Synchronous request/response is the only interaction mode required at launch\n   451|- impact_if_wrong: asynchronous or batch interaction modes would require additional API surface not covered by FR-04\n   452|- source_basis: discovery-transcript.md §06:00 (\"Real-time usage?\" / \"Mostly synchronous, yes.\"); batch calculations explicitly deferred (\"Maybe later, not day one\")\n   453|- status: active\n   454|\n   455|### ASM-07: MoSCoW priorities inferred from source phrasing\n   456|- impact_if_wrong: phasing in mvp_phasing would change; operations or capabilities assumed must-have could be deferred or vice versa\n   457|- source_basis: inferred from project-brief.md §2 (\"Standard Operations\" listed first, \"History Tracking (Optional)\", \"Future Capability (Planned)\") and discovery-transcript.md §01:30 (\"Basic arithmetic first\"), §07:30 (\"Maybe later, not day one\")\n   458|- status: active\n   459|\n   460|---\n   461|\n   462|## constraints\n   463|\n   464|### budget\n   465|- [PENDING: budget_constraints] | value: \"unknown — stakeholder input required\" | status: pending\n   466|\n   467|### timeline\n   468|- [PENDING: timeline_constraints] | value: \"unknown — stakeholder input required\" | status: pending\n   469|\n   470|### resource\n   471|- [PENDING: resource_constraints] | value: \"unknown — stakeholder input required\" | status: pending\n   472|\n   473|### regulatory\n   474|- [PENDING: regulatory_constraints] | status: pending\n   475|\n   476|### organizational\n   477|- History is diagnostic, not regulatory: history tracking is not required to meet a regulatory audit obligation | source: discovery-transcript.md §11:00 (\"History is more diagnostic than regulatory\") | status: complete\n   478|- Persistence must be replaceable without changing the API contract | source: project-brief.md §3 (\"persistence can be replaced without changing the API contract\") | status: complete\n   479|\n   480|---\n   481|\n   482|## mvp_phasing\n   483|\n   484|### phase_1_mvp\n   485|- must_have: FR-01 (Execute standard arithmetic operations), FR-03 (Produce deterministic, high-precision results), FR-04 (Expose a calculate operation via `POST /api/v1/math/calculate`), FR-05 (Return clear, fail-fast errors for invalid input), FR-09 (Support extensibility so new operations are added without breaking existing consumers), FR-10 (Operate as a stateless, independently deployable, horizontally scalable service) | source: project-brief.md §2 Standard Operations, §4 API Contract, §5; discovery-transcript.md §01:30 (\"Basic arithmetic first\"), §10:00, §13:30 | status: assumption [ASM-07]\n   486|\n   487|### phase_2\n   488|- should_have: FR-02 (Execute advanced operations) | source: project-brief.md §2 Advanced Operations; discovery-transcript.md §01:30 (\"powers, square roots, some trigonometry\" listed after basic arithmetic) | status: assumption [ASM-07]\n   489|\n   490|### phase_3\n   491|- could_have: FR-06 (Record calculation history as optional, auxiliary local persistence), FR-08 (Provide history retrieval of recent calculation records) | source: project-brief.md §2 History Tracking (Optional), §4 History Retrieval (Optional); discovery-transcript.md §04:30 (\"Not necessarily every one\") | status: assumption [ASM-07]\n   492|\n   493|### deferred\n   494|- wont_have: batch calculations, expression evaluation, workflow integration with automation agents, event-based history | source: project-brief.md §2 Future Capability (Planned); discovery-transcript.md §07:30 (\"Maybe later, not day one\", \"not required now\") | status: complete\n   495|\n   496|### sequencing_notes\n   497|- FR-07 (Calculation must succeed even if history persistence fails) requires FR-06 (Record calculation history as optional, auxiliary local persistence) to exist before its guarantee is testable | source: project-brief.md §5 Reliability | status: complete\n   498|- FR-08 (Provide history retrieval of recent calculation records) depends on FR-06 (Record calculation history as optional, auxiliary local persistence) completion | source: project-brief.md §4 | status: complete\n   499|- FR-02 (Execute advanced operations) extends FR-01 (Execute standard arithmetic operations) and follows it in delivery order | source: discovery-transcript.md §01:30 | status: complete\n   500|\n   501|---\n   502|\n   503|## literal_registry\n   504|\n   505|| id | name | exact_value | type | applies_to_ids | source_ref |\n   506||----|------|-------------|------|----------------|------------|\n   507|| LIT-01 | calculate endpoint route | POST /api/v1/math/calculate | route | FR-04, FR-04-AC-01 | project-brief.md §4 API Contract |\n   508|| LIT-02 | request field: operand1 | operand1 | field | FR-04, FR-04-AC-01 | project-brief.md §4 API Contract |\n   509|| LIT-03 | request field: operand2 | operand2 | field | FR-04, FR-04-AC-01 | project-brief.md §4 API Contract |\n   510|| LIT-04 | request field: operation | operation | field | FR-04, FR-04-AC-01 | project-brief.md §4 API Contract |\n   511|| LIT-05 | response field: resultValue | resultValue | field | FR-01, FR-04, FR-04-AC-01 | project-brief.md §4 API Contract |\n   512|| LIT-06 | response field: operationType | operationType | field | FR-01, FR-04, FR-04-AC-01 | project-brief.md §4 API Contract |\n   513|| LIT-07 | response field: timestamp | timestamp | field | FR-04, FR-04-AC-01 | project-brief.md §4 API Contract |\n   514|| LIT-08 | operation enum: ADD | ADD | enum | FR-01, FR-01-AC-01, FR-04-AC-01 | project-brief.md §4 API Contract |\n   515|| LIT-09 | example request values | operand1: 10.5, operand2: 5.0, operation: ADD → resultValue: 15.5 | other | FR-04-AC-01 | project-brief.md §4 API Contract |\n   516|| LIT-10 | latency goal | millisecond-level response time for standard operations | constraint | NFR-01 | project-brief.md §5 Non-Functional Goals |\n   517|| LIT-11 | persistence strategy | H2-based lightweight persistence strategy | constraint | FR-06, ASM-03 | project-brief.md alignment note (v1.1) |\n   518|\n   519|---\n   520|\n   521|## glossary\n   522|\n   523|| term_id | term | definition | first_used | source_ref |\n   524||---------|------|------------|------------|------------|\n   525|| TERM-01 | Calc-Service | The math operations microservice: a lightweight, stateless microservice providing centralized mathematical computation for the platform | project_info | project-brief.md Overview |\n   526|| TERM-02 | math engine | The deterministic computation core of Calc-Service consumed by backend services, automation workflows, and AI-driven processes | project_info | project-brief.md Overview |\n   527|| TERM-03 | standard operations | The four basic arithmetic operations: addition, subtraction, multiplication, division | scope | project-brief.md §2 Standard Operations |\n   528|| TERM-04 | advanced operations | Exponentiation, roots, and trigonometric functions beyond the standard operations | scope | project-brief.md §2 Advanced Operations |\n   529|| TERM-05 | history tracking | Optional recording of calculation metadata for traceability and diagnostics, implemented as lightweight local auxiliary persistence | scope | project-brief.md §2 History Tracking |\n   530|| TERM-06 | history retrieval | Capability providing recent calculation records for diagnostics and auditing | scope | project-brief.md §4 History Retrieval |\n   531|| TERM-07 | determinism | Property that the same input always produces the same result | non_functional_requirements | project-brief.md §5 Determinism |\n   532|| TERM-08 | embedded database abstraction | The replaceable local persistence layer behind history tracking; persistence can be replaced without changing the API contract | dependencies | project-brief.md §3 Architectural Positioning |\n   533|| TERM-09 | calculate operation | The primary API operation exposed at `POST /api/v1/math/calculate` accepting operands and an operation and returning a result | functional_requirements | project-brief.md §4 API Contract |\n   534|\n   535|---\n   536|\n   537|## open_questions\n   538|\n   539|This section is the **single consolidated registry** of all unresolved items.\n   540|Every `[PENDING]` marker, every `orphan` traceability entry, every `assumption`\n   541|that needs validation — all appear here once.\n   542|\n   543|Downstream skills (Epics, User Stories, Architecture, Implementation) read\n   544|ONLY this section to understand what is unresolved. They do NOT scan other\n   545|sections for [PENDING] markers — this registry IS the source of truth.\n   546|\n   547|### pending_inputs\n   548|\n   549|| id | affected_ids | blocking | type | section | item_id | field | impact | question | fallback_behavior | source_ref |\n   550||----|--------------|----------|------|---------|---------|-------|--------|----------|-------------------|------------|\n   551|| PI-01 | FR-03, FR-03-AC-01, FR-03-AC-02, RSK-04 | no | missing | functional_requirements | FR-03 | design_note (precision policy) | HIGH | What precision policy (number of decimal digits and rounding mode) must the high-precision internal computation apply so results are identical across systems? | Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: a single high-precision decimal computation strategy is applied service-wide and treated as an implementation detail until stated | project-brief.md §2 Precision Strategy; discovery-transcript.md §03:00 |\n   552|| PI-02 | FR-08, FR-08-AC-01 | no | missing | functional_requirements | FR-08 | design_note (retrieval contract) | MEDIUM | What is the history retrieval contract — endpoint path, response shape, and how many recent records are returned? | Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: FR-08 is emitted with status pending and no endpoint contract; history retrieval is excluded from the MVP phase | project-brief.md §4 History Retrieval |\n   553|| PI-03 | NFR-01 | no | missing | non_functional_requirements | NFR-01 | target | HIGH | What numeric latency threshold should \"millisecond-level response time\" commit to for standard operations? | Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: millisecond-level interpreted as sub-second response (< 1000 ms) for standard operations | project-brief.md §5 Latency; discovery-transcript.md §09:00 |\n   554|| PI-04 | NFR-04 | no | missing | non_functional_requirements | NFR-04 | target | MEDIUM | What concurrency or request-volume target must horizontal scaling support? | Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: horizontal scaling by adding instances with no numeric concurrency target committed | project-brief.md §5 Scalability; discovery-transcript.md §09:00 |\n   555|| PI-05 | NFR-05 | no | missing | non_functional_requirements | NFR-05 | target | MEDIUM | What observability capability must be ready at launch — structured logs only, or also metrics and tracing hooks? | Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: structured logging sufficient for diagnostics; metrics and tracing integration deferred | project-brief.md §5 Observability readiness |\n   556|| PI-06 | NFR-06 | no | missing | non_functional_requirements | NFR-06 | target | HIGH | What security and access-control requirements apply to the calculation API (authentication, network exposure, input limits)? | Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: service treated as an internal platform component with no additional authentication assumed | not stated in inputs |\n   557|| PI-07 | NFR-07 | no | missing | non_functional_requirements | NFR-07 | target | LOW | What usability standard should the API contract commit to, given adoption depends on simplicity? | Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: simple payload design (operands + operation in, result out) treated as the usability baseline | discovery-transcript.md §07:30, §13:30 |\n   558|| PI-08 | KPI-05, KPI-06, KPI-07 | no | missing | kpis | KPI-05, KPI-06, KPI-07 | target | MEDIUM | What numeric targets should replace the qualitative success criteria (teams stop duplicating math logic, results become consistent, calculations traceable)? | Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: qualitative success criteria tracked as binary (met / not met) until numeric targets are set | discovery-transcript.md §14:30 |\n   559|| PI-09 | DEP-01, KPI-01 | no | missing | dependencies | DEP-01 | owner | MEDIUM | Which upstream system is the first integration target for the calculate operation? | Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: any one upstream system qualifies for the success criterion; integration sequencing left to delivery planning | project-brief.md §7 Success Criteria |\n   560|| PI-10 | constraints.budget, constraints.timeline, constraints.resource | no | missing | constraints | budget, timeline, resource | value | MEDIUM | What are the budget, timeline, and staffing constraints for delivering the service? | Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: no budget ceiling, no calendar deadline, and no staffing cap assumed; scope treated as uncapped until stated | not stated in inputs |\n   561|| PI-11 | constraints.regulatory | no | missing | constraints | regulatory | constraint | LOW | Are there regulatory or compliance obligations that apply to the service or its calculation records? | Deferred to Discovery; interim baseline: no regulatory constraints assumed; history treated as diagnostic-only per the discovery transcript | discovery-transcript.md §11:00 (\"History is more diagnostic than regulatory\") |\n   562|\n   563|### traceability_gaps\n   564|\n   565|| id | affected_ids | blocking | direction | fr_id | jtbd_id | impact | note | fallback_behavior | source_ref |\n   566||----|--------------|----------|-----------|-------|---------|--------|------|-------------------|------------|\n   567|\n   568|### assumptions_to_validate\n   569|\n   570|| id | affected_ids | blocking | assumption | impact_if_wrong | validation_method | fallback_behavior | source_ref |\n   571||----|--------------|----------|------------|-----------------|-------------------|-------------------|------------|\n   572|| ASM-01 | P-01, P-02, P-03, P-04 | no | Consumer personas are technically proficient engineering roles | Persona goals and pain points may not reflect actual consumer behavior, skewing API usability prioritization | Confirm consumer roles and proficiency with the product owner | Personas treated as engineering-role consumers for prioritization | project-brief.md §3; discovery-transcript.md §06:00 |\n   573|| ASM-02 | FR-04, FR-04-AC-01, LIT-02, LIT-03, LIT-04, LIT-05, LIT-06, LIT-07 | no | The brief's conceptual API contract field names are the intended contract names | Acceptance criteria and API documentation would use field names differing from the final implemented contract | Confirm field names when the API contract is finalized | Field names from project-brief.md §4 used verbatim in acceptance criteria | project-brief.md §4 API Contract |\n   574|| ASM-03 | FR-06, DEP-02, LIT-11 | no | History persistence uses a lightweight local embedded database abstraction | Persistence design, deployment footprint, and replaceability guarantees in FR-06 would need rework | Confirm persistence technology choice during architecture definition | Persistence kept behind a replaceable abstraction with no specific engine mandated | project-brief.md §2, §3, alignment note |\n   575|| ASM-04 | FR-10, NFR-04 | no | Stateless design is the mechanism that enables horizontal scaling | Scaling behavior would depend on stateful components, invalidating FR-10 acceptance criteria | Confirm no session or in-memory state is introduced during architecture definition | Service designed and verified stateless | project-brief.md §3, §5 |\n   576|| ASM-05 | FR-07, NFR-03 | no | The reliability guarantee is stated at both FR and NFR level intentionally | Duplicate coverage could cause inconsistent verification; if unintended, NFR-03 should be merged into FR-07 | Reviewer confirms whether the dual statement is intentional | Both statements retained and verified against the same test (calculation succeeds while persistence fails) | project-brief.md §5 Reliability |\n   577|| ASM-06 | FR-04, scope.out_of_scope | no | Synchronous request/response is the only interaction mode required at launch | Asynchronous or batch interaction modes would require additional API surface not covered by FR-04 | Confirm with the product owner that no async or batch mode is needed for the MVP | Only synchronous request/response modeled; batch and async excluded from scope | discovery-transcript.md §06:00, §07:30 |\n   578|| ASM-07 | FR-01, FR-02, FR-06, FR-08, mvp_phasing | no | MoSCoW priorities inferred from source phrasing (standard operations first, history optional, future capabilities planned) | Phasing would change; capabilities assumed must-have could be deferred or vice versa | Confirm priorities with the product owner | Phasing follows the inferred MoSCoW assignment in mvp_phasing | project-brief.md §2; discovery-transcript.md §01:30, §07:30 |\n   579|\n   580|### summary\n   581|- total_frs: 10\n   582|- total_nfrs: 7\n   583|- total_jtbds: 4\n   584|- total_kpis: 7\n   585|- pending_inputs: 11\n   586|- traceability_gaps: 0\n   587|- assumptions: 7\n   588|- risks: 4\n   589|- dependencies: 2\n   590|- status: draft\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":6}}
